(window.UPANISHAD_DATA=window.UPANISHAD_DATA||{})["katha"]={"slug":"katha","name":"Kaṭha","deva":"कठ","note":"","intro":["The Kaṭha Upaniṣad belongs to the Kāṭhaka śākhā of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda. A closely related (largely identical) text appears in the Caraka-Kāṭha branch. The text below follows the Śāṅkara-vulgate (the recension on which Śaṅkara wrote his bhāṣya), checked against the sanskritdocuments.org digital edition and Olivelle's <em>Early Upaniṣads</em> (Oxford 1998) critical apparatus.","The locus classicus of Advaita exegesis on Kaṭha. Śaṅkara reads the entire Upaniṣad as a graduated unveiling of the non-dual ātman: Vallī I.i sets up the renunciate context (the third boon = brahma-vidyā, not karma-phala); I.ii distinguishes the path of preferable good (śreyas) from pleasant good (preyas); I.iii introduces the chariot allegory and the hierarchical scale from indriya up to puruṣa; II.i–iii systematically deconstruct embodiment, transmigration, and finally reveal the self-luminous Brahman beyond sun, moon, and fire. Gambhīrānanda's English faithfully renders the bhāṣya with Sanskrit citations."],"sections":[{"t":"Introduction","u":[{"ref":"Manuscript variations and recensional notes","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Kaṭha Upaniṣad belongs to the Kāṭhaka śākhā of the Kṛṣṇa-Yajurveda. A closely related (largely identical) text appears in the Caraka-Kāṭha branch. The text below follows the Śāṅkara-vulgate (the recension on which Śaṅkara wrote his bhāṣya), checked against the sanskritdocuments.org digital edition and Olivelle's <em>Early Upaniṣads</em> (Oxford 1998) critical apparatus.","<table><tr><td>Locus</td><td>Variant</td><td>Note</td></tr><tr><td>I.i.16</td><td>sṛṅkām (Śaṅkara) ∥ sṛṅkām … anekarūpām</td><td>Śaṅkara reads sṛṅkā as a garland of jewels; some MSS read sṛṅgām (peak).</td></tr><tr><td>I.ii.16</td><td>aum ity etat ∥ omity etat</td><td>Sandhi resolution differs; sense unchanged. Pratīka of all the Vedas.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.1</td><td>parārdhe ∥ parārdhye</td><td>Śaṅkara: parārdhe = the supreme abode (locative). MS variant parārdhye is metrically equivalent.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.9</td><td>tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam</td><td>Quotes Ṛgveda I.22.20. Some MSS append etad vai tat after this hemistich.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.17–18</td><td>Authenticity debated</td><td>Olivelle and Whitney mark these phala-śruti verses as possibly later; Śaṅkara comments on them as integral.</td></tr><tr><td>II.i.10–11</td><td>yad eveha tad amutra …</td><td>Identical wording to Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka IV.iv.19. Both Upaniṣads claim originality; date disputed.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.1</td><td>ūrdhva-mūlaḥ ∥ ūrdhvamūlo</td><td>The inverted aśvattha. Cited in Bhagavad-Gītā 15.1 with extensions.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.16</td><td>śataṃ caikā ca hṛdayasya nāḍyaḥ</td><td>Compare Chāndogya VIII.6.6; Praśna III.6; Maitrī VI.21. The 101st nāḍī = suṣumnā.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.18</td><td>Closing phala-śruti</td><td>Marks the end. Followed by the repeated śānti-pāṭha shared with Taittirīya.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"The six commentaries summarised","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Śaṅkara (Gambhīrānanda translation, Advaita Ashrama 1957)","The locus classicus of Advaita exegesis on Kaṭha. Śaṅkara reads the entire Upaniṣad as a graduated unveiling of the non-dual ātman: Vallī I.i sets up the renunciate context (the third boon = brahma-vidyā, not karma-phala); I.ii distinguishes the path of preferable good (śreyas) from pleasant good (preyas); I.iii introduces the chariot allegory and the hierarchical scale from indriya up to puruṣa; II.i–iii systematically deconstruct embodiment, transmigration, and finally reveal the self-luminous Brahman beyond sun, moon, and fire. Gambhīrānanda's English faithfully renders the bhāṣya with Sanskrit citations.","Rangarāmānuja (Viśiṣṭādvaita-Upaniṣad-bhāṣya, ed. P.B. Annangaracharya 1939)","The Śrīvaiṣṇava reading. Where Śaṅkara takes Kaṭha as ātman-only, Rangarāmānuja insists the ‘aṇuṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ’ (I.ii.12; II.i.12) is the jīvātman within the heart, while the Supreme is Nārāyaṇa-Viṣṇu (the puruṣaḥ paraḥ of I.iii.11). The pratyagātman of II.i.1 is the embodied self turning inward; the param padam viṣṇoḥ of I.iii.9 is Vaikuṇṭha. Throughout, the body–soul relation prefigures the śarīra–śarīrin doctrine.","Madhva (Kāṭhaka-Upaniṣad-bhāṣya, in Sarvamūla-grantha-saṅgraha)","The Dvaita reading. Madhva sharply distinguishes the jīva (the aṇuṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ) from Paramātman (Viṣṇu) at every turn. The famous I.ii.23 — ‘yam evaiṣa vṛṇute tena labhyaḥ’ — is read as the absolute sovereignty of the Lord's prasāda, not the Advaitic non-instrumentality of effort. Madhva quotes the Mahābhārata-Tātparya-Nirṇaya and Brahma-Sūtra extensively to anchor each verse to a five-fold difference (pañca-bheda).","Swāmī Sivānanda (The Kathopanishad, Divine Life Society 1949)","A practitioner's reading in lucid English. Sivānanda treats Kaṭha as a manual of self-enquiry and renunciation: each verse is unpacked into a moral exhortation (śreyas over preyas, viveka, vairāgya, mumukṣutva). The chariot allegory is the centrepiece of his pedagogy on indriya-nigraha. Quotations frequently bridge to Bhagavad-Gītā parallels.","Paul Deussen (Sechzig Upanishad's des Veda, Leipzig 1897 — English tr. Bedekar/Palsule 1980)","The seminal European critical edition. Deussen translates Kaṭha into German on the basis of the Śāṅkara recension and provides a historical-comparative introduction linking it to the Naciketas legend in Taittirīya-Brāhmaṇa III.11.8 and the early philosophical strata of the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka. He flags I.iii.17–18 as probable phala-śruti interpolation and reads the aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa as an early form of the puruṣa-tattva.","Śrī Aurobindo (Kena and Other Upanishads, Pondicherry 1952; partial Kaṭha 1909–10)","Aurobindo's incomplete but luminous Kaṭha commentary (covering chiefly Vallī I.i–I.iii.9) reads the text as a Yoga of integral ascent: Naciketā is the awakened soul rejecting the lower triple boon for the supreme Real; Yama is the discriminative dharma-power; the chariot is the integrated being of body–mind–intellect–puruṣa. The aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa is the central Person whose discovery is the gateway to amṛtatva."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Śānti-pāṭha","title":"","sa":["ॐ सह नाववतु । सह नौ भुनक्तु । सह वीर्यं करवावहै ।","तेजस्वि नावधीतमस्तु । मा विद्विषावहै ।","ॐ शान्तिः शान्तिः शान्तिः ॥"],"iast":["oṃ saha nāv avatu | saha nau bhunaktu | saha vīryaṃ karavāvahai |","tejasvi nāv adhītam astu | mā vidviṣāvahai |","oṃ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ śāntiḥ ||"],"words":[],"lit":"May (Brahman) protect us both (teacher and pupil); may He sustain us both; may we work together in vigour; may our study together be brilliantly effective; may we not quarrel. Om, peace, peace, peace.","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Kaṭha shares its peace-invocation with the Taittirīya Upaniṣad (Brahmānanda-Vallī, prātaḥ-śānti)."]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya I · Vallī i","u":[{"ref":"Mantra I.i.1","title":"","sa":["ॐ उशन् ह वै वाजश्रवसः सर्ववेदसं ददौ । तस्य ह नचिकेता नाम पुत्र आस"],"iast":["oṃ uśan ha vai vājaśravasaḥ sarvavedasaṃ dadau | tasya ha naciketā nāma putra āsa"],"words":[["uśan","pres. act. ptcp. nom.sg.m. of √vaś (to wish)","wishing (the fruit of sacrifice)"],["ha vai","emphatic indeclinable pair","verily, it is told"],["vājaśravasaḥ","nom.sg.m. (proper name / patronymic)","the son of Vājaśravas (= Uddālaka Āruṇi)"],["sarva-vedasaṃ","acc.sg.n. tatpuruṣa cpd.","his whole wealth"],["dadau","perf. 3.sg.act. of √dā","he gave away"],["tasya","gen.sg.m.","his"],["naciketā","nom.sg.m. proper name","Naciketas (by name)"],["nāma","adv. acc.sg.n.","by name"],["putraḥ","nom.sg.m.","a son"],["āsa","perf. 3.sg. of √as","there was"]],"lit":"Verily, desiring (the fruit), Vājaśravas's son gave away all his wealth. He had a son, Naciketā by name.","para":"The narrative frame opens with the viśva-jit sacrifice. Vājaśravasa (= Uddālaka Āruṇi, per Śaṅkara), seeking heaven, performs the ritual that requires giving away the totality of one's wealth (sarva-vedasam). He has a son, Naciketā, who will soon expose the spiritual emptiness of his father's gift.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘uśan’ = phalakāmaḥ; the giver acts from desire (kāma), the lowest motive for ritual. Sarva-vedasam dadau is the lakṣaṇa of the viśva-jit; the bhāṣya stresses that Naciketā's whole intervention is to substitute brahma-jijñāsā for kāma-prayukta-karma."],["Rangarāmānuja","The opening sets up bhakti as the corrective: the father's outward kāma will be redeemed by the son's inward turning to the Lord. Vājaśravas = the line of those who hear (śravas) for the sake of food (vāja) — Rangarāmānuja foregrounds śravaṇa as Vedānta's first instrument."],["Madhva","Madhva identifies Vājaśravas as Auddālaki Āruṇi following the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka tradition. ‘Naciketā’ is etymologised as na-cikitsate, ‘he who does not waver’ — a foretaste of his steadfastness before Yama."],["Sivānanda","Even the highest external sacrifice is incomplete without inner enquiry. The father's fame-seeking ritual is precisely the situation that breeds disillusion in the discerning son. This is the seed of all genuine spiritual life."],["Deussen","The frame story has a Brāhmaṇa parallel (Taittirīya-Brāhmaṇa III.11.8). Deussen sees in this opening the Upaniṣadic critique of mere ritualism, dating the redaction to the late-Brāhmaṇa transitional period."],["Aurobindo","Vājaśravas symbolises the outer life of getting and giving — even his vast sacrifice is ‘all that he had’ in the outward sense alone. The son represents the dawning soul-consciousness for whom the outer giving becomes the question of the inner Self."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.2","title":"","sa":["तं ह कुमारं सन्तं दक्षिणासु नीयमानासु श्रद्धाविवेश सोऽमन्यत"],"iast":["taṃ ha kumāraṃ santaṃ dakṣiṇāsu nīyamānāsu śraddhāviveśa so'manyata"],"words":[["taṃ","acc.sg.m. (Naciketā)","him"],["ha","emph. indecl.","verily"],["kumāraṃ santaṃ","acc.sg.m. + pres.ptcp. acc.sg.m.","while he was still a boy"],["dakṣiṇāsu","loc.pl.f. of dakṣiṇā (sacrificial gift)","when the ritual gifts (cows) were being led out"],["nīyamānāsu","pass.pres.ptcp. loc.pl.f. of √nī","while they were being led"],["śraddhā","nom.sg.f.","faith / earnest reflection"],["āviveśa","perf. 3.sg. of ā-√viś","entered (him)"],["saḥ amanyata","pron. 3.sg.m. + impf. 3.sg. of √man","he reflected"]],"lit":"Into him, still a boy, while the dakṣiṇā-cows were being led away, faith entered. He reflected (thus):","para":"Although a child, Naciketā watches the procession of gift-cows — and observes that they are no longer fit gifts. Śraddhā (here = penetrating earnest insight, not mere belief) enters him; he reflects on what this defective sacrifice will yield his father.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śraddhā is glossed as āstikya-buddhi-viśeṣa, a particular kind of intellect convinced of the reality of the unseen. The child's response is not emotion but jñāna-mūla-vicāra."],["Rangarāmānuja","Śraddhā here = the preparatory bhakti-pūrvika that initiates the search. The child's spontaneous discernment shows that the spiritual aptitude is not age-bound."],["Madhva","Madhva treats Naciketā's reflection as evidence of pūrva-saṃskāra — past-life merit manifesting in this boy. The śraddhā is itself īśvara-prasāda-janyā."],["Sivānanda","Notice that even a boy with viveka surpasses an elder without it. Śraddhā is the entry-point of the spiritual life; without it no progress is possible."],["Deussen","Faith enters him: a specifically religious-philosophical conviction takes hold. Deussen parallels this to the awakening of philosophical wonder in early Greek thinkers."],["Aurobindo","Śraddhā is the soul's own light entering the consciousness — the inner reply of the Spirit to the outer call of the truth that the senses imperfectly transmit."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.3","title":"","sa":["पीतोदका जग्धतृणा दुग्धदोहा निरिन्द्रियाः । अनन्दा नाम ते लोकास्तान् स गच्छति ता ददत्"],"iast":["pītodakā jagdhatṛṇā dugdhadohā nirindriyāḥ | anandā nāma te lokāstān sa gacchati tā dadat"],"words":[["pīta-udakāḥ","cpd. nom.pl.f. (water-drunk)","whose water has been drunk up"],["jagdha-tṛṇāḥ","cpd. nom.pl.f. (grass-eaten)","whose grass has been chewed up"],["dugdha-dohāḥ","cpd. nom.pl.f. (milked-of-milk)","whose milk has been drained"],["nir-indriyāḥ","bv. cpd. nom.pl.f.","devoid of (reproductive) power"],["anandāḥ nāma","nom.pl.m. + adv.","called the joyless-worlds"],["te lokāḥ","nom.pl.m. dem. + noun","those worlds"],["tān saḥ gacchati","acc.pl.m. + pron. + pres.3.sg.","to those he goes"],["tāḥ dadat","acc.pl.f. + pres.act.ptcp. nom.sg.m.","giving them away"]],"lit":"Their water already drunk, their grass already eaten, their milk already drawn, barren — ‘joyless’ are the worlds to which one goes by giving such (cows away).","para":"Naciketā's silent indictment: the gift-cows are past their usefulness. By the ritual logic of ‘gift = world earned’, his father's offering will earn him only worlds named anandāḥ — the joyless realms — not the svarga he seeks.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śaṅkara reads anandāḥ as an-andāḥ ‘joyless’, but notes the variant ānandāḥ ‘blissful’ in some recensions — and dismisses the latter as inverting the sense: an inferior gift cannot yield a superior world. The verse is an argument by inversion of dakṣiṇā-vidhi."],["Rangarāmānuja","The verse exposes a-pātra-dāna — gifting to the unworthy or gifting the worthless. Both vitiate the puṇya. Only that which is given with bhāva yields the lokas of bliss."],["Madhva","Madhva is firm: nirindriyāḥ = past child-bearing. The verse establishes the principle that fruit must match the substance of the offering — a doctrine he extends throughout Vedānta in his pramāṇa-vivāka."],["Sivānanda","The boy sees what his father will not: external sacrifice without inner sincerity is a transaction of dross. The teaching is that we must give what is dear, not what is depleted."],["Deussen","Deussen calls this the most ancient surviving fragment of internal ritual critique — anticipating the Mīmāṃsā-Vedānta tension by centuries."],["Aurobindo","The outer sacrifice is hollow when it does not arise from the truth of the being. Naciketā's perception is the first spark of the inner sacrifice that the Upaniṣad will unfold."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.4","title":"","sa":["स होवाच पितरं तत कस्मै मां दास्यसीति । द्वितीयं तृतीयं तं होवाच मृत्यवे त्वा ददामीति"],"iast":["sa hovāca pitaraṃ tata kasmai māṃ dāsyasīti | dvitīyaṃ tṛtīyaṃ taṃ hovāca mṛtyave tvā dadāmīti"],"words":[["saḥ ha uvāca","pron. + emph. + perf. 3.sg. of √vac","he said"],["pitaraṃ tata","acc.sg.m. + voc. (father)","to his father, \"O father\""],["kasmai","dat.sg. interr.","to whom"],["māṃ dāsyasi iti","acc.sg. + fut. 2.sg. + quotative","\"will you give me?\""],["dvitīyaṃ tṛtīyaṃ","acc.sg.n. ord.","a second time, a third time"],["taṃ ha uvāca","acc.sg.m. + perf. 3.sg.","(the father) said to him"],["mṛtyave","dat.sg.m.","to Death"],["tvā dadāmi iti","enclitic 2.sg.acc. + pres.1.sg. + quotative","\"I give thee\""]],"lit":"He said to his father, ‘O father, to whom will you give me?’ A second and a third time. (The father) said: ‘To Death I give thee.’","para":"The boy presses the question — confronting his father with the inconsistency: if you give everything, you must also give me. Goaded by the boy's persistence, the father blurts out, ‘To Death!’ — words that he cannot now retract.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śaṅkara's bhāṣya emphasises the threefold pressing (trir uktaṃ satyaṃ bhavati): only after the third asking does the father's vow bind. The father's anger is a passing kopa, not a deliberate dispatch."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord's working is visible in the father's involuntary speech: it is the divine will that Naciketā meet Yama, not the father's intention. The line carries no ritual culpability on the father."],["Madhva","Madhva treats this as a satya-vacana that even the father, by Vedic principle, cannot retract. The whole subsequent dialogue rests on the inviolability of the spoken vow."],["Sivānanda","A spiritually mature son turns his father's careless word into a doorway. He does not complain; he goes. The teaching: take whatever life gives as an opportunity for awakening."],["Deussen","Deussen reads the threefold question as a folk-narrative motif (Märchenzahl) deepened into ritual seriousness — a typical Upaniṣadic re-purposing of older story-fragments."],["Aurobindo","The soul itself asks: ‘To whom will you give me?’ The father (= the outer mind) replies in anger, ‘To death’ — and the soul accepts this as the very path to the deathless."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.5","title":"","sa":["बहूनामेमि प्रथमो बहूनामेमि मध्यमः । किं स्विद्यमस्य कर्तव्यं यन्मयाऽद्य करिष्यति"],"iast":["bahūnāmemi prathamo bahūnāmemi madhyamaḥ | kiṃ svidyamasya kartavyaṃ yanmayā'dya kariṣyati"],"words":[["bahūnām","gen.pl.m.","among many"],["emi prathamaḥ","pres.1.sg. of √i + nom.sg.m.","I go (rank) as first"],["bahūnām emi madhyamaḥ","similarly","among many I rank as middle"],["kiṃ svid","interr. + indecl.","what then"],["yamasya","gen.sg.m.","of Yama"],["kartavyam","gerundive nom.sg.n.","is to be done"],["yat mayā adya kariṣyati","rel. + 1.sg.instr. + adv. + fut.3.sg.","that he (Yama) will do with me today"]],"lit":"Among many (sons) I go as first; among many as middling. What is Yama's purpose that, by means of me, he will accomplish today?","para":"Naciketā reasons: I am not the worst of sons; my father has no cause to dispatch me to Death. Therefore there must be some purpose latent in this — what does Yama have in mind to do through me?","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Bahūnām prathamaḥ = among many sons I am foremost in śuśrūṣā (filial service). Naciketā exonerates himself: the gift to Yama is not punishment but providence."],["Rangarāmānuja","The boy's calm self-assessment is itself sāttvika ātma-jñāna in seed. His confidence is not pride but the recognition that the Lord is working through this episode."],["Madhva","The reading is straightforward: a good son questioning a hasty word. Madhva uses the verse to underline that even a father's word must be examined for its dharma-status."],["Sivānanda","Notice the absence of self-pity. The discerning soul accepts the situation and asks, ‘What is this for?’ — the question of viveka."],["Deussen","Deussen parallels this to Job's reflective acceptance — a wisdom-literature motif."],["Aurobindo","The soul measures itself against the lower selves and finds itself sound; it accepts the dispatch to Death as a calling, not a condemnation."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.6","title":"","sa":["अनुपश्य यथा पूर्वे प्रतिपश्य तथाऽपरे । सस्यमिव मर्त्यः पच्यते सस्यमिवाजायते पुनः"],"iast":["anupaśya yathā pūrve pratipaśya tathā'pare | sasyamiva martyaḥ pacyate sasyamivājāyate punaḥ"],"words":[["anupaśya","imv. 2.sg. of anu-√paś","look back"],["yathā pūrve","rel.adv. + nom.pl.m.","how the men of old (did)"],["pratipaśya","imv. 2.sg. of prati-√paś","look forward (at)"],["tathā apare","so + nom.pl.m.","how the others (will do)"],["sasyam iva","nom.sg.n. + indecl.","like corn"],["martyaḥ","nom.sg.m.","mortal man"],["pacyate","pres.pass. 3.sg. of √pac","ripens (and falls)"],["sasyam iva ajāyate punaḥ","nom.sg.n. + indecl. + pres.3.sg. + adv.","like corn is born again"]],"lit":"Look back at how the ancients fared; look forward at how those (now living) will fare. Like corn, a mortal ripens; like corn, he is born again.","para":"Naciketā consoles his father with a deep philosophical observation: human life is a perishable harvest, repeated. Why should his father grieve over a son's death when it is the universal lot? The boy already grasps saṃsāra.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The verse establishes the doctrine of birth and rebirth implicitly: sasya = annual crop, renewed each season. The mortal's fate is no different. Śaṅkara reads this as Naciketā's instruction to his father."],["Rangarāmānuja","Even the ancestors were given to Death; lamenting one's son's destiny only delays insight. The proper response is dispassion (vairāgya), the first step to mokṣa."],["Madhva","Madhva stresses the punaḥ ajāyate — rebirth is real; the jīva continues. This is a key early Upaniṣadic affirmation of saṃsāra, against any nihilist reading."],["Sivānanda","The teaching of impermanence is delivered by the dying-going son to the grieving father. This inversion is the Upaniṣad's pedagogy: the young teach the old when wisdom asks."],["Deussen","A folk-proverbial cadence: sasyam iva twice repeated. Deussen calls this one of the earliest explicit affirmations of the doctrine of rebirth in the Upaniṣadic corpus."],["Aurobindo","The soul views the wheel of birth and death from a height already half-emancipated. Naciketā speaks already as one freed from grief because he is freed from the illusion of the merely outer life."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.7","title":"","sa":["वैश्वानरः प्रविशत्यतिथिर्ब्राह्मणो गृहान् । तस्यैतां शान्तिं कुर्वन्ति हर वैवस्वतोदकम्"],"iast":["vaiśvānaraḥ praviśatyatithirbrāhmaṇo gṛhān | tasyaitāṃ śāntiṃ kurvanti hara vaivasvatodakam"],"words":[["vaiśvānaraḥ","nom.sg.m.","Vaiśvānara (= Agni, the fire-of-all-men)"],["praviśati","pres.3.sg. of pra-√viś","enters"],["atithiḥ","nom.sg.m.","as a guest"],["brāhmaṇaḥ","nom.sg.m.","a brāhmaṇa"],["gṛhān","acc.pl.m.","houses"],["tasya etāṃ śāntiṃ","gen. + acc.f. dem. + acc.sg.f.","for him this propitiation"],["kurvanti","pres.3.pl. of √kṛ","they perform"],["hara vaivasvata-udakam","imv. + voc. + acc.sg.n.","\"O son-of-Vivasvant (Yama), fetch water\""]],"lit":"Vaiśvānara (= Fire) enters houses as a brāhmaṇa-guest. For him this propitiation is performed: ‘O Vaivasvata (Yama), fetch the water (of welcome).’","para":"Naciketā arrives at Yama's house but Yama is away. The text recalls the ritual rule that an uninvited brāhmaṇa-guest is Agni himself, who must be received with water. The voice now warns Yama (through his household) that the boy has waited three nights unfed — a grave breach.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The verse is in the vocative tone of Yama's household servants speaking to Yama on his return. Vaiśvānara-Agni is the latent fire in every brāhmaṇa-guest; failure to welcome him burns the host's puṇya."],["Rangarāmānuja","The hospitality-doctrine (atithi-deva) is grounded in the Lord indwelling in every brāhmaṇa. Yama, as cosmic functionary, is bound by the very dharma he administers."],["Madhva","Madhva enumerates the items of the ārghya-vidhi: pādya, arghya, ācamana. Each one missed annuls a specific puṇya — the doctrine of yathā-yathā-puṇya-nāśa."],["Sivānanda","The fire in the guest is not metaphor: spiritual energy concentrated in the brāhmaṇa-atithi is real. Disrespect to him produces karmic injury as surely as fire burns flesh."],["Deussen","Echoes Atharvaveda IX.6 (atithi-sūkta). Deussen notes the deep Vedic-ritual substrate still active in this Upaniṣad's pedagogy."],["Aurobindo","The fire that enters as guest is the inner Agni that the seeker carries to every door. How the door receives him determines the cosmic fruits."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.8","title":"","sa":["आशाप्रतीक्षे संगतं सूनृतां चेष्टापूर्ते पुत्रपशूंश्च सर्वान् । एतद्।ह्वृङ्क्ते पुरुषस्याल्पमेधसो यस्यानश्नन्वसति ब्राह्मणो गृहे"],"iast":["āśāpratīkṣe saṃgataṃ sūnṛtāṃ ceṣṭāpūrte putrapaśūṃśca sarvān | etad|hvṛṅkte puruṣasyālpamedhaso yasyānaśnanvasati brāhmaṇo gṛhe"],"words":[["āśā-pratīkṣe","acc.dual.f. (hope and expectation)","hope and expectation"],["saṃgataṃ sūnṛtām","acc.sg.f. friendly-converse","pleasant intercourse"],["ceṣṭā-pūrte","acc.dual.n. (ritual-works and meritorious-works)","sacrifices and good works"],["putra-paśūn ca sarvān","cpd. acc.pl.m. + nom.acc.pl.","sons and cattle, all (of them)"],["etat vṛṅkte","acc.sg.n. + pres.3.sg.middle of √vṛj","it (= the negligence) takes away"],["puruṣasya alpa-medhasaḥ","gen.sg.m. + cpd. gen.sg.m.","of the man of little understanding"],["yasya anaśnan vasati","rel. + neg.pres.ptcp. + pres.3.sg.","in whose (house) he resides unfed"],["brāhmaṇaḥ gṛhe","nom.sg. + loc.sg.","a brāhmaṇa, in the house"]],"lit":"Hope and expectation, pleasant fellowship, ritual works and merits, sons and cattle, all — (such are) taken away from the dull-witted man in whose house a brāhmaṇa dwells unfed.","para":"The household voice enumerates seven things the offending host loses: hope, expectation, social bond, sacrifices, charity, progeny, cattle. The boy's three unfed nights threaten Yama with cosmic damage.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śaṅkara enumerates the seven losses precisely: āśā (immediate desire), pratīkṣā (future expectation), saṃgata (companionship), sūnṛtā (sweet speech), iṣṭa (ritual), pūrta (charity), progeny-and-cattle. The verse functions as Yama's dharma-instruction by his own household."],["Rangarāmānuja","The seven items together constitute all human welfare. The Lord protects them only when his form-in-the-guest is honoured. The verse is a strong injunction."],["Madhva","Madhva treats the verse as a satya-vacanam binding even on Yama. The threefold dharma (yajña, dāna, tapas) is voided by a single act of atithi-anādara."],["Sivānanda","A practical reminder: hospitality is not optional but constitutive of householder dharma. Neglect of the unannounced guest erodes the very fabric of the home's protection."],["Deussen","The ‘seven losses’ formula has Atharvavedic parallels. Deussen reads it as quasi-magical in origin, sublimated here into ethical-religious doctrine."],["Aurobindo","All the seven goods belong to the soul's outer fields; they are sustained from a deeper centre. Slight that centre, and the outer goods drain away."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.9","title":"","sa":["तिस्रो रात्रीर्यदवात्सीर्गृहे मे ऽनश्नन् ब्रह्मन्नतिथिर्नमस्यः । नमस्तेऽस्तु ब्रह्मन् स्वस्ति मेऽस्तु तस्मात्प्रति त्रीन्वरान्वृणीष्व"],"iast":["tisro rātrīryadavātsīrgṛhe me 'naśnan brahmannatithirnamasyaḥ | namaste'stu brahman svasti me'stu tasmātprati trīnvarānvṛṇīṣva"],"words":[["tisraḥ rātrīḥ","acc.pl.f.","three nights"],["yat avātsīḥ","rel. + aor. 2.sg. of √vas","(in that) thou hast dwelt"],["gṛhe me","loc.sg. + gen.1.sg.","in my house"],["anaśnan","pres.act.ptcp. nom.sg.m. neg.","unfed"],["brahman atithir namasyaḥ","voc. + nom.sg.m. + gerundive","O brāhmaṇa, a guest worthy of homage"],["namaḥ te astu brahman","nom.n. + dat.2.sg. + 3.sg.imv.","homage be to thee, O brāhmaṇa"],["svasti me astu","nom.n. + dat.1.sg. + 3.sg.imv.","let well-being be mine"],["tasmāt prati trīn varān vṛṇīṣva","abl. + acc.pl. + acc.pl.m. + imv. 2.sg.","therefore, in return, choose three boons"]],"lit":"Because thou hast dwelt three nights in my house unfed, O brāhmaṇa, a guest worthy of homage — homage to thee, O brāhmaṇa; let well-being be mine. Therefore, in return, choose three boons.","para":"Yama returns, makes formal homage, and offers Naciketā one boon for each night the boy waited unfed — the structural pivot on which the rest of the Upaniṣad turns.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Three boons exactly match three nights — a precise prāyaścitta. Śaṅkara underlines that the third boon will, by Yama's own institution, have to be brahma-vidyā, since lesser things have been offered first."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord's līlā: Yama, against his will, must offer the supreme jñāna. The orchestration of three nights and three boons is the divine pedagogical scheme."],["Madhva","Madhva notes that this is the locus classicus for the prāyaścitta-vidhi of having an uninvited brāhmaṇa unfed: one boon per night, no exceptions."],["Sivānanda","Yama's humility is exemplary. Even the Lord of Dharma admits his lapse and atones. The ego-less acknowledgement is the mark of a true elder."],["Deussen","The structural device (three nights / three boons) is folkloric in origin but here it becomes the architecture of the entire Upaniṣad's doctrine."],["Aurobindo","Dharma itself bows to the soul that has waited in fasting silence at the threshold of the supreme. Three boons = three doors progressively opening to the Real."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.10","title":"","sa":["शान्तसंकल्पः सुमना यथा स्याद् वीतमन्युर्गौतमो माऽभि मृत्यो । त्वत्प्रसृष्टं माऽभिवदेत्प्रतीत एतत् त्रयाणां प्रथमं वरं वृणे"],"iast":["śāntasaṃkalpaḥ sumanā yathā syād vītamanyurgautamo mā'bhi mṛtyo | tvatprasṛṣṭaṃ mā'bhivadetpratīta etat trayāṇāṃ prathamaṃ varaṃ vṛṇe"],"words":[["śānta-saṃkalpaḥ","cpd. nom.sg.m.","with calmed resolve"],["sumanāḥ yathā syāt","nom.sg.m. + rel. + opt.3.sg.","so that (he) becomes well-disposed"],["vīta-manyuḥ","cpd. nom.sg.m.","free from anger"],["gautamaḥ","nom.sg.m. (patronymic)","Gautama (= the father, Auddālaki)"],["mā abhi mṛtyo","proh. + voc.","O Death, (let him be) towards me (so)"],["tvat-pra-sṛṣṭam","past pass. ptcp. cpd. acc.sg.m.","dispatched by thee"],["mā abhi-vadet","proh. + 3.sg.opt. of √vad","let him speak to me (welcoming)"],["pratītaḥ","past pass. ptcp. nom.sg.m.","recognising (me)"],["etat trayāṇāṃ prathamaṃ varaṃ vṛṇe","dem. + gen.pl. + ord. + acc. + 1.sg.","this I choose as the first of the three boons"]],"lit":"(That my father) Gautama, his resolve calmed, well-disposed, free from anger, and recognising me as dispatched by thee, may speak to me (welcoming). This I choose as the first of the three boons.","para":"Boon One — Naciketā does not ask for himself, but for his father: that on his return Gautama should greet him calmly, without anger, his śoka resolved. A son's first thought is his father's peace.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The boy's first boon is for another's peace, not his own welfare — a moral exemplar. Śānta-saṃkalpa = ārjava + uparati of the father's troubled saṃkalpa about the son's fate."],["Rangarāmānuja","Naciketā exemplifies the bhakta who places others' welfare ahead of his own. The boon is not a transaction but a prayer of love."],["Madhva","The vīta-manyutā here is the cessation of the father's kopa from verse 4. Madhva notes the structural symmetry: the curse and the boon are both verbal acts requiring resolution."],["Sivānanda","Mark the order of priorities: father first, self last. The boy who has been condemned to death asks first for the comfort of the one who condemned him. This is dharma in person."],["Deussen","Deussen reads this as the moral sublimation of an old story-trope: the returnee's safe reception. The Upaniṣad turns it into an ethical paradigm."],["Aurobindo","The soul's first work is to set right its outer relations — to leave the lower nature calm before it ascends to the higher realms. Self-conquest begins with care for the other."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.11","title":"","sa":["यथा पुरस्ताद् भविता प्रतीत औद्दालकिरारुणिर्मत्प्रसृष्टः । सुखं रात्रीः शयिता वीतमन्युः त्वां ददृशिवान्मृत्युमुखात् प्रमुक्तम्"],"iast":["yathā purastād bhavitā pratīta auddālakirāruṇirmatprasṛṣṭaḥ | sukhaṃ rātrīḥ śayitā vītamanyuḥ tvāṃ dadṛśivānmṛtyumukhāt pramuktam"],"words":[["yathā purastāt","adv. + adv.","as (he was) before"],["bhavitā pratītaḥ","periphr.fut. 3.sg. + p.p.p.","he shall be — recognising thee"],["auddālakir āruṇiḥ","patronymics nom.sg.m.","Auddālaki Āruṇi"],["mat-prasṛṣṭaḥ","cpd. nom.sg.m.","dispatched by me"],["sukhaṃ rātrīḥ śayitā","adv. + acc.pl.f. + periph.fut.","he shall sleep peacefully through the nights"],["vīta-manyuḥ","cpd. nom.sg.m.","free from anger"],["tvāṃ dadṛśivān","2.sg.acc. + perf.ptcp. nom.sg.m.","having seen thee"],["mṛtyu-mukhāt pramuktam","cpd. abl.sg. + p.p.p. acc.sg.m.","released from the jaws of death"]],"lit":"He shall be as before, recognising (thee) — Auddālaki Āruṇi, dispatched by me; he shall sleep peacefully through the nights, free of anger, having seen thee released from the jaws of death.","para":"Yama grants the first boon in full: the father will recognise his son, hold no grievance, and rest easy. Yama refers to the father by his proper lineage-name to seal the personal nature of the grant.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śaṅkara emphasises mṛtyu-mukhāt pramuktam — the father is to know that his son was, by Yama's own dispensation, returned alive. The boon's fulfilment requires this exact recognition."],["Rangarāmānuja","Yama himself names the father — Auddālaki Āruṇi — by lineage. The Lord-in-Yama identifies and dignifies the bhakta's father, restoring familial peace."],["Madhva","Madhva uses Auddālaki Āruṇi to anchor the Naciketā story within the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka and Chāndogya genealogies. The same Āruṇi is Śvetaketu's father in Chāndogya VI."],["Sivānanda","Yama keeps his promise specifically and fully. There is no half-grant. This is the model of vow-keeping for the spiritual aspirant."],["Deussen","Deussen reads Auddālaki Āruṇi here as a literary cross-reference to the Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka tradition — the redactor consciously links these texts."],["Aurobindo","The first boon's fulfilment seals the outer order; the soul is freed to seek the higher."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.12","title":"","sa":["स्वर्गे लोके न भयं किंचनास्ति न तत्र त्वं न जरया बिभेति । उभे तीर्त्वाऽशनायापिपासे शोकातिगो मोदते स्वर्गलोके"],"iast":["svarge loke na bhayaṃ kiṃcanāsti na tatra tvaṃ na jarayā bibheti | ubhe tīrtvā'śanāyāpipāse śokātigo modate svargaloke"],"words":[["svarge loke","loc.sg.m. + loc.sg.m.","in the world of heaven"],["na bhayaṃ kiṃcana asti","neg. + nom.sg.n. + indef. + 3.sg.","there is no fear at all"],["na tatra tvaṃ","neg.adv. + 2.sg.nom.","thou (= Death) art not there"],["na jarayā bibheti","neg. + instr.f. + pres.3.sg.","nor does one fear from old age"],["ubhe tīrtvā aśanāyā-pipāse","acc.dual.f. + abs. + cpd.acc.dual","having crossed both, hunger and thirst"],["śoka-atigaḥ","cpd. nom.sg.m.","gone beyond grief"],["modate svarga-loke","pres.3.sg. + loc.sg.","rejoices in heaven-world"]],"lit":"In the heaven-world there is no fear whatsoever; thou (O Death) art not there; nor does one fear from old age. Having crossed both hunger and thirst, gone beyond grief, one rejoices in heaven.","para":"Naciketā now states the conditions of svarga to set up his second boon — knowledge of the fire-sacrifice that leads to heaven. Svarga is freedom from fear, age, hunger, thirst, grief — but not yet from death itself.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Crucial verse for Śaṅkara: svarga = relative freedom only. Yama is absent there (no death in the heavenly span), but svarga is itself time-bound; the dweller will fall back. Hence svarga is not paramārtha."],["Rangarāmānuja","Svarga is a real loka attained by the proper agni; but Vaikuṇṭha — the param padaṃ viṣṇoḥ — exceeds it. Naciketā will ask first for the door to svarga, then for what is beyond."],["Madhva","Svarga is a genuine higher abode for the puṇyavān; but its bhoga ends. The verse establishes that there are graded lokas, against any monistic conflation."],["Sivānanda","Note the negations: no fear, no death, no decay, no hunger, no grief. Svarga is by definition freedom from the lower five afflictions — but the supreme is freedom from saṃsāra itself."],["Deussen","Deussen identifies the svarga-description as Ṛgveda IX.113.7–9 echo — the soma-pavamāna's depiction of the heavenly world."],["Aurobindo","The first step out of mortality is into a freer condition still bound by time — a luminous interlude. The Upaniṣad will press past it to the true immortality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.13","title":"","sa":["स त्वमग्निं स्वर्ग्यमध्येषि मृत्यो प्रब्रूहि त्वं श्रद्दधानाय मह्यम् । स्वर्गलोका अमृतत्वं भजन्त एतद् द्वितीयेन वृणे वरेण"],"iast":["sa tvamagniṃ svargyamadhyeṣi mṛtyo prabrūhi tvaṃ śraddadhānāya mahyam | svargalokā amṛtatvaṃ bhajanta etad dvitīyena vṛṇe vareṇa"],"words":[["saḥ tvam","pron. + 2.sg.nom.","thou (who knowest this)"],["agniṃ svargyam","acc.sg.m. + acc.sg.m. (cpd.adj.)","the heaven-leading fire"],["adhyeṣi","pres.2.sg. of adhi-√i","thou knowest by study"],["mṛtyo","voc.sg.m.","O Death"],["prabrūhi tvaṃ","imv. 2.sg. + 2.sg.nom.","declare it, thou"],["śraddadhānāya mahyam","pres.act.ptcp. dat.sg.m. + dat.1.sg.","to me who am full of faith"],["svarga-lokāḥ amṛtatvaṃ bhajante","nom.pl. + acc.sg. + pres.3.pl.","the heaven-worlds enjoy (relative) immortality"],["etat dvitīyena vṛṇe vareṇa","dem. + instr.sg.m. + 1.sg.middle + instr.","this I choose with the second boon"]],"lit":"Thou knowest, O Death, that fire that leads to heaven; declare it to me, full of faith. The heaven-worlds enjoy (relative) immortality. This I choose with the second boon.","para":"Boon Two — Naciketā asks for the Nāciketa-agni, the fire-altar arrangement that secures the heavenly world. He is precise: he is not asking for heaven itself, but for the knowledge of the agni.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Adhyeṣi = thou holdest in repeated study. Śraddadhānāya — the qualified pupil. Śaṅkara stresses that this second boon is preparatory: ritual-fire-knowledge as karma-mārga, precursor to the jñāna of boon three."],["Rangarāmānuja","The agnir-vidyā is itself a śaraṇāgati-pūrvika upāya: through right ritual the bhakta is prepared for the supreme bhakti. The two boons are graded means."],["Madhva","Madhva identifies the agni as the prajāpati-agni (a specific Vedic altar form). The vidyā is taught with precise mantra-saṃkhyā; ritual-precision is irreducible."],["Sivānanda","Even the seeker of brahma-vidyā does not despise karma-vidyā. Naciketā first secures the lower (ritual heaven), only then asks for the higher (brahma)."],["Deussen","Deussen analyses the Nāciketa-agni-cayana as preserved in Taittirīya-Brāhmaṇa III.11.7–8 — a specific altar construction with its own metrical mantras."],["Aurobindo","The second boon is the secret of the inner fire that, once lit, leads through the heavens. But Naciketā will not stop there — the fire is a step, not a destination."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.14","title":"","sa":["प्र ते ब्रवीमि तदु मे निबोध स्वर्ग्यमग्निं नचिकेतः प्रजानन् । अनन्तलोकाप्तिमथो प्रतिष्ठां विद्धि त्वमेतं निहितं गुहायाम्"],"iast":["pra te bravīmi tadu me nibodha svargyamagniṃ naciketaḥ prajānan | anantalokāptimatho pratiṣṭhāṃ viddhi tvametaṃ nihitaṃ guhāyām"],"words":[["pra te bravīmi","pre-verb + dat.2.sg. + pres.1.sg.","I declare unto thee"],["tat u me nibodha","dem. + emph. + dat.1.sg. + imv.2.sg.","attend to me on this"],["svargyam agniṃ naciketaḥ","cpd. acc.sg.m. + voc.","the heaven-leading fire, O Naciketā"],["prajānan","pres.act.ptcp. nom.sg.m.","(I) knowing well"],["ananta-loka-āptim","cpd. acc.sg.f.","the gaining of the endless world"],["atho pratiṣṭhām","and + acc.sg.f.","and also (its) foundation"],["viddhi tvam etam","imv.2.sg. + 2.sg.nom. + acc.dem.","know thou this"],["nihitam guhāyām","p.p.p. + loc.sg.f.","(as) placed in the (heart-)cave"]],"lit":"I declare it unto thee; mark me. Knowing (it) well, (I declare) the heaven-leading fire, O Naciketā. Know this — the (means of) gaining the endless world and (its) foundation — placed in the cave (of the heart).","para":"Yama begins the teaching of the Nāciketa-agni. The agni is not merely an outer altar but a principle placed (nihitaṃ) within the guhā — the heart-cave. Already the ritual is being internalised toward the upcoming brahma-vidyā.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Guhāyāṃ nihitam — the agni-jñāna ultimately resides not on the altar but in the buddhi. Even the ritual boon becomes internal; the bhāṣya prepares for the brahma-jñāna by this interiorisation."],["Rangarāmānuja","Nihitam guhāyām = the antaryāmin established in the heart, of which the outer altar is the visible counterpart. The agni-vidyā is, even at this stage, theistic in core."],["Madhva","Madhva reads the verse strictly: the agni-vidyā has both an outer ritual form and an inner contemplative form; both are necessary, neither suffices alone."],["Sivānanda","Note the dual emphasis: ananta-loka-āpti (gaining the endless world) AND its pratiṣṭhā (foundation). The teacher always gives both the means and the ground."],["Deussen","Deussen sees ‘nihitam guhāyām’ as a key Upaniṣadic formula recurring at I.ii.12, II.i.7 — the heart-cave as the locus of the divine principle."],["Aurobindo","The Fire that opens the endless world is already the Fire hidden in the heart's cave — the outer ritual is only the diagram of this inner Agni-vidyā."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.15","title":"","sa":["लोकादिमग्निं तमुवाच तस्मै या इष्टका यावतीर्वा यथा वा । स चापि तत्प्रत्यवदद्यथोक्तं अथास्य मृत्युः पुनरेवाह तुष्टः"],"iast":["lokādimagniṃ tamuvāca tasmai yā iṣṭakā yāvatīrvā yathā vā | sa cāpi tatpratyavadadyathoktaṃ athāsya mṛtyuḥ punarevāha tuṣṭaḥ"],"words":[["loka-ādim agniṃ","cpd. acc.sg.m. + acc.sg.m.","the fire (which is) the source of the worlds"],["tam uvāca tasmai","acc.sg.m. + perf.3.sg. + dat.","he taught him this"],["yāḥ iṣṭakāḥ yāvatīḥ vā yathā vā","rel.nom.pl.f. + correl. + emph.","what bricks, how many, how arranged"],["saḥ ca api tat prati-avadat","pron. + emph. + dem. + perf.3.sg.","and he (Naciketā) repeated it back"],["yathā-uktam","adv.","just as taught"],["atha asya mṛtyuḥ","then + gen.sg.m. + nom.sg.m.","then his (= Naciketā's) Death (= Yama)"],["punar eva āha tuṣṭaḥ","adv. + emph. + perf.3.sg. + nom.","spoke once again, pleased"]],"lit":"(Yama) taught him that fire, the source of the worlds — what bricks (are required), how many, and how arranged. (Naciketā) repeated it back just as it was told; and then Death, pleased, spoke to him once again.","para":"Yama gives precise altar-instructions; Naciketā perfectly repeats them. Yama is pleased and, as a spontaneous bonus (not part of the three boons), offers an additional grace.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The verse establishes that the agni-vidyā has determinate ritual parameters (which bricks, how many, how arranged). Naciketā's perfect repetition shows the qualification of the adhikārin: śraddhā + retentive medhā."],["Rangarāmānuja","Loka-ādim = the very seed of the cosmos. The brick-arrangement is a yantra of the cosmos itself. Naciketā has the bhakta-buddhi to receive and repeat without error."],["Madhva","Madhva enumerates the brick-types (lokampṛṇā, etc.) by name; the agni-cayana is one of the most precise of all Vedic vidhis and admits no laxity."],["Sivānanda","Observation: a teaching is only received when it can be returned unbroken. Naciketā's repetition is the test of true learning — śravaṇa fruitful in dhāraṇā."],["Deussen","Deussen notes the loka-ādim = pra-jāpati-agni; the cosmic firstborn whose altar is constructed in 360 bricks (one per day of the year). A precise calendar ritual."],["Aurobindo","The Fire that is the first creation of the worlds is also the first creation of the seeker's yoga; Naciketā has received it perfectly because he has lit it within."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.16","title":"","sa":["तमब्रवीत् प्रीयमाणो महात्मा वरं तवेहाद्य ददामि भूयः । तवैव नाम्ना भविताऽयमग्निः सृङ्कां चेमामनेकरूपां गृहाण"],"iast":["tamabravīt prīyamāṇo mahātmā varaṃ tavehādya dadāmi bhūyaḥ | tavaiva nāmnā bhavitā'yamagniḥ sṛṅkāṃ cemāmanekarūpāṃ gṛhāṇa"],"words":[["tam abravīt prīyamāṇaḥ mahātmā","acc.sg. + impf.3.sg. + pres.ptcp. + nom.sg.m.","him said the high-souled one, well-pleased"],["varaṃ tava iha adya dadāmi bhūyaḥ","acc.sg. + gen.2.sg. + adv. + adv. + pres.1.sg. + adv.","I give thee here today a further boon"],["tava eva nāmnā bhavitā ayam agniḥ","gen.2.sg. + emph. + instr.sg. + periph.fut. + dem. + nom.","by thy very name shall this fire be (called)"],["sṛṅkām ca imām aneka-rūpām gṛhāṇa","acc.sg.f. + acc.dem. + cpd.acc.f. + imv.2.sg.","and accept this many-formed garland (sṛṅkā)"]],"lit":"Pleased, the great soul (Yama) said to him: ‘I give thee here today a further boon: by thy very name shall this fire henceforth be called. Accept also this many-formed sṛṅkā (garland).’","para":"Yama, beyond the three boons, grants Naciketā two further gifts: the eponym (this fire shall henceforth be the Nāciketa-agni) and a sṛṅkā — a wreath of jewels (per Śaṅkara) or, per some MSS, the technical apparatus of the rite itself.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Sṛṅkām = ratna-mayīṃ srajam, a garland of jewels — a tangible token of the grace. The naming of the fire is the lasting honour; the garland is the immediate symbol."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord-in-Yama grants name-fame as the secondary fruit; the bhakta receives both the vidyā and its association with his name through all later tradition."],["Madhva","Aneka-rūpām = many-formed: the sṛṅkā has many facets, like the agni-vidyā itself, which has many bricks, many mantras, many phala. Each form is enumerable."],["Sivānanda","The teacher's spontaneous grace exceeds the agreed contract. When the disciple is genuine, the guru gives more than was asked."],["Deussen","Deussen reads sṛṅkām with the variant sṛṅgām (peak) — referring to the topmost brick of the altar. The textual question is open."],["Aurobindo","The aspirant's name becomes part of the Yoga itself: the Fire is now Nāciketa, marked forever by the soul that first asked it of Death."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.17","title":"","sa":["त्रिणाचिकेतस्त्रिभिरेत्य सन्धिं त्रिकर्मकृत्तरति जन्ममृत्यू । ब्रह्मजज्ञं देवमीड्यं विदित्वा निचाय्येमां शान्तिमत्यन्तमेति"],"iast":["triṇāciketastribhiretya sandhiṃ trikarmakṛttarati janmamṛtyū | brahmajajñaṃ devamīḍyaṃ viditvā nicāyyemāṃ śāntimatyantameti"],"words":[["tri-nāciketaḥ","bv. cpd. nom.sg.m.","he who has the three-fold Nāciketa fire"],["tribhiḥ etya sandhim","instr.pl. + abs. + acc.sg.m.","having attained union with the three (= mother, father, teacher OR three Vedas)"],["tri-karma-kṛt","cpd. nom.sg.m.","doer of the threefold work (yajña, adhyayana, dāna)"],["tarati janma-mṛtyū","pres.3.sg. + acc.dual.","crosses birth and death"],["brahma-jajñam","cpd. acc.sg.m.","born of Brahman"],["devam īḍyaṃ","acc.sg.m. + gerundive acc.sg.m.","the praiseworthy deva"],["viditvā nicāyya","two abs.","having known, having contemplated"],["imāṃ śāntim atyantam eti","acc.dem.f. + acc.sg.f. + adv. + pres.3.sg.","attains this absolute peace"]],"lit":"He who has thrice (built) the Nāciketa-fire, attained union with the three, performed the threefold work — crosses birth and death. Knowing the deva born of Brahman, the worshipful, contemplating Him, he attains this peace, absolute.","para":"The phala of the Nāciketa-agni: it is not merely svarga but progressively transcendence. Three constructions of the altar, three classes of dharma, three forms of relation — all lead through ritual to the brahma-knowledge that ends rebirth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> tri-nāciketa = one who has constructed the Nāciketa fire three times. The three (tribhiḥ) are mother, father, teacher per one tradition; the three Vedas per another. The verse is the bridge from karma-mārga to jñāna-mārga."],["Rangarāmānuja","Brahma-jajñam = born of Brahman (= Nārāyaṇa). The agni-vidyā, perfectly performed, becomes itself the door to bhakti-mokṣa."],["Madhva","Madhva enumerates the three karmas as yajña, adhyayana, dāna — the three dharmas of the twice-born. The threefold-thrice ritual completes the karmādhikāra and qualifies for jñāna."],["Sivānanda","Karma, when perfected, leads to jñāna of its own accord. The verse is karma-mīmāṃsā transcended into Vedānta."],["Deussen","Deussen reads this verse and the next as possibly redactional — the phala-śruti that links the agni-vidyā firmly into the upaniṣadic ‘outgrowing of ritual’."],["Aurobindo","The three flames of the Nāciketa-fire are the body, life, and mind purified by sacrifice; their union opens the gate beyond birth and death."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.18","title":"","sa":["त्रिणाचिकेतस्त्रयमेतद्विदित्वा य एवं विद्वांश्चिनुते नाचिकेतम् । स मृत्युपाशान् पुरतः प्रणोद्य शोकातिगो मोदते स्वर्गलोके"],"iast":["triṇāciketastrayametadviditvā ya evaṃ vidvāṃścinute nāciketam | sa mṛtyupāśān purataḥ praṇodya śokātigo modate svargaloke"],"words":[["tri-nāciketaḥ trayam etat viditvā","bv.cpd.nom. + acc.sg.n. + dem. + abs.","the three-Nāciketa-knower, having known this triad"],["yaḥ evaṃ vidvān cinute nāciketam","rel. + adv. + pres.act.ptcp. + pres.3.sg.middle + acc.","who, thus knowing, performs the Nāciketa (rite)"],["saḥ mṛtyu-pāśān purataḥ praṇodya","pron. + cpd.acc.pl. + adv. + abs.","he, casting off the snares of death from before him"],["śoka-atigaḥ modate svarga-loke","cpd.nom. + pres.3.sg. + loc.sg.","gone beyond grief, rejoices in the heaven-world"]],"lit":"Whoever, knowing this threefold (instruction) thus, performs the Nāciketa-fire — he casts off death's snares before him, and, gone beyond grief, rejoices in the heaven-world.","para":"The phala-śruti continues: thus equipped, the performer overcomes death's bonds (in their lower form) and dwells in the heaven-world free from grief. The Upaniṣad has now completed the ritual boon; from I.ii onward, brahma-vidyā begins.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Trayam = the three: knowledge, fire-construction, and the performance. Mṛtyu-pāśān is a-mṛta in the lower sense (heaven), not the ultimate amṛtatva, which only the third boon will yield."],["Rangarāmānuja","The verse closes the karma-leg. Subsequent verses begin the bhakti-jñāna-leg. The pāśās of mṛtyu are loosed first; full amṛtatva awaits boon three."],["Madhva","Madhva closes the agni-section with a precise phala: svarga-loke modate — relative heaven, not yet param padam. The graded fruits doctrine is maintained."],["Sivānanda","Each step on the path has its own joy; the seeker should not despise the lower attainment while seeking the higher."],["Deussen","Deussen reads this as the formal close of the first vallī's ritual phase. The transition to philosophical doctrine at I.ii.1 is structurally marked."],["Aurobindo","The lower bonds of death loosen; the heaven-world opens; but the soul that has tasted the Fire's true name will not rest here."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.19","title":"","sa":["एष तेऽग्निर्नचिकेतः स्वर्ग्यो यमवृणीथा द्वितीयेन वरेण । एतमग्निं तवैव प्रवक्ष्यन्ति जनासः तृतीयं वरं नचिकेतो वृणीष्व"],"iast":["eṣa te'gnirnaciketaḥ svargyo yamavṛṇīthā dvitīyena vareṇa | etamagniṃ tavaiva pravakṣyanti janāsaḥ tṛtīyaṃ varaṃ naciketo vṛṇīṣva"],"words":[["eṣaḥ te agniḥ naciketaḥ","pron. + dat.2.sg. + nom.sg.m. + voc.","this is thy fire, O Naciketā"],["svargyaḥ","adj. nom.sg.m.","heaven-leading"],["yam avṛṇīthāḥ dvitīyena vareṇa","rel. + impf.2.sg.middle + instr.","which thou hast chosen with the second boon"],["etam agniṃ tava eva pravakṣyanti janāsaḥ","acc.dem. + gen.2.sg.emph. + fut.3.pl. + nom.pl.","this fire shall the people call by thy name"],["tṛtīyaṃ varaṃ naciketaḥ vṛṇīṣva","ord.acc. + acc.sg. + voc. + imv.2.sg.middle","choose thou the third boon, O Naciketā"]],"lit":"This is thy fire, O Naciketā, the heaven-leading, which thou hast chosen with the second boon. This fire the people shall call by thy very name. Now choose the third boon, O Naciketā.","para":"Yama formally closes boon two — naming the fire after the boy — and invites him to ask the third. This is the structural pivot: the Upaniṣad's doctrinal heart begins with boon three.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Yama formally closes the karma-grant and prompts the next. Śaṅkara marks this as the structural break: from here on, brahma-vidyā is the subject."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord readies the bhakta for the supreme gift. The third boon is the param-bhakti request that will redefine the dialogue."],["Madhva","Tṛtīyaṃ varam = the supreme; Madhva notes the structural climax. The dialogue's centre of gravity shifts from ritual to ontology."],["Sivānanda","After fulfilling the lesser, the teacher invites the disciple to ask the greater. The student is brought step by step."],["Deussen","The boon-by-boon structure is here used to layer the doctrine: karma (boon 2), jñāna (boon 3). The redactional architecture is masterful."],["Aurobindo","The outer fires lit, named, and given to the world — now the soul turns to the inner secret that even Death is reluctant to share."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.20","title":"","sa":["येयं प्रेते विचिकित्सा मनुष्ये ऽस्तीत्येके नायमस्तीति चैके । एतद्विद्यामनुशिष्टस्त्वयाऽहं वराणामेष वरस्तृतीयः"],"iast":["yeyaṃ prete vicikitsā manuṣye 'stītyeke nāyamastīti caike | etadvidyāmanuśiṣṭastvayā'haṃ varāṇāmeṣa varastṛtīyaḥ"],"words":[["yā iyaṃ prete vicikitsā manuṣye","rel. + dem. + loc.sg.m. + nom.sg.f. + loc.","this doubt about the dead man"],["asti iti eke","pres.3.sg. + quotative + nom.pl.","some (say) \"he is\""],["na ayam asti iti ca eke","neg. + dem. + 3.sg. + quot. + nom.pl.","and others (say) \"he is not\""],["etat vidyām","dem. + 1.sg.opt. of √vid","this would I know"],["anuśiṣṭaḥ tvayā ahaṃ","p.p.p. + instr.2.sg. + nom.1.sg.","I, taught by thee"],["varāṇāṃ eṣaḥ varaḥ tṛtīyaḥ","gen.pl. + nom.dem. + nom.ord.","of (all) boons, this (is) the third boon"]],"lit":"This doubt concerning a dead man — some say ‘he exists’, others ‘he does not exist’ — this would I know, taught by thee. This, among (all) boons, is my third boon.","para":"Boon Three — Naciketā cuts through to ultimate ontology: when a person dies, does anything remain? Some traditions affirm, some deny. He asks the unprecedented question of the Self's survival of bodily death. From Yama's response will unfold the brahma-vidyā.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śaṅkara's bhāṣya on this verse is foundational: vicikitsā = saṃśaya about ātma-svarūpa. Some affirm an enduring ātman, some (the Lokāyatas) deny. Naciketā asks Yama, who knows directly, to resolve it."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta asks the supreme ontological question: does the jīva survive? Rangarāmānuja reads the resolution that follows as the affirmation of the eternal jīva-and-Lord both."],["Madhva","Madhva makes this verse a cornerstone of his refutation of nihilism (śūnyavāda) and of monistic conflation. The vicikitsā is genuine; the answer must affirm the jīva's irreducible reality."],["Sivānanda","The supreme question, asked at last. Notice it is asked only after all lesser inquiries are exhausted. The soul comes to mokṣa-vicāra last, when nothing else satisfies."],["Deussen","Deussen reads the asti / na asti debate as evidence of an early proto-Cārvāka tradition active in the Upaniṣadic period. The question is historical as well as philosophical."],["Aurobindo","The supreme question of human existence is at last directly posed: ‘Is there or is there not?’ All philosophy turns here."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.21","title":"","sa":["देवैरत्रापि विचिकित्सितं पुरा न हि सुविज्ञेयमणुरेष धर्मः । अन्यं वरं नचिकेतो वृणीष्व मा मोपरोत्सीरति मा सृजैनम्"],"iast":["devairatrāpi vicikitsitaṃ purā na hi suvijñeyamaṇureṣa dharmaḥ | anyaṃ varaṃ naciketo vṛṇīṣva mā moparotsīrati mā sṛjainam"],"words":[["devaiḥ atra api vicikitsitam purā","instr.pl. + adv. + emph. + p.p.p. + adv.","even by the devas this was doubted of old"],["na hi suvijñeyam aṇuḥ eṣaḥ dharmaḥ","neg. + emph. + adj.nom. + nom.sg.m. + dem. + nom.","for not easily known is this subtle dharma"],["anyam varam naciketaḥ vṛṇīṣva","acc.sg.m. + acc. + voc. + imv.","choose another boon, O Naciketā"],["mā mā uparotsīḥ ati mā sṛja enam","proh.×2 + aor.2.sg. + adv. + imv. + acc.dem.","do not press me, release me from this"]],"lit":"Even the gods of old doubted this; for not easily knowable is this subtle dharma. Choose another boon, O Naciketā; do not press me — release me from this (question).","para":"Yama tries to dissuade the boy: even the devas have not penetrated this question; the subject is too subtle. He asks Naciketā to pick a different boon. The Upaniṣad will set up Yama's evasion in order to dramatise the test of the genuine adhikārin.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Yama's reluctance is not refusal but pareṣaṇa — testing the seriousness of the seeker. Aṇuḥ dharmaḥ = the subtle dharma, brahma-vidyā, knowable only to one of supreme adhikāra."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord-in-Yama tests the bhakta's nirvṛtti. The temptations to come are calibrated to expose any residual kāma. Only the steadfast can be given."],["Madhva","the devas themselves do not fully know; only Viṣṇu does. Yama warns truthfully, not falsely. The verse establishes the ontological hierarchy of knowers."],["Sivānanda","The teacher is right to test the disciple. A teaching given to the unqualified is wasted and dangerous."],["Deussen","Deussen sees in this verse the formal upākhyāna-device: the test of the disciple before the great teaching. Standard in mystery-traditions."],["Aurobindo","Death tries to send the soul back: ‘This is too subtle; ask for something else.’ The soul that yields here loses the path; the soul that presses on finds the secret."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.22","title":"","sa":["देवैरत्रापि विचिकित्सितं किल त्वं च मृत्यो यन्न सुज्ञेयमात्थ । वक्ता चास्य त्वादृगन्यो न लभ्यो नान्यो वरस्तुल्य एतस्य कश्चित्"],"iast":["devairatrāpi vicikitsitaṃ kila tvaṃ ca mṛtyo yanna sujñeyamāttha | vaktā cāsya tvādṛganyo na labhyo nānyo varastulya etasya kaścit"],"words":[["devaiḥ atra api vicikitsitam kila","instr.pl. + adv. + emph. + p.p.p. + indeed","indeed even by the devas it was doubted"],["tvam ca mṛtyo yan na sujñeyam āttha","2.sg. + voc. + rel. + adj. + perf.2.sg. of √ah","and thou, O Death, sayest it is not easily known"],["vaktā ca asya tvādṛk anyo na labhyaḥ","nom.sg.m. + gen.dem. + adj.nom. + nom. + neg. + adj.","an expounder of this like thee, another is not to be found"],["na anyaḥ varaḥ tulyaḥ etasya kaścit","neg. + nom.dem. + adj. + gen.dem. + indef.","no other boon, equal to this, (exists) at all"]],"lit":"Indeed, even the gods doubted it of old; and thou, O Death, sayest it is hard to know. An expounder of it equal to thee is not to be found; no other boon is equal to this — none.","para":"Naciketā holds his ground. The very difficulty of the question is the reason to ask of Yama; there is no other teacher. No substitute boon will satisfy.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Naciketā's reasoning is impeccable: the rarity of the question makes the rare teacher indispensable. Tvādṛg-anyaḥ — none like thee — is the recognition of unique guru-adhikāra."],["Rangarāmānuja","Only the Lord himself can teach the Lord's nature; Yama, as the Lord's deputy here, is the proximate śabda-pramāṇa. The bhakta knows no substitute."],["Madhva","Madhva treats Naciketā's reply as the model praśna of the qualified śiṣya: the question, once asked, must be pressed until answered. There is no other path to mokṣa-jñāna."],["Sivānanda","The qualified disciple does not let go. Spiritual perseverance is the test. ‘No other boon is equal’ — meaning, no compromise."],["Deussen","The dramatic exchange functions structurally: the disciple's perseverance qualifies him for the teaching that follows. A standard upākhyāna-form."],["Aurobindo","Death's hesitation is met by the soul's insistence. The soul knows that this question, and only this question, exhausts all questions."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.23","title":"","sa":["शतायुषः पुत्रपौत्रान्वृणीष्वा बहून्पशून् हस्तिहिरण्यमश्वान् । भूमेर्महदायतनं वृणीष्व स्वयं च जीव शरदो यावदिच्छसि"],"iast":["śatāyuṣaḥ putrapautrānvṛṇīṣvā bahūnpaśūn hastihiraṇyamaśvān | bhūmermahadāyatanaṃ vṛṇīṣva svayaṃ ca jīva śarado yāvadicchasi"],"words":[["śatāyuṣaḥ putra-pautrān vṛṇīṣva","cpd.acc.pl.m. + cpd.acc.pl. + imv.","choose sons and grandsons of a hundred years"],["bahūn paśūn hasti-hiraṇyam aśvān","acc.pl. + acc.pl. + cpd.acc.sg.n. + acc.pl.","many cattle, elephants, gold, horses"],["bhūmeḥ mahat āyatanam vṛṇīṣva","gen.f. + acc.adj. + acc.sg.n. + imv.","choose vast dominion of the earth"],["svayaṃ ca jīva śaradaḥ yāvat icchasi","self + and + imv. + acc.pl.f. + rel. + 2.sg.","and thou thyself live as many autumns as thou dost wish"]],"lit":"Choose sons and grandsons of a hundred years (each), many cattle, elephants, gold, horses; choose a vast dominion of the earth; and live thou thyself for as many autumns as thou wishest.","para":"Yama begins the trial of substitution: progeny, livestock, wealth, kingdom, longevity — all the objects of human desire are offered as a single counter-boon. The classical catalogue of preyas.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> this is the canonical list of preyas — the objects that are dear (preya) but not the supreme good (śreyas). The list is exhaustive of vyavahārika kāma."],["Rangarāmānuja","Material rewards including longevity — the totality of the kāmya-mārga is laid before the bhakta as the test. The temptation is complete."],["Madhva","The kāma-pradāna is offered as a real, fulfillable alternative. Madhva insists these are not illusions but lesser goods — really had, but really limited."],["Sivānanda","All that men desire offered in one sentence. The seeker is now tested: can he refuse even the totality of saṃsāric pleasures?"],["Deussen","Deussen catalogues the offer: progeny, longevity, wealth, dominion. The Upaniṣad makes Naciketā refuse them one by one, dramatising the doctrine of viveka."],["Aurobindo","Death offers the soul all the magnificences of the outer life — and waits to see whether the soul will be content with these splendours of the perishing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.24","title":"","sa":["एतत्तुल्यं यदि मन्यसे वरं वृणीष्व वित्तं चिरजीविकां च । महाभूमौ नचिकेतस्त्वमेधि कामानां त्वा कामभाजं करोमि"],"iast":["etattulyaṃ yadi manyase varaṃ vṛṇīṣva vittaṃ cirajīvikāṃ ca | mahābhūmau naciketastvamedhi kāmānāṃ tvā kāmabhājaṃ karomi"],"words":[["etat-tulyam yadi manyase varaṃ","cpd.acc. + cond. + 2.sg.opt. + acc.","if thou regardest a boon as equivalent to this"],["vṛṇīṣva vittaṃ cira-jīvikām ca","imv. + acc.sg. + cpd.acc.f. + and","choose wealth and long-livedness"],["mahā-bhūmau naciketaḥ tvam edhi","loc.cpd. + voc. + 2.sg. + imv.2.sg. of √edh","on the vast earth, O Naciketā, do thou prosper"],["kāmānāṃ tvā kāma-bhājaṃ karomi","gen.pl. + 2.sg.acc. + cpd.acc.sg.m. + pres.1.sg.","I make thee an enjoyer of (all) desires"]],"lit":"If thou deemest any boon equivalent to this, choose wealth and long life; prosper on the vast earth, O Naciketā. I make thee an enjoyer of (all) desires.","para":"Yama redoubles the offer: kingdom, wealth, longevity, enjoyment of every desire — anything Naciketā can name as equivalent will be given. The trial intensifies.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Kāma-bhājam = an enjoyer in the full sense. The temptation is now to the kāma-loka-rāja status — divine kingship on earth. Yama deploys his full repertoire."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta is offered universal kingship as alternative. The Lord tests through Yama: every offered substitute is a means of measuring the seeker's nirvṛtti."],["Madhva","even sovereign kingship and full kāma-bhoga are within Yama's gift, since they are puṇya-phalas under his dispensation. The offer is genuine."],["Sivānanda","Each iteration of Yama's offer escalates. So too the seeker's resolve must escalate. The teacher's test is graduated, never trivial."],["Deussen","Deussen reads this as an early literary doublet — verses 23 and 24 form a couplet of increasing offer."],["Aurobindo","Death extends the bait: enjoy all desires, become king of all the earth. The soul must see clearly that even universal kingship is bounded and falls short of the Real."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.25","title":"","sa":["ये ये कामा दुर्लभा मर्त्यलोके सर्वान् कामांश्छन्दतः प्रार्थयस्व । इमा रामाः सरथाः सतूर्या न हीदृशा लम्भनीया मनुष्यैः । आभिर्मत्प्रत्ताभिः परिचारयस्व नचिकेतो मरणं माऽनुप्राक्षीः"],"iast":["ye ye kāmā durlabhā martyaloke sarvān kāmāṃśchandataḥ prārthayasva | imā rāmāḥ sarathāḥ satūryā na hīdṛśā lambhanīyā manuṣyaiḥ | ābhirmatprattābhiḥ paricārayasva naciketo maraṇaṃ mā'nuprākṣīḥ"],"words":[["ye ye kāmāḥ dur-labhāḥ martya-loke","rel.×2 + nom.pl. + adj.pl. + loc.cpd.","whatever desires are hard to attain in the mortal world"],["sarvān kāmān chandataḥ prārthayasva","acc.pl. + adv. + imv.2.sg.middle","ask freely for all desires"],["imāḥ rāmāḥ sa-rathāḥ sa-tūryāḥ","nom.pl.f. + adj. + adj.","these lovely women, with chariots, with music"],["na hi īdṛśāḥ lambhanīyāḥ manuṣyaiḥ","neg. + emph. + adj.nom.pl. + gerundive + instr.pl.","such as these are not obtainable by men"],["ābhiḥ mat-prattābhiḥ pari-cārayasva","instr.pl.f. + cpd.instr.pl. + imv.","let thyself be attended by these, given by me"],["naciketo maraṇaṃ mā anu-prākṣīḥ","voc. + acc.sg.n. + proh. + aor.2.sg.","O Naciketā, do not press concerning death"]],"lit":"Whatever desires are hard to obtain in the mortal world — for all desires ask freely. These lovely (heavenly) women, with chariots and music, such as cannot be had by (ordinary) men — given by me, let them attend thee. O Naciketā, do not press (me) concerning death.","para":"The third escalation: heavenly women (apsarases), chariots, music — the very pleasures of svarga. The boy is offered every conceivable enjoyment. Yama makes a final plea: ‘do not press me concerning death.’","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> the apsarases are offered to test even the supra-mundane kāma. Yama exhausts the trial. ‘Maraṇaṃ mā anuprākṣīḥ’ — the final personal plea."],["Rangarāmānuja","Even the divyāḥ rāmāḥ — celestial women — are offered. The Lord-in-Yama tests every stratum of kāma. Only the bhakta with paripūrṇa vairāgya passes."],["Madhva","The offer of apsarases is the highest form of svarga-bhoga, here brought to the mortal plane. Madhva: even this is a lesser phala; mokṣa stands beyond."],["Sivānanda","Note the comprehensive nature: every form of pleasure, every form of distraction. The Upaniṣad shows that pravṛtti has no upper bound except the seeker's own viveka."],["Deussen","A vivid passage. The catalogue of celestial enjoyments runs in the same metre as the earlier offers, building a literary climax."],["Aurobindo","All the heavenly seductions — the celestial femininity, the divine music, the chariots of the gods. The soul that turns from these is fit for the highest immortality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.26","title":"","sa":["श्वोभावा मर्त्यस्य यदन्तकैतत् सर्वेंद्रियाणां जरयन्ति तेजः । अपि सर्वं जीवितमल्पमेव तवैव वाहास्तव नृत्यगीते"],"iast":["śvobhāvā martyasya yadantakaitat sarveṃdriyāṇāṃ jarayanti tejaḥ | api sarvaṃ jīvitamalpameva tavaiva vāhāstava nṛtyagīte"],"words":[["śvaḥ-bhāvāḥ martyasya yat-antaka etat","adj.nom.pl. + gen.sg.m. + voc. + dem.","transient of tomorrow are these (pleasures), O Ender (= Yama)"],["sarva-indriyāṇāṃ jarayanti tejaḥ","gen.pl. + pres.3.pl. + acc.","they wear away the vigour of all the senses"],["api sarvam jīvitam alpam eva","emph. + nom. + nom. + nom. + emph.","indeed all life (-span) is short"],["tava eva vāhāḥ tava nṛtya-gīte","gen.2.sg.emph. + nom.pl. + gen.2.sg. + nom.dual.","let the chariots, the dance and song, remain with thee"]],"lit":"These (pleasures) are of tomorrow's making, O Ender — they wear out the vigour of all the senses. Indeed all life is short. Let the chariots, the dance and song remain with thee.","para":"Naciketā's first reply: these pleasures are evanescent (śvaḥ-bhāvāḥ — ‘what is here today is gone tomorrow’) and corrosive (they wear out the very senses they delight). Keep them, O Yama; they are not what I seek.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śvaḥ-bhāvāḥ = perishing on the morrow. The verse is the locus classicus for the Vedāntic doctrine of bhoga-jarā — that enjoyment itself ages the enjoyer."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta sees through the temptation immediately. Indriya-tejas, expended on kāma, is lost; expended on bhakti, it is amplified."],["Madhva","the kāma-bhoga has a real but finite effect; it actually does wear away the indriyas; the verse is empirical observation as much as ethical exhortation."],["Sivānanda","Pleasures actually corrode. This is not poetic exaggeration — observe the body and senses of those who have over-indulged. The verse is medical as much as moral."],["Deussen","Deussen calls śvaḥ-bhāvāḥ ‘ein einziges Wort, das die ganze Upaniṣad zusammenfasst’ — a single word that summarises the whole Upaniṣad's ethics."],["Aurobindo","The soul sees that even the heavenly pleasures are of tomorrow's making — perishables dressed in beauty — and asks for the Real that is of no day, of no tomorrow."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.27","title":"","sa":["न वित्तेन तर्पणीयो मनुष्यो लप्स्यामहे वित्तमद्राक्ष्म चेत्त्वा । जीविष्यामो यावदीशिष्यसि त्वं वरस्तु मे वरणीयः स एव"],"iast":["na vittena tarpaṇīyo manuṣyo lapsyāmahe vittamadrākṣma cettvā | jīviṣyāmo yāvadīśiṣyasi tvaṃ varastu me varaṇīyaḥ sa eva"],"words":[["na vittena tarpaṇīyaḥ manuṣyaḥ","neg. + instr.sg.n. + gerundive + nom.sg.m.","not by wealth is man to be satisfied"],["lapsyāmahe vittam adrākṣma cet tvā","fut.1.pl. + acc. + aor.1.pl. + cond. + 2.sg.acc.","shall we acquire wealth if we have seen thee?"],["jīviṣyāmaḥ yāvat īśiṣyasi tvam","fut.1.pl. + rel. + fut.2.sg. + 2.sg.nom.","we shall live (only) so long as thou dost reign"],["varaḥ tu me varaṇīyaḥ saḥ eva","nom.sg. + emph. + dat.1.sg. + gerundive + dem.","my boon, to be chosen, is just that (one)"]],"lit":"Man is not to be satisfied by wealth: shall we get wealth (when) we have seen thee? We shall live (only) so long as thou rulest. My one boon, the one to be chosen, is just that.","para":"Naciketā's second reply: wealth cannot satisfy; in any case, having met Death, what use is wealth? Our life-span itself depends on Yama. He returns to the same one boon — knowledge of what survives death.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> na vittena tarpaṇīyo manuṣyaḥ — the locus classicus of vairāgya. Even gained, wealth does not satisfy; and gained from Death's hand, it merely measures one's time."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta has seen the Lord-in-Yama; nothing less now suffices. Vittam — material wealth — is structurally beneath the bhakti-vidyā being sought."],["Madhva","tarpaṇīyaḥ in the negative is absolute. No quantity of vitta produces tṛpti; the verse establishes the principle of asantoṣa-in-bhoga."],["Sivānanda","The famous teaching: man is never satisfied with wealth. The verse is to be inscribed in the seeker's heart at the outset of the path."],["Deussen","Deussen quotes Schopenhauer's identical observation: ‘Reichtümer sind wie Seewasser; je mehr man trinkt, je durstiger wird man’ — wealth is like sea-water: the more one drinks, the thirstier one becomes."],["Aurobindo","All wealth is calculated by the seer in the light of the inevitable termination; he sees Death looking on as the universal counterweight to every gain."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.28","title":"","sa":["अजीर्यताममृतानामुपेत्य जीर्यन्मर्त्यः क्वधःस्थः प्रजानन् । अभिध्यायन् वर्णरतिप्रमोदान् अतिदीर्घे जीविते को रमेत"],"iast":["ajīryatāmamṛtānāmupetya jīryanmartyaḥ kvadhaḥsthaḥ prajānan | abhidhyāyan varṇaratipramodān atidīrghe jīvite ko rameta"],"words":[["a-jīryatām a-mṛtānām upetya","gen.pl. + gen.pl. + abs.","having approached the ageless, the deathless"],["jīryan martyaḥ kva adhaḥsthaḥ prajānan","pres.act.ptcp. + nom.sg. + interr.adv. + adj. + ptcp.","what aging mortal, situated below, knowing (his lot)"],["abhidhyāyan varṇa-rati-pramodān","pres.act.ptcp. + cpd.acc.pl.","pondering colour, love, pleasures"],["ati-dīrghe jīvite ko rameta","adj.loc. + loc.sg. + interr. + 3.sg.opt.","in an over-long life, who would take pleasure?"]],"lit":"Having approached the un-aging and deathless (gods), what mortal — himself aging, situated below (in saṃsāra), knowing his lot — pondering colour, love, pleasures, would take delight in over-long life?","para":"Naciketā delivers the philosophical thrust: having stood before the deathless, who could settle for prolonged mortality? The temporary cannot satisfy one who has tasted (the prospect of) the eternal.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> the contrast between ajara-amartya (the gods, here = Yama himself) and the jīryan-martya (aging mortal) is structural. Once seen, the lower can never satisfy."],["Rangarāmānuja","Once the bhakta has glimpsed amṛtatva, even śatāyu becomes burdensome. The verse is the ekāgrātā of vairāgya born of darśana."],["Madhva","Madhva reads this as the locus classicus of viraktitā: the seer of the amṛta does not linger over the mṛtya, however prolonged."],["Sivānanda","The soul that has tasted (or even glimpsed) the eternal finds the temporal flat. This is the natural sequel of darśana."],["Deussen","Deussen notes the formal mode of contrast: ajīryatām (gen.) of those + jīryan (ptcp.) of him — Sanskrit's grammar reinforces the philosophical point."],["Aurobindo","The soul has seen the deathless face; the dust of the earthly dance can no longer hold it. The verse is the song of awakened vairāgya."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.i.29","title":"","sa":["यस्मिन्निदं विचिकित्सन्ति मृत्यो यत्साम्पराये महति ब्रूहि नस्तत् । योऽयं वरो गूढमनुप्रविष्टो नान्यं तस्मान्नचिकेता वृणीते"],"iast":["yasminnidaṃ vicikitsanti mṛtyo yatsāmparāye mahati brūhi nastat | yo'yaṃ varo gūḍhamanupraviṣṭo nānyaṃ tasmānnaciketā vṛṇīte"],"words":[["yasmin idaṃ vicikitsanti mṛtyo","loc.sg.m. + nom.dem. + pres.3.pl. + voc.","about which they doubt, O Death"],["yat sāmparāye mahati brūhi nas tat","rel. + loc.sg.n. + adj. + imv. + dat.1.pl. + dem.","what (lies) in the great hereafter — tell us that"],["yaḥ ayaṃ varaḥ gūḍham anu-praviṣṭaḥ","rel. + dem. + nom.dem. + adj.acc. + p.p.p.","this boon, that has entered into the hidden"],["na anyaṃ tasmāt naciketā vṛṇīte","neg. + acc. + abl.dem. + nom.proper + pres.3.sg.middle","no other than that does Naciketā choose"]],"lit":"About what (the wise) doubt, O Death — what (lies) in the great hereafter — that, tell us. This boon, which has entered into the hidden (truth) — no other than that does Naciketā choose.","para":"Naciketā fixes the question with final precision: the doubt about post-mortem ontology, the ‘great hereafter’ (sāmparāya) — only that question, no substitute. The vallī ends with this absolute refusal to deflect.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Mahati sāmparāye = ‘in the great hereafter’ = in the supreme post-mortem state, i.e., mokṣa. Gūḍham anupraviṣṭam = the boon has entered into the hidden, the rahasya. Śaṅkara: this seals the boon's intent as brahma-vidyā."],["Rangarāmānuja","Sāmparāya here is param-padam-prāpti, the Lord's abode. The bhakta closes off every evasion. The Lord-in-Yama must now teach."],["Madhva","the mahati sāmparāye is the highest possible attainment — Viṣṇu-loka — beyond the svarga that the second boon secured. The graduated phala-stack is now complete."],["Sivānanda","The seeker's resolve is absolute. Until the supreme is taught, no consolation prize will be accepted. This is the qualified disciple."],["Deussen","Vallī closes with the boy's final formula. Deussen marks the structural pivot: the next vallī begins with Yama's actual teaching."],["Aurobindo","The soul has refused every concession. The question of the supreme Reality stands alone. Now Death must speak."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya I · Vallī ii","u":[{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.1","title":"","sa":["अन्यच्छ्रेयोऽन्यदुतैव प्रेय स्ते उभे नानार्थे पुरुषं सिनीतः । तयोः श्रेय आददानस्य साधु भवति हीयतेऽर्थाद्य उ प्रेयो वृणीते"],"iast":["anyacchreyo'nyadutaiva preya ste ubhe nānārthe puruṣaṃ sinītaḥ | tayoḥ śreya ādadānasya sādhu bhavati hīyate'rthādya u preyo vṛṇīte"],"words":[["anyat śreyaḥ","nom.sg.n. + nom.sg.n.","different (is) the preferable good"],["anyat uta eva preyaḥ","nom. + emph. + nom.sg.n.","different again (is) the pleasant"],["te ubhe nānā-arthe","nom.dual + adj.dual.f.","these two, having different aims"],["puruṣaṃ sinītaḥ","acc.sg.m. + pres.3.dual of √si","bind a man"],["tayoḥ śreyaḥ ādadānasya","gen.dual + acc.sg.n. + gen.pres.ptcp.","of him who takes hold of the śreyas of these two"],["sādhu bhavati","adv. + pres.3.sg.","it goes well (with him)"],["hīyate arthāt","pres.3.sg.pass. + abl.sg.m.","he falls from the (true) goal"],["yaḥ u preyaḥ vṛṇīte","rel. + emph. + acc.sg.n. + pres.3.sg.middle","who chooses the preyas"]],"lit":"Different is the śreyas, different again the preyas — these two of different aims bind a man. Of him who chooses the śreyas it goes well; he who chooses the preyas falls from the (true) goal.","para":"Yama opens the brahma-vidyā with the doctrinal pivot: there are two goods — the preferable (śreyas) and the pleasant (preyas). They look similar; they are not. The path divides here.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The two are not opposed in object but in puruṣārtha-orientation: śreyas tends to mokṣa, preyas to bhoga. Sinītaḥ — they bind (both ways); both engage the puruṣa, only one frees."],["Rangarāmānuja","Śreyas = bhagavat-prāpti; preyas = saṃsāra-rati. The two paths are objectively real and objectively divergent; the bhakta must discriminate at the outset."],["Madhva","Madhva insists on the metaphysical reality of the distinction: śreyas (mokṣa, viṣṇu-prāpti) and preyas (svarga and lesser) are both real attainments; only one is the supreme."],["Sivānanda","The foundational discrimination of the spiritual life. Every choice is a śreyas-preyas choice, however small. Train viveka by deciding rightly in small matters first."],["Deussen","Deussen parallels this to Plato's distinction of agathon and hēdy — the good and the pleasant. The Kaṭha formulation is older and sharper."],["Aurobindo","The two ways open out from every act of the will: the way of the higher Good and the way of the present pleasure. The whole evolution turns on the choice."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.2","title":"","sa":["श्रेयश्च प्रेयश्च मनुष्यमेतः तौ सम्परीत्य विविनक्ति धीरः । श्रेयो हि धीरोऽभि प्रेयसो वृणीते प्रेयो मन्दो योगक्षेमाद्।ह्वृणीते"],"iast":["śreyaśca preyaśca manuṣyametaḥ tau samparītya vivinakti dhīraḥ | śreyo hi dhīro'bhi preyaso vṛṇīte preyo mando yogakṣemād|hvṛṇīte"],"words":[["śreyaḥ ca preyaḥ ca manuṣyam etaḥ","nom.×2 + acc.sg. + pres.3.dual of √i","śreyas and preyas, these two, approach the man"],["tau samparītya vivinakti dhīraḥ","acc.dual + abs. + pres.3.sg. + nom.","having considered the two together, the wise one discriminates"],["śreyaḥ hi dhīraḥ abhi preyasaḥ vṛṇīte","nom.n. + emph. + nom. + adv. + abl.sg.n. + 3.sg.middle","for the wise prefers the śreyas above the preyas"],["preyaḥ mandaḥ yogakṣemāt vṛṇīte","nom.n. + nom.adj. + abl.cpd. + 3.sg.middle","the dull chooses preyas (only) for sake of getting and keeping (worldly goods)"]],"lit":"Śreyas and preyas both approach a man; the wise (dhīra), having considered both together, discriminates. The wise prefers śreyas above preyas; the dull chooses preyas for the sake of getting and keeping.","para":"Both options confront every person. Viveka is the act of seeing them side by side and choosing on principle, not impulse. Yogakṣema — acquisition (yoga) and retention (kṣema) of things — is the motive of the dull.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Samparītya = having taken the two together in cognition. The dhīra is defined here: not the learned but the discriminating. Yogakṣema-driven choice is mūḍha-vṛtti."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta is the dhīra of this verse. The mandaḥ is one whose intellect is still bound in karma-kāṇḍa-prayojana — even ritual undertaken for yoga-kṣema is preyas."],["Madhva","Madhva emphasises ‘abhi’ — preferentially. The wise actively chooses, not merely accepts. Yoga-kṣema is here glossed as artha-anartha-praviveka — discernment of gain and loss."],["Sivānanda","Yogakṣema = the cycle of acquisition and protection that occupies most lives. The wise step out of it; the dull are absorbed in it. The verse is a mirror for self-examination."],["Deussen","Yoga-kṣema as Vedic term for ‘livelihood’ recurs in Gītā 9.22 (yoga-kṣemaṃ vahāmy aham). The Kaṭha uses it pejoratively here."],["Aurobindo","The wise discrimination places the Higher above the dear; the dull mind is held captive by the seductions of getting and keeping the perishable."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.3","title":"","sa":["स त्वं प्रियान्प्रियरूपांश्च कामान् अभिध्यायन्नचिकेतोऽत्यस्राक्षीः । नैतां सृङ्कां वित्तमयीमवाप्तो यस्यां मज्जन्ति बहवो मनुष्याः"],"iast":["sa tvaṃ priyānpriyarūpāṃśca kāmān abhidhyāyannaciketo'tyasrākṣīḥ | naitāṃ sṛṅkāṃ vittamayīmavāpto yasyāṃ majjanti bahavo manuṣyāḥ"],"words":[["saḥ tvaṃ priyān priya-rūpān ca kāmān","dem. + 2.sg. + acc.pl. + cpd.acc.pl. + and + acc.pl.","thou — (over) dear and dear-formed desires"],["abhidhyāyan naciketaḥ ati-asrākṣīḥ","pres.ptcp.nom. + voc. + aor.2.sg. of ati-√sṛj","reflecting, O Naciketā, hast cast away"],["na etāṃ sṛṅkām vitta-mayīm avāptaḥ","neg. + acc.dem.f. + cpd.acc.sg.f. + p.p.p.","thou hast not taken this wealth-built garland (= the lure of riches)"],["yasyāṃ majjanti bahavaḥ manuṣyāḥ","loc.rel.f. + pres.3.pl. + nom.pl. + nom.","in which many men sink"]],"lit":"Thou, O Naciketā, having pondered (well), hast cast away (the) desires — dear, dear-formed; thou hast not seized this wealth-garland in which many men sink.","para":"Yama acknowledges the disciple's qualification: Naciketā has actively rejected (atyasrākṣīḥ) the kāmas of vv. 23–25 — including the apsarases-with-chariots-and-music. He has passed the test.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Atyasrākṣīḥ = thou hast pushed away. The verse's vocabulary is forceful — the rejection is not passive ignoring but active casting-off. Sṛṅkām vitta-mayīm = the same sṛṅkā now exposed as a snare of wealth."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta's vairāgya is praised by the Lord himself. Vitta-mayī sṛṅkā = the gold-and-jewels that lure the unbhakti-minded; the bhakta sees through it instantly."],["Madhva","Naciketā's rejection is real, not merely verbal. Many would-be seekers reject in words and accept in heart; Naciketā has rejected absolutely."],["Sivānanda","‘Majjanti bahavaḥ manuṣyāḥ’ — sink, the many. The image is of drowning in wealth. The warning is sober and exact."],["Deussen","Deussen reads vitta-mayī sṛṅkā as the ‘gold-chain of wealth’ — a metaphor whose negative image will recur in Western literature long after."],["Aurobindo","The soul that has cast off the dear and the seemingly dear has earned the right to the supreme teaching. The Master now speaks."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.4","title":"","sa":["दूरमेते विपरीते विषूची अविद्या या च विद्येति ज्ञाता । विद्याभीप्सिनं नचिकेतसं मन्ये न त्वा कामा बहवोऽलोलुपन्त"],"iast":["dūramete viparīte viṣūcī avidyā yā ca vidyeti jñātā | vidyābhīpsinaṃ naciketasaṃ manye na tvā kāmā bahavo'lolupanta"],"words":[["dūram ete viparīte viṣūcī","adv. + nom.dual + nom.dual.f. + nom.dual.f.","far apart these two, opposed, divergent"],["avidyā yā ca vidyā iti jñātā","nom.f. + rel.f. + and + nom.f. + quot. + p.p.p.f.","what is known as avidyā and what as vidyā"],["vidyā-abhīpsinaṃ naciketasaṃ manye","cpd.acc.sg.m. + acc.proper + 1.sg. of √man","I deem Naciketā desirous of vidyā"],["na tvā kāmāḥ bahavaḥ alolupanta","neg. + 2.sg.acc. + nom.pl. + adj. + impf.3.pl.","thou hast not been led astray by many desires"]],"lit":"Wide apart, these two, opposed, divergent: that which is known as avidyā and that as vidyā. I deem Naciketā a seeker of vidyā; the many desires have not led thee astray.","para":"Yama formally distinguishes vidyā from avidyā, structurally parallel to śreyas-preyas. They are not gradations but contraries. Naciketā belongs to the vidyā-seeking class — confirmed by his refusal to be deflected.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Viṣūcī = pulling in opposite directions. The bhāṣya is firm: vidyā and avidyā are incompatible orientations of the buddhi, not blendable. Pravṛtti and nivṛtti diverge from this root."],["Rangarāmānuja","Vidyā = brahma-jñāna with bhakti at its core; avidyā = karma-kāṇḍa pursued for yoga-kṣema. Both are paths, but their phalas are categorically different."],["Madhva","the two are ontologically distinct. Vidyā produces mokṣa; avidyā produces saṃsāra. There is no middle category, no synthesis."],["Sivānanda","‘Far apart, opposed’ — the language is uncompromising. Half-heartedness is the great enemy of the spiritual life."],["Deussen","Vidyā and avidyā here are not yet the technical Advaita terms (māyā-doctrine). They are the two cognitive postures of seeker and worldling."],["Aurobindo","The Knowledge and the Ignorance diverge in their very direction of seeking — one inward and upward, the other outward and downward. They never meet."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.5","title":"","sa":["अविद्यायामन्तरे वर्तमानाः स्वयं धीराः पण्डितंमन्यमानाः । दन्द्रम्यमाणाः परियन्ति मूढा अन्धेनैव नीयमाना यथान्धाः"],"iast":["avidyāyāmantare vartamānāḥ svayaṃ dhīrāḥ paṇḍitaṃmanyamānāḥ | dandramyamāṇāḥ pariyanti mūḍhā andhenaiva nīyamānā yathāndhāḥ"],"words":[["avidyāyām antare vartamānāḥ","loc.sg.f. + adv. + pres.ptcp.nom.pl.","dwelling in the midst of ignorance"],["svayaṃ dhīrāḥ paṇḍitam-manyamānāḥ","adv. + nom.pl. + cpd.pres.ptcp.","themselves thinking themselves wise, considering (themselves) learned"],["dandramyamāṇāḥ pariyanti mūḍhāḥ","intens.pres.ptcp. + pres.3.pl. + nom.pl.","crookedly running, they go around — the deluded"],["andhena eva nīyamānāḥ yathā andhāḥ","instr.sg.m. + emph. + pass.pres.ptcp. + rel. + nom.pl.","as the blind led by the blind"]],"lit":"Dwelling in the midst of ignorance, thinking themselves wise, considering themselves learned, the deluded crookedly run around — like blind men led by the blind.","para":"The famous image: scholars in avidyā are not merely ignorant, they are deceived about their own ignorance — and they lead others. The line is the locus of the ‘blind leading the blind’ image in world literature.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Dandramyamāṇāḥ = an intensive of √dram (to run), conveying frantic circling. Śaṅkara: such are the karma-kāṇḍin pedants whose works only deepen avidyā."],["Rangarāmānuja","Paṇḍitam-manyamānāḥ — the danger of mere śāstra-pāṭha without bhakti. Without the inner guru, the outer learning becomes itself a fetter."],["Madhva","Madhva uses this verse against the Advaita-pakṣa: those who claim that bondage is mere ignorance and liberation mere knowledge, themselves run in circles. The verse exposes all reductionist approaches."],["Sivānanda","The verse is a permanent warning to every spiritual student. Pride of learning, of lineage, of austerity, of devotion — each can produce a paṇḍitam-manyatā that blocks vidyā."],["Deussen","The phrase ‘blind led by the blind’ recurs in Matthew 15.14, which raises the question of cultural transmission. Deussen treats it as independent parallel."],["Aurobindo","The dance of mutual delusion — minds that conceal their own darkness with the masks of wisdom, leading others into the same maze."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.6","title":"","sa":["न साम्परायः प्रतिभाति बालं प्रमाद्यन्तं वित्तमोहेन मूढम् । अयं लोको नास्ति पर इति मानी पुनः पुनर्वशमापद्यते मे"],"iast":["na sāmparāyaḥ pratibhāti bālaṃ pramādyantaṃ vittamohena mūḍham | ayaṃ loko nāsti para iti mānī punaḥ punarvaśamāpadyate me"],"words":[["na sāmparāyaḥ pratibhāti bālaṃ","neg. + nom.sg.m. + pres.3.sg. + acc.sg.","the hereafter does not become evident to the child(ish one)"],["pramādyantaṃ vitta-mohena mūḍham","pres.ptcp.acc. + cpd.instr. + acc.","heedless, deluded by infatuation with wealth"],["ayaṃ lokaḥ na asti paraḥ iti mānī","dem. + nom. + neg. + 3.sg. + nom.dem. + quot. + nom.sg.m.","holding \"this world (alone) is, no other\""],["punaḥ punaḥ vaśam āpadyate me","adv.×2 + acc. + pres.3.sg.middle + dat.1.sg.","again and again falls into my power"]],"lit":"The hereafter does not shine forth to the child(ish) one — heedless, deluded by wealth, holding ‘this world (alone) is, no other’; again and again he falls into my power.","para":"The fate of the materialist: rejecting the para-loka, he is reborn under Yama's dominion endlessly. The doctrine of saṃsāra is here put in Yama's own mouth — the most authoritative voice.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Bālam = the child-like in discrimination, not literal child. The verse establishes the Vedāntic critique of the nāstika position — the rejection of after-life perpetuates the very condition of saṃsāra it denies."],["Rangarāmānuja","Vitta-moha-mūḍha = blinded by attachment to wealth. The Lord himself, through Yama, diagnoses materialism as the chronic disease of saṃsāra."],["Madhva","Madhva names this position as that of the Cārvākas explicitly. Yama dismisses them: their denial of the hereafter only deepens their bondage to it."],["Sivānanda","‘Punaḥ punaḥ vaśam āpadyate me’ — Yama himself says: again and again, into my power. The materialist worldview is its own most reliable engine of rebirth."],["Deussen","‘Ayaṃ loko nāsti para iti mānī’ — Deussen notes this as our earliest documented characterisation of the Lokāyata position. The doxographical value is considerable."],["Aurobindo","The man of the surface sees only the surface; what is beyond cannot come into his view. His denial is the very seal of his recurrent imprisonment."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.7","title":"","sa":["श्रवणायापि बहुभिर्यो न लभ्यः शृण्वन्तोऽपि बहवो यं न विद्युः । आश्चर्यो वक्ता कुशलोऽस्य लब्धा आश्चर्यो ज्ञाता कुशलानुशिष्टः"],"iast":["śravaṇāyāpi bahubhiryo na labhyaḥ śṛṇvanto'pi bahavo yaṃ na vidyuḥ | āścaryo vaktā kuśalo'sya labdhā āścaryo jñātā kuśalānuśiṣṭaḥ"],"words":[["śravaṇāya api bahubhiḥ yaḥ na labhyaḥ","dat.sg.n. + emph. + instr.pl. + rel. + neg. + gerundive","He who is not obtained even for hearing by many"],["śṛṇvantaḥ api bahavaḥ yaṃ na vidyuḥ","pres.ptcp.pl. + emph. + nom.pl. + acc.rel. + neg. + opt.3.pl.","many, though hearing, do not know Him"],["āścaryaḥ vaktā kuśalaḥ asya labdhā","adj.nom. + nom.sg.m. + adj. + gen.dem. + nom.","wonderful is the expounder; skilled, the receiver of Him"],["āścaryaḥ jñātā kuśala-anuśiṣṭaḥ","adj. + nom. + cpd. p.p.p.","wonderful is the knower, taught by the skilled"]],"lit":"He whom many do not even attain to (the chance of) hearing about; whom many, even hearing, do not know — wonderful is the expounder, skilled is the receiver; wonderful the knower, taught by a skilful (teacher).","para":"The famous lament-and-celebration: the ātman is rarely heard of, rarely understood when heard. Three rarities: a true teacher, a true hearer, a true knower. The lineage of ātma-vidyā is a chain of marvels.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Śaṅkara's bhāṣya here is itself famous: each rarity compounds the next. Even when the teaching is given, only the prepared can receive; even when received, only by grace is it actualised as direct knowledge."],["Rangarāmānuja","Each ‘āścarya’ is a miracle of divine grace. The teacher is the Lord's instrument, the disciple the Lord's gift, the knowledge the Lord's own self-disclosure."],["Madhva","only Viṣṇu's krpā produces all three rarities together — and only in a yogyā who has done sufficient pūrva-puṇya. The verse vindicates the doctrine of īśvara-prasāda."],["Sivānanda","A verse to humble every spiritual aspirant. Each of the three conditions — being heard, being understood, being known — must coincide. Honour the rare chance when it comes."],["Deussen","Quoted in Bhagavad-Gītā 2.29 with slight verbal variation. The Kaṭha is the source. Deussen marks this as one of the most often-cited Upaniṣadic verses in Sanskrit literature."],["Aurobindo","Each step of the soul's awakening is a marvel — the chance of hearing, the power of comprehending, the depth of realising. Grace works at each transition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.8","title":"","sa":["न नरेणावरेण प्रोक्त एष सुविज्ञेयो बहुधा चिन्त्यमानः । अनन्यप्रोक्ते गतिरत्र नास्ति अणीयान् ह्यतर्क्यमणुप्रमाणात्"],"iast":["na nareṇāvareṇa prokta eṣa suvijñeyo bahudhā cintyamānaḥ | ananyaprokte gatiratra nāsti aṇīyān hyatarkyamaṇupramāṇāt"],"words":[["na nareṇa avareṇa proktaḥ eṣaḥ","neg. + instr.adj. + instr.adj. + p.p.p. + nom.dem.","not by a lower man taught, this (One)"],["su-vijñeyaḥ bahudhā cintyamānaḥ","gerundive + adv. + pres.pass.ptcp.","easily known, (though) variously reflected on"],["an-anya-prokte gatiḥ atra na asti","neg.cpd. + p.p.p. loc. + nom.sg.f. + adv. + neg.adv.","when taught by other than (a knower), here there is no going forward"],["aṇīyān hi atarkyaḥ aṇu-pramāṇāt","adj. + emph. + gerundive + abl.adj.","for subtler than the subtle, beyond reasoning (alone)"]],"lit":"Not by an inferior man taught is this (One) easily knowable, even when meditated on much. When taught by other (than a true knower), here there is no progress; for subtler than the subtle (is He), and beyond mere reasoning.","para":"The role of the guru is irreducible: the teaching must come from a knower; otherwise even long reflection yields no progress. The reason: the ātman is too subtle for unaided tarka.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Avareṇa = inferior, here = one not himself a brahma-jñānin. Atarkyaḥ aṇu-pramāṇāt: argumentation, by its very mode of measurement, falls short. Only śabda-pramāṇa from an adhikārī guru works."],["Rangarāmānuja","Ananya-prokte = ‘when taught by one not other’ — the Vaiṣṇava guru-paramparā. Only the ācārya rooted in the lineage can transmit; private speculation is futile."],["Madhva","the guru must himself be in unbroken paramparā from Viṣṇu (through Brahma). Sectarian as it sounds, the principle is universal: only a knower can transmit knowledge."],["Sivānanda","Reason, by itself, cannot reach the ātman. It can prepare the mind, but only śravaṇa from a sat-guru actualises the knowing."],["Deussen","Deussen reads ‘atarkyaḥ aṇu-pramāṇāt’ as one of the earliest statements of the limits-of-reason argument in Indian philosophy."],["Aurobindo","The Truth is subtler than thought's finest measure; it can only be received from one in whom it has become living. The lineage is alive or it is nothing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.9","title":"","sa":["नैषा तर्केण मतिरापनेया प्रोक्तान्येनैव सुज्ञानाय प्रेष्ठ । यां त्वमापः सत्यधृतिर्बतासि त्वादृङ्।ह्नो भूयान्नचिकेतः प्रष्टा"],"iast":["naiṣā tarkeṇa matirāpaneyā proktānyenaiva sujñānāya preṣṭha | yāṃ tvamāpaḥ satyadhṛtirbatāsi tvādṛṅ|hno bhūyānnaciketaḥ praṣṭā"],"words":[["na eṣā tarkeṇa matiḥ āpaneyā","neg. + nom.dem.f. + instr.sg.m. + nom.sg.f. + gerundive","not by reasoning is this insight to be reached"],["proktā anyena eva su-jñānāya preṣṭha","p.p.p.f. + instr. + emph. + dat.sg.n. + voc.sg.m.","taught by another (knower) for clear knowing, O beloved"],["yāṃ tvam āpaḥ satya-dhṛtiḥ bata asi","acc.f. + 2.sg. + aor.2.sg. of √āp + cpd.nom. + emph. + 2.sg.","which thou hast attained — truly thou art of true resolve"],["tvādṛk no bhūyāt naciketaḥ praṣṭā","adj.nom. + dat.1.pl. + opt.3.sg. + voc. + nom.sg.m.","may a questioner like thee, O Naciketā, come to us"]],"lit":"Not by reasoning is this insight to be attained; (only) when taught by another (knower) is it (made) suitable for clear knowing, O beloved. The (insight) thou hast attained — truly thou art of firm-resolve. May a questioner like thee, O Naciketā, (always) come to us.","para":"Yama makes explicit the requirement of guru-upadeśa and praises Naciketā's satya-dhṛti. The teacher rejoices in such a student: ‘may questioners like thee always come.’","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Apaneyā = to be reached. Reasoning is not even an instrument; only śabda from a knower is. The verse seals the doctrine of pramāṇa-vyavasthā: ātman is śruti-eka-gocara."],["Rangarāmānuja","Satya-dhṛti = the firmness rooted in śraddhā-prajñāna. The bhakta is to be praised for such steadfastness; the Lord's own delight is in such a śiṣya."],["Madhva","anyena prokta — by another, i.e., by Viṣṇu's grace through the guru. Satya-dhṛti is the lakṣaṇa of the mumukṣu. Yama's wish is itself a blessing."],["Sivānanda","Note Yama's spontaneous warmth: ‘may a questioner like thee come to us.’ The genuine teacher's deepest happiness is in genuine students."],["Deussen","Tarka-nirāsa is here paradigmatic. Deussen sees Kaṭha's anti-rationalist clause as balanced by its reasoned exposition — śruti supplemented by yukti."],["Aurobindo","The teacher's heart is opened by the student's resolve. The transmission has already begun in this very recognition."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.10","title":"","sa":["जानाम्यहं शेवधिरित्यनित्यं न ह्यध्रुवैः प्राप्यते हि ध्रुवं तत् । ततो मया नाचिकेतश्चितोऽग्निः अनित्यैर्द्रव्यैः प्राप्तवानस्मि नित्यम्"],"iast":["jānāmyahaṃ śevadhirityanityaṃ na hyadhruvaiḥ prāpyate hi dhruvaṃ tat | tato mayā nāciketaścito'gniḥ anityairdravyaiḥ prāptavānasmi nityam"],"words":[["jānāmi ahaṃ śevadhiḥ iti a-nityaṃ","pres.1.sg. + 1.sg. + nom.sg.m. + quot. + adj.acc.","I know — the \"treasure\" (of svarga) is impermanent"],["na hi a-dhruvaiḥ prāpyate hi dhruvaṃ tat","neg. + emph. + instr.pl.adj. + pres.pass.3.sg. + emph. + nom.dem.","for not by impermanent (means) is the permanent attained"],["tataḥ mayā nāciketaḥ citaḥ agniḥ","adv. + instr.1.sg. + nom.cpd. + p.p.p. + nom.","therefore by me the Nāciketa fire (was) heaped (built)"],["a-nityaiḥ dravyaiḥ prāptavān asmi nityam","instr.pl.adj. + instr.pl.n. + p.p.p.act. + 1.sg.","by impermanent means I have attained the permanent"]],"lit":"I know — the ‘treasure’ (of svarga) is impermanent; for the permanent is not attained by the impermanent. Therefore by me was the Nāciketa-fire heaped — and by impermanent substances I have (yet) attained the permanent.","para":"Yama (as a knower) speaks from his own experience: he himself attained his Yama-ship — a long but finite tenure — through the Nāciketa-agni. The verse signals the structural limit of even the highest karma.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The verse is autobiographical: Yama testifies that even the heavenly attainment is impermanent. The phrase ‘an-ityaiḥ prāptavān asmi nityam’ is a stunning concession: he calls his own status nityam relative to mortals but really transient."],["Rangarāmānuja","Yama confesses the gap between his attainment and the true nitya. The bhakta should note: even devatā-padavi is not param-padam."],["Madhva","Madhva uses this verse to establish the hierarchy of phalas: Yama's lokādhipatya is vast in duration but bounded; Viṣṇu-loka alone is truly nitya."],["Sivānanda","A sobering testimony. Even Yama, lord of death, has attained only relative durability. What hope, then, has the unawakened mortal in his short span?"],["Deussen","Deussen parallels this to the Greek tradition of even the gods being subject to Moira/Anankē. The Vedic gods are similarly bounded."],["Aurobindo","Even cosmic functionaries occupy a passing seat. The Real is beyond all functions and all stations."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.11","title":"","sa":["कामस्याप्तिं जगतः प्रतिष्ठां क्रतोरानन्त्यमभयस्य पारम् । स्तोममहदुरुगायं प्रतिष्ठां दृष्ट्वा धृत्या धीरो नचिकेतोऽत्यस्राक्षीः"],"iast":["kāmasyāptiṃ jagataḥ pratiṣṭhāṃ kratorānantyamabhayasya pāram | stomamahadurugāyaṃ pratiṣṭhāṃ dṛṣṭvā dhṛtyā dhīro naciketo'tyasrākṣīḥ"],"words":[["kāmasya āptiṃ jagataḥ pratiṣṭhāṃ","gen.sg.m. + acc.sg.f. + gen.sg.n. + acc.sg.f.","the attainment of desire, the foundation of the world"],["kratoḥ ānantyam abhayasya pāram","gen.sg.m. + acc.sg.n. + gen.sg.n. + acc.sg.n.","the endlessness of (vedic) action, the shore of fearlessness"],["stoma-mahat uru-gāyaṃ pratiṣṭhāṃ dṛṣṭvā","cpd.acc. + cpd.acc. + acc. + abs.","having seen the praise-greatness, the wide-going, the foundation"],["dhṛtyā dhīraḥ naciketaḥ ati-asrākṣīḥ","instr. + nom. + voc. + aor.2.sg.","with steadfastness, the wise Naciketā, hast cast (these) away"]],"lit":"Having seen the fulfilment of desire, the foundation of the world, the endlessness of ritual action, the shore of fearlessness, the great-praise, the wide-going, the foundation — with steadfastness, O wise Naciketā, thou hast cast (all this) away.","para":"Yama enumerates the supreme attainments within the karma-mārga (puṇya-loka, ritual-eternity, fearlessness, fame) and notes that Naciketā has rejected even these. The qualification for brahma-vidyā is now beyond doubt.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Each item is a peak of karma-phala: kāma-prāpti (every desire fulfilled), jagat-pratiṣṭhā (world-foundation = Hiraṇyagarbha-status), kratu-ānantya (endless yajñas), abhaya-pāra (beyond all fear). Naciketā walks past them all."],["Rangarāmānuja","Even the highest possible loka-phalas are renounced. The bhakta seeks only the Lord, not even the highest of His gifts."],["Madhva","the enumeration is exhaustive of all bhoga-prāpti. Madhva uses the verse to underline the categorical distinction between bhoga-mokṣa and mokṣa-proper."],["Sivānanda","The grand catalogue of saṃsāric pinnacles, all set aside. This is the dhīra par excellence."],["Deussen","Stoma-mahat = ‘the great praise’ — Deussen reads as a technical term for Hiraṇyagarbha's status as receiving universal praise. A high theological peak."],["Aurobindo","All the supreme attainments of the lower nature — fame, foundation, endlessness, freedom-from-fear — the soul sees through them and aspires only to the Real beyond."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.12","title":"","sa":["तं दुर्दर्शं गूढमनुप्रविष्टं गुहाहितं गह्वरेष्ठं पुराणम् । अध्यात्मयोगाधिगमेन देवं मत्वा धीरो हर्षशोकौ जहाति"],"iast":["taṃ durdarśaṃ gūḍhamanupraviṣṭaṃ guhāhitaṃ gahvareṣṭhaṃ purāṇam | adhyātmayogādhigamena devaṃ matvā dhīro harṣaśokau jahāti"],"words":[["taṃ dur-darśam gūḍham anu-praviṣṭam","acc.dem. + adj. + adj. + p.p.p.acc.","Him, hard to see, hidden, having entered (into)"],["guhā-hitam gahvare-ṣṭham purāṇam","cpd.acc. + cpd.acc. + acc.adj.","placed in the (heart-)cave, dwelling in the deep, ancient"],["adhyātma-yoga-adhigamena devam","cpd.instr. + acc.sg.m.","through the attainment by adhyātma-yoga, the deva"],["matvā dhīraḥ harṣa-śokau jahāti","abs. + nom. + cpd.acc.dual + pres.3.sg.","having thought (on Him), the wise abandons joy and grief"]],"lit":"(Him), hard to see, hidden, having entered (the heart), placed in the (heart-)cave, dwelling in the deep, ancient — having known (Him) by the attainment of adhyātma-yoga, the wise one abandons (both) joy and grief.","para":"The first direct description of the ātman: hidden in the cave of the heart, ancient, knowable through adhyātma-yoga. The phala: harṣa-śokau jahāti — the dual emotions are transcended together.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Adhyātma-yoga = the yoga of withdrawing the antaḥkaraṇa from viṣayas and resting in the ātman. The bhāṣya makes guhā = buddhi; gahvara = the deep recess of consciousness. Harṣa-śoka are abandoned together because both are buddhi-vṛttis."],["Rangarāmānuja","Guhā-hitam = the antaryāmin in the heart-lotus. Adhyātma-yoga = bhakti-yoga directed inward. The bhakta who realises this dwells in samatva."],["Madhva","guhā = the hṛdaya-padma; gahvara = a-vyākṛta. The Lord dwells within. Adhyātma-yoga = the discipline of inner worship-and-meditation in a fivefold sequence."],["Sivānanda","Harṣa-śokau jahāti — abandons joy and grief. The result of true knowledge is not ascetic gloom but freedom from oscillation. Equanimity is the lakṣaṇa of the knower."],["Deussen","Adhyātma-yoga as technical term appears here for the first time in the Upaniṣadic corpus. Deussen sees it as the proto-form of what later becomes Sāṃkhya-Yoga."],["Aurobindo","The ancient Person hidden in the cave of the heart, found only through the deep inward Yoga of the Self. To know Him is to live above the play of joy and sorrow."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.13","title":"","sa":["एतच्छ्रुत्वा सम्परिगृह्य मर्त्यः प्रवृह्य धर्म्यमणुमेतमाप्य । स मोदते मोदनीयं हि लब्ध्वा विवृतं सद्म नचिकेतसं मन्ये"],"iast":["etacchrutvā samparigṛhya martyaḥ pravṛhya dharmyamaṇumetamāpya | sa modate modanīyaṃ hi labdhvā vivṛtaṃ sadma naciketasaṃ manye"],"words":[["etat śrutvā samparigṛhya martyaḥ","dem. + abs. + abs. + nom.","having heard and fully grasped this, the mortal"],["pravṛhya dharmyam aṇum etam āpya","abs. + acc.adj. + acc.adj. + acc.dem. + abs.","having extracted this dharma-aligned, subtle (truth), having attained (it)"],["saḥ modate modanīyaṃ hi labdhvā","pron. + pres.3.sg. + acc.gerundive + emph. + abs.","he rejoices, having attained the joy-worthy"],["vivṛtaṃ sadma naciketasaṃ manye","acc.adj. + acc.sg.n. + acc.proper + 1.sg.","I deem (your) door-of-the-abode open, O Naciketā"]],"lit":"Hearing and fully grasping this, having drawn forth this subtle dharma-aligned (truth), having attained it, the mortal rejoices — for he has obtained the joy-worthy. I deem the door (of the abode of Brahman) open for thee, O Naciketā.","para":"Yama signals Naciketā's readiness: the door (sadma) is open. The verse closes the preparatory section; the next verses begin the doctrinal core.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Vivṛtaṃ sadma = the open abode, i.e., brahma-loka access. Śaṅkara: Yama recognises Naciketā's full qualification; the teaching can now proceed without restriction."],["Rangarāmānuja","Sadma = the Lord's own abode. The bhakta has the door wide open before him. Bhakti-vidyā now unfolds."],["Madhva","vivṛtaṃ sadma is param-pada-prāpti-yogyatā — the qualification for attaining the supreme abode is now complete in Naciketā."],["Sivānanda","The teacher's recognition: ‘the door is open for thee.’ Every genuine disciple eventually hears this from his teacher, spoken or unspoken."],["Deussen","Vivṛtaṃ sadma evokes the Vedic image of the heavenly doors thrown open. Deussen parallels with Atharvavedic and Brāhmaṇa loka-dvāra imagery."],["Aurobindo","The Master sees the soul's readiness and announces it. The threshold has been crossed."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.14","title":"","sa":["अन्यत्र धर्मादन्यत्राधर्मा दन्यत्रास्मात्कृताकृतात् । अन्यत्र भूताच्च भव्याच्च यत्तत्पश्यसि तद्वद"],"iast":["anyatra dharmādanyatrādharmā danyatrāsmātkṛtākṛtāt | anyatra bhūtācca bhavyācca yattatpaśyasi tadvada"],"words":[["anyatra dharmāt anyatra adharmāt","adv. + abl.sg.m. + adv. + abl.sg.m.","other than dharma, other than adharma"],["anyatra asmāt kṛta-akṛtāt","adv. + abl.sg.n. + abl.cpd.","other than this done-and-undone"],["anyatra bhūtāt ca bhavyāt ca","adv. + abl.adj. + and + abl.adj. + and","other than past and future"],["yat tat paśyasi tat vada","rel.acc.dem. + 2.sg. + dem. + imv.2.sg.","what thou seest as that — tell (us) that"]],"lit":"Other than dharma, other than adharma, other than this done-and-undone, other than past and future — what thou seest as That, tell (us) that.","para":"Naciketā asks the supreme question with surgical precision: what is beyond all dualities? Beyond moral categories (dharma/adharma), beyond karma (kṛta/akṛta), beyond time (bhūta/bhavya). Famously cited in Gītā 8.11.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Anyatra = otherwise, beyond. Śaṅkara: the question reaches for the para-brahman, which transcends all paired oppositions. The five anyatra-s form a complete negation: dharma, karma, kāla all set aside."],["Rangarāmānuja","‘Anyatra’ here means beyond, not other-than. The supreme Lord transcends dharma-adharma (He is their giver), kṛta-akṛta (He is their source), bhūta-bhavya (He is their time-master)."],["Madhva","Viṣṇu transcends the three pairs as the their adhiṣṭhāna. The question is the highest ontological probe possible in language."],["Sivānanda","A verse worth meditating on daily. The supreme is what remains when all categories are set aside — and yet it is not nothing."],["Deussen","This verse is quoted verbatim in Bhagavad-Gītā 8.11. Deussen treats the Kaṭha as the source. The five-fold negation prefigures the neti-neti method."],["Aurobindo","The soul asks for what is beyond every measure of the world — the supreme Beyond, which is also the supreme Within."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.15","title":"","sa":["सर्वे वेदा यत्पदमामनन्ति तपांसि सर्वाणि च यद्वदन्ति । यदिच्छन्तो ब्रह्मचर्यं चरन्ति तत्ते पदं सङ्ग्रहेण ब्रवीम्योमित्येतत्"],"iast":["sarve vedā yatpadamāmananti tapāṃsi sarvāṇi ca yadvadanti | yadicchanto brahmacaryaṃ caranti tatte padaṃ saṅgraheṇa bravīmyomityetat"],"words":[["sarve vedāḥ yat padam āmananti","nom.pl. + nom.pl. + rel.acc.sg.n. + pres.3.pl.","what (supreme) state all the Vedas declare"],["tapāṃsi sarvāṇi ca yat vadanti","acc.pl.n. + adj. + and + rel. + pres.3.pl.","what all austerities proclaim"],["yat icchantaḥ brahma-caryam caranti","rel.acc. + pres.ptcp.pl. + acc.sg.n. + pres.3.pl.","desiring which (men) live brahmacarya"],["tat te padaṃ saṃgraheṇa bravīmi: OM iti etat","dem. + dat.2.sg. + acc.sg.n. + instr.sg.m. + pres.1.sg. + quot.","that state I declare to thee in brief: it is OM"]],"lit":"What state all the Vedas declare, what all austerities proclaim, desiring which (men) live brahmacarya — that state, in brief, I declare to thee: it is OM.","para":"The convergence verse: the goal of all scriptures, all austerities, all brahmacarya is one — and is summarised in one syllable, OM. The Praṇava is the doorway to the supreme.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Saṃgraheṇa = in brief, in concentrate. OM is the abbreviation and the totality. Śaṅkara links this to Māṇḍūkya's OM = ātman doctrine."],["Rangarāmānuja","Padam = the abode (= Vaikuṇṭha). All scripture converges on the Praṇava as the name-form-key to bhagavat-prāpti."],["Madhva","Madhva enumerates the Vedic loci that culminate in OM: ṚV X.90, the Brahma-mantra-vidyās in YV-saṃhitā, the Sāma-OM. The verse synthesises Vedic teleology."],["Sivānanda","OM is the symbol, the seed, the destination. Chanting, contemplating, becoming OM — this is the practical entry."],["Deussen","‘Etad vai tat akṣaram’ — the verse links Kaṭha to Praśna V (where OM-meditation is given fullest exposition). Cross-textual convergence."],["Aurobindo","OM is the seal of all the Veda — the syllable that holds the supreme as a mantra holds its power. Through OM the soul touches the Beyond."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.16","title":"","sa":["एतद्।ह्ध्येवाक्षरं ब्रह्म एतद्।ह्ध्येवाक्षरं परम् । एतद्।ह्ध्येवाक्षरं ज्ञात्वा यो यदिच्छति तस्य तत्"],"iast":["etad|hdhyevākṣaraṃ brahma etad|hdhyevākṣaraṃ param | etad|hdhyevākṣaraṃ jñātvā yo yadicchati tasya tat"],"words":[["etat hi eva akṣaraṃ brahma","dem. + emph.×2 + acc.sg.n. + acc.sg.n.","this very syllable is Brahman"],["etat hi eva akṣaraṃ param","dem. + emph.×2 + acc.sg.n. + adj.","this very syllable is supreme"],["etat hi eva akṣaraṃ jñātvā","dem. + emph.×2 + acc.sg.n. + abs.","having known this very syllable"],["yaḥ yat icchati tasya tat","rel. + rel.acc. + 3.sg. + gen.dem. + dem.acc.","whatever (one) wishes, his that (becomes)"]],"lit":"This very Imperishable is Brahman; this very Imperishable is supreme; having known this very Imperishable, whatever (one) wishes, his that (is).","para":"OM = the akṣara = Brahman = the supreme = the fulfiller of every wish. The triple identification is decisive.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Akṣaram has the double sense: the syllable OM and the imperishable Brahman. Śaṅkara: by upāsanā-jñāna of either form (svarūpa or sabda-svarūpa), the result is mokṣa."],["Rangarāmānuja","Akṣaraṃ param = the supreme imperishable = Nārāyaṇa as the Praṇava-vācya. The bhakta obtains every desire ultimately fulfilled in mokṣa."],["Madhva","‘yo yad icchati tasya tat’ does not mean kāmya-prāpti but the universal satisfaction of the ātma-jñānin. Desires are not gratified but transcended."],["Sivānanda","Knowing the akṣara, every desire is fulfilled — because the seeker no longer desires anything other than this Knowing."],["Deussen","Triple ‘etad’ formula intensifies. The verse is metrically loose but rhetorically powerful — the kind of incantatory teaching that surrounds the Praṇava doctrine."],["Aurobindo","The supreme Word is the supreme Reality; whoever knows the Word knows that of which the Word is the form, and to such a one all things are accomplished."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.17","title":"","sa":["एतदालम्बनं श्रेष्ठमेतदालम्बनं परम् । एतदालम्बनं ज्ञात्वा ब्रह्मलोके महीयते"],"iast":["etadālambanaṃ śreṣṭhametadālambanaṃ param | etadālambanaṃ jñātvā brahmaloke mahīyate"],"words":[["etat ālambanaṃ śreṣṭham","dem. + acc.sg.n. + adj.acc.","this is the best support"],["etat ālambanam param","dem. + acc. + adj.","this is the supreme support"],["etat ālambanaṃ jñātvā","dem. + acc. + abs.","having known this support"],["brahma-loke mahīyate","loc.cpd. + pres.3.sg.pass.","one is exalted in brahma-loka"]],"lit":"This is the best support; this is the supreme support. Knowing this support, one is exalted in the brahma-loka.","para":"OM is repeated as the ālambana — the cognitive support of meditation. The phala is brahma-loka-mahimā — exaltation in the highest world.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Ālambanam = the prop of the citta in upāsanā. The verse refers to saguṇa-brahma-upāsanā via OM, whose phala is krama-mukti through brahma-loka."],["Rangarāmānuja","Brahma-loke = Vaikuṇṭha. The bhakta who meditates on OM as the Lord's vācaka attains the param padam — krama-mukti from the Lord's own loka."],["Madhva","krama-mukti is a real path. Brahma-loka is a real destination, not metaphor. The OM-meditator reaches it and from there attains Viṣṇu-loka."],["Sivānanda","OM as the supreme ālambana of dhyāna. There is no support firmer; no meditation-object higher. Make OM the centre of your sādhanā."],["Deussen","Two consecutive verses on OM-meditation suggest a redactional insert from a praṇava-vidyā tradition. The doctrine is consistent with Māṇḍūkya."],["Aurobindo","The supreme Support, the Praṇava — leaning upon which the soul is upheld through every stage of its ascent to the world of Brahman."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.18","title":"","sa":["न जायते म्रियते वा विपश्चि न्नायं कुतश्चिन्न बभूव कश्चित् । अजो नित्यः शाश्वतोऽयं पुराणो न हन्यते हन्यमाने शरीरे"],"iast":["na jāyate mriyate vā vipaści nnāyaṃ kutaścinna babhūva kaścit | ajo nityaḥ śāśvato'yaṃ purāṇo na hanyate hanyamāne śarīre"],"words":[["na jāyate mriyate vā vipaścit","neg. + pres.3.sg. + pres.3.sg. + or + nom.adj.","He neither is born nor dies — the wise (-One)"],["na ayam kutaścit na babhūva kaścit","neg. + dem. + adv. + neg. + perf.3.sg. + nom.indef.","He came not from anywhere, nor became anyone (else)"],["ajaḥ nityaḥ śāśvataḥ ayaṃ purāṇaḥ","adj.×4 + dem.","unborn, eternal, perennial, ancient is He"],["na hanyate hanyamāne śarīre","neg. + pres.3.sg.pass. + loc.pres.pass.ptcp. + loc.sg.n.","He is not slain when the body is slain"]],"lit":"He is not born, nor dies; nor did He come from anywhere; nor did He become anyone. Unborn, eternal, perennial, ancient — He is not slain when the body is slain.","para":"The ātman's nature: unborn, undying, unchanging. The locus classicus quoted in Gītā 2.20 almost verbatim. The killing of the body does not touch the ātman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> each negation is exhaustive — na jāyate (rules out origin), na mriyate (rules out end), na babhūva kaścit (rules out alteration). The ātman is the contradiction of every change-attribute."],["Rangarāmānuja","The verse applies primarily to the jīvātman (the aṇuṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ) and a fortiori to Paramātman. Both are nitya, both ajāḥ — the doctrine of nityo nityānāṃ."],["Madhva","the verse applies to both jīva and Paramātman in their nitya-svarūpa, but the categorical distinction is preserved — jīva is aṇu-nitya, Paramātman vibhu-nitya."],["Sivānanda","Cited in Gītā 2.20. The verse to be repeated at the death-bed: ‘na hanyate hanyamāne śarīre’ — the Self is not slain when the body is slain."],["Deussen","Locus classicus of Indian doctrine of the immortal Self. Deussen translates each clause with care: the negations are not merely poetic but precisely categorical."],["Aurobindo","The eternal in us is untouched by the comings and goings of the body. ‘Slain’ is a word that has no application to the deathless Person."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.19","title":"","sa":["हन्ता चेन्मन्यते हन्तुं हतश्चेन्मन्यते हतम् । उभौ तौ न विजानीतो नायं हन्ति न हन्यते"],"iast":["hantā cenmanyate hantuṃ hataścenmanyate hatam | ubhau tau na vijānīto nāyaṃ hanti na hanyate"],"words":[["hantā cet manyate hantum","nom.sg.m. + cond. + 3.sg. + inf.","if the slayer thinks (he) slays"],["hataḥ cet manyate hatam","nom.p.p.p. + cond. + 3.sg. + acc.p.p.p.","if the slain thinks himself slain"],["ubhau tau na vijānītaḥ","nom.dual + dem.dual + neg. + pres.3.dual","both these two do not know"],["na ayaṃ hanti na hanyate","neg. + dem. + pres.3.sg. + neg. + pres.3.sg.pass.","He neither slays nor is slain"]],"lit":"If the slayer thinks he slays, if the slain thinks he is slain — both these two do not know: He neither slays nor is slain.","para":"The categorical dismissal of both subject and object of killing: the ātman is untouched by the action. Quoted again in Gītā 2.19.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Na ayaṃ hanti = the ātman is non-doer (akartṛ); na hanyate = the ātman is non-experiencer of being-killed (a-bhoktṛ in this dimension). The dual negation establishes ātman's transcendence of agency-and-patiency in the dehātmaka-sphere."],["Rangarāmānuja","The ātman is here distinguished from the body that does the killing-or-being-killed. But Rangarāmānuja: agency is real at the embodied level; only at the para-mārthika does it dissolve."],["Madhva","the verse is about the inner ātman's invulnerability, not about absolute non-agency. Karma operates on the upādhi, not the svarūpa."],["Sivānanda","The verse should not be misused to justify violence. Kṛṣṇa to Arjuna: the killing of the body is not the killing of the soul. But this is metaphysical truth, not licence."],["Deussen","Cited in Gītā 2.19 almost verbatim. Deussen notes the explicit Kaṭha-Gītā transmission."],["Aurobindo","The eternal Person stands above the play of death-bringers and death-receivers; neither acts upon Him, neither passes through Him."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.20","title":"","sa":["अणोरणीयान्महतो महीया नात्माऽस्य जन्तोर्निहितो गुहायाम् । तमक्रतुः पश्यति वीतशोको धातुप्रसादान्महिमानमात्मनः"],"iast":["aṇoraṇīyānmahato mahīyā nātmā'sya jantornihito guhāyām | tamakratuḥ paśyati vītaśoko dhātuprasādānmahimānamātmanaḥ"],"words":[["aṇoḥ aṇīyān mahataḥ mahīyān","abl.adj. + adj.compar. + abl.adj. + adj.compar.","smaller than the small, greater than the great"],["ātmā asya jantoḥ ni-hitaḥ guhāyām","nom. + gen.dem. + gen.sg.m. + p.p.p. + loc.","the ātman is set within the cave (of the heart) of this creature"],["tam akratuḥ paśyati vīta-śokaḥ","acc. + adj.nom. + pres.3.sg. + cpd.nom.","the desireless (one) sees Him, freed from sorrow"],["dhātu-prasādāt mahimānam ātmanaḥ","cpd.abl. + acc.sg.m. + gen.","through the grace of the Creator/Dhātṛ, (sees) the majesty of the ātman"]],"lit":"Smaller than the small, greater than the great — the ātman is set within the (heart-)cave of this creature. The desireless one, freed from sorrow, sees Him — through the grace of the Creator, (sees) the majesty of the ātman.","para":"The famous paradox: smaller than smallest, greater than greatest. The ātman is in the heart-cave (guhāyām) of every being. The seeing requires akratu (desirelessness) and dhātu-prasāda (divine grace).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> aṇoḥ aṇīyān refers to the ātman as subtler than the most subtle puruṣa-tattva; mahato mahīyān as vaster than the largest mahat. Dhātu-prasāda = sense-and-mind-rendered-still (Śaṅkara reads dhātu as the senses)."],["Rangarāmānuja","Dhātu-prasāda = the prasāda of the Dhātṛ (Creator = Nārāyaṇa). The vision of the ātman depends ultimately on Lord's grace, even when sādhanā is in place."],["Madhva","Dhātu-prasāda is unambiguously the prasāda of Viṣṇu. Madhva makes this the doctrinal cornerstone: jñāna-darśana requires īśvara-anugraha; sādhanā alone is insufficient."],["Sivānanda","Akratu = desireless. Vīta-śoka = sorrow-free. These are the qualifications. The grace of the Creator completes what sādhanā prepares."],["Deussen","Deussen splits the commentaries on ‘dhātu-prasādāt’ — Śaṅkara reads ‘senses-and-mind stilled’; Vaiṣṇavas read ‘grace of the Dhātṛ’. Both readings are defensible from grammar."],["Aurobindo","The Self is smaller than the atom of consciousness and vaster than the largest space; He is found by the still mind to whom the grace of the supreme is given."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.21","title":"","sa":["आसीनो दूरं व्रजति शयानो याति सर्वतः । कस्तं मदामदं देवं मदन्यो ज्ञातुमर्हति"],"iast":["āsīno dūraṃ vrajati śayāno yāti sarvataḥ | kastaṃ madāmadaṃ devaṃ madanyo jñātumarhati"],"words":[["āsīnaḥ dūraṃ vrajati","pres.ptcp.nom. + adv. + pres.3.sg.","sitting still, He moves far"],["śayānaḥ yāti sarvataḥ","pres.ptcp.nom. + pres.3.sg. + adv.","lying down, He goes everywhere"],["kaḥ taṃ mada-amadaṃ devaṃ","interr. + acc. + cpd.acc.sg.m. + acc.","who that god — joyful-and-yet-joyless"],["mat-anyaḥ jñātum arhati","cpd.nom. + inf. + pres.3.sg.","other than me, is fit to know?"]],"lit":"Sitting still, He moves far; lying down, He goes everywhere. Who, other than me, is fit to know that god — joyful-yet-joyless?","para":"The paradoxes intensify: sitting, He travels; lying, He pervades. He is mada-amada — pleasure-and-displeasure (or active-and-quiescent). Only the qualified knows.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Mada-amadaṃ = at once joyous and joyless, i.e., beyond such categories. Sitting-yet-moving expresses the ātman's all-pervasiveness despite localised manifestation. ‘Mat-anyaḥ’ = other than I (Yama, here speaking from the standpoint of brahma-jñāna)."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord is ubiquitous-yet-located. ‘Mada-amadam’ = the controller of joy and its absence. ‘Mat-anyaḥ’ = other than the Lord himself or his bhakta-knower."],["Madhva","Madhva reads ‘mada-amadam’ as the seat of pure ānanda. The verse extols Viṣṇu's paradoxical attributes — sthira-yet-sarvagata."],["Sivānanda","The supreme paradoxes are not contradictions but signposts to that which is beyond spatial-temporal logic. Read them as Zen-like upāyas of the buddhi."],["Deussen","Deussen treats these paradoxes as Eleatic — comparable to Zeno's antinomies — and notes their common philosophical function: pushing thought past the ordinary categories."],["Aurobindo","Still and yet swift, asleep and yet pervading — the contradictions of the Real that the small mind cannot reconcile, but the awakened consciousness directly sees."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.22","title":"","sa":["अशरीरं शरीरेष्वनवस्थेष्ववस्थितम् । महान्तं विभुमात्मानं मत्वा धीरो न शोचति"],"iast":["aśarīraṃ śarīreṣvanavastheṣvavasthitam | mahāntaṃ vibhumātmānaṃ matvā dhīro na śocati"],"words":[["a-śarīraṃ śarīreṣu","adj.acc. + loc.pl.","bodiless among bodies"],["an-avastheṣu avasthitam","adj.loc.pl. + p.p.p.acc.","among the non-abiding, abiding"],["mahāntaṃ vibhum ātmānaṃ","acc.adj. + acc.adj. + acc.","the great, all-pervading ātman"],["matvā dhīraḥ na śocati","abs. + nom. + neg. + pres.3.sg.","having thought (on Him), the wise does not grieve"]],"lit":"Bodiless among bodies, abiding among the non-abiding, vast, all-pervading — having thought (on this) ātman, the wise (one) does not grieve.","para":"Three crystalline contrasts: bodiless / bodied; abiding / non-abiding; vast / contained. The fruit is the cessation of grief — already a recurring leitmotif (na śocati).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Aśarīram = without a kārmika body. Avasthitam = stably-present, unchanged amid changing upādhis. Śaṅkara: na śocati is the lakṣaṇa of jñāna — grief presupposes false identification."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord-and-jīva, each in its own degree, is body-less-among-bodies. The verse applies primarily to Paramātman as antaryāmin."],["Madhva","vibhu-ātmā = Viṣṇu, the all-pervading; the jīva is aṇu but stably-present in its own measure. The verse honours both, in their proper categories."],["Sivānanda","‘Matvā dhīro na śocati’ — having thought, the wise does not grieve. Make this your daily mantra in moments of loss."],["Deussen","‘Aśarīraṃ śarīreṣu’ is one of the most quoted lines in Vedānta literature, cited by Śaṅkara in his Brahma-Sūtra-Bhāṣya at multiple points."],["Aurobindo","He is bodiless in the bodies, abiding in the unabiding — the eternal Person whose knowing dissolves the soul's sorrow once for all."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.23","title":"","sa":["नायमात्मा प्रवचनेन लभ्यो न मेधया न बहुना श्रुतेन । यमेवैष वृणुते तेन लभ्यः तस्यैष आत्मा विवृणुते तनूं स्वाम्"],"iast":["nāyamātmā pravacanena labhyo na medhayā na bahunā śrutena | yamevaiṣa vṛṇute tena labhyaḥ tasyaiṣa ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām"],"words":[["na ayam ātmā pravacanena labhyaḥ","neg. + dem. + nom.sg.m. + instr.sg.n. + gerundive","this ātman is not gained by mere exposition"],["na medhayā na bahunā śrutena","neg. + instr. + neg. + instr.adj. + instr.p.p.p.","not by intellect, not by much hearing"],["yam eva eṣaḥ vṛṇute tena labhyaḥ","rel.acc. + emph. + dem.nom. + pres.3.sg.middle + instr. + gerundive","whom this (ātman) chooses, by him He is gained"],["tasya eṣaḥ ātmā vivṛṇute tanūṃ svām","gen.dem. + dem.nom. + nom. + pres.3.sg. + acc.f. + adj.acc.","to him this ātman reveals His own form"]],"lit":"This ātman is not gained by exposition, nor by intellect, nor by much hearing. Whom this (ātman) chooses, by him He is gained; to him this ātman reveals His own form.","para":"The most controversial verse of Kaṭha. ātma-jñāna is not the fruit of effort alone but of the ātman's self-revelation to the chosen. Read variously as grace-doctrine (Vaiṣṇava), or as ‘whom the ātman chooses’ = the one who chooses ātman (Śaṅkara's clever reading).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> yam eva eṣa vṛṇute = whom the ātman (= the ātman-seeking, the aspirant himself) chooses. The self-as-seeker chooses itself; the self-as-known reveals itself. The grace-reading is rejected by Śaṅkara in favour of an introspective tautology."],["Rangarāmānuja","The verse is foundational for Vaiśiṣṭādvaita doctrine of grace. ‘Yam eva eṣa vṛṇute’ — whomever the Lord-ātman chooses, that one gains. Sādhanā prepares the receptacle; prasāda fills it."],["Madhva","the verse is the locus classicus of īśvara-prasāda doctrine. The Lord chooses; the choice is rooted in His sovereign will, not in the jīva's qualifications alone."],["Sivānanda","Sādhanā is necessary but not sufficient. The final revelation is a grace. Both pakṣas — effort and grace — are real; only the synthesis is liberation."],["Deussen","The verse troubles every Vedānta school. Deussen's translation leaves the grace-tone intact: ‘wen er sich erwählt, von dem wird er ergriffen’. The grammar permits both readings."],["Aurobindo","The Self is found only when It chooses to be found — and It chooses when the soul has become ready. Effort and grace are the two faces of one movement."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.24","title":"","sa":["नाविरतो दुश्चरितान्नाशान्तो नासमाहितः । नाशान्तमानसो वाऽपि प्रज्ञानेनैनमाप्नुयात्"],"iast":["nāvirato duścaritānnāśānto nāsamāhitaḥ | nāśāntamānaso vā'pi prajñānenainamāpnuyāt"],"words":[["na a-virataḥ duḥ-caritāt","neg. + nom.adj. + abl.cpd.","not (one) un-restrained from evil conduct"],["na a-śāntaḥ na a-samāhitaḥ","neg. + adj.×2","not the un-peaceful, not the unconcentrated"],["na a-śānta-mānasaḥ vā api","neg. + cpd.adj. + or + emph.","nor even the one whose mind is un-quiet"],["prajñānena enam āpnuyāt","instr.sg.n. + acc.dem. + opt.3.sg.","can attain Him by knowledge"]],"lit":"Not one un-restrained from evil conduct, not the un-peaceful, not the unconcentrated, nor even one whose mind is restless — can attain Him by knowledge.","para":"The moral preconditions: ethical restraint (uparati from duḥ-carita), inner peace (śānti), concentration (samādhi), mental quiet — these are the prerequisites for prajñāna-darśana.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The four-fold qualification: vairāgya from evil; śānti; samādhi; manas-praśānti. Without all four, prajñāna does not bear fruit. The verse fixes the adhikāri-lakṣaṇa."],["Rangarāmānuja","The four conditions are sādhana-cātuṣṭaya's prefigurement. Without them, even the Lord's grace cannot operate — the receptacle is not ready."],["Madhva","The ethical qualification is irreducible. Madhva insists the verse is not metaphorical: actual cessation from evil acts, actual peace, actual samādhi are required."],["Sivānanda","The verse is the bedrock of all sādhanā. No amount of study or japa can substitute for ethical purification. Start here."],["Deussen","Deussen sees this verse as fusing Sāṃkhya-Yoga and Vedānta requirements: outer restraint (yama-niyama-equivalents), inner stillness (samādhi-equivalent), then jñāna."],["Aurobindo","Knowledge is for the still and pure consciousness; the troubled mind cannot mirror the Real. Self-conquest is the first foundation of all Yoga."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.ii.25","title":"","sa":["यस्य ब्रह्म च क्षत्रं च उभे भवत ओदनः । मृत्युर्यस्योपसेचनं क इत्था वेद यत्र सः"],"iast":["yasya brahma ca kṣatraṃ ca ubhe bhavata odanaḥ | mṛtyuryasyopasecanaṃ ka itthā veda yatra saḥ"],"words":[["yasya brahma ca kṣatraṃ ca ubhe bhavataḥ odanaḥ","gen.rel. + nom.×2 + nom.dual + pres.3.dual + nom.","of whom brāhmaṇa and kṣatra both become (his) food"],["mṛtyuḥ yasya upasecanam","nom. + gen. + nom.sg.n.","of whom Death is the seasoning"],["kaḥ itthā veda yatra saḥ","interr. + adv. + pres.3.sg. + rel.adv. + pron.","who thus knows where He is?"]],"lit":"Of whom brāhmaṇa and kṣatra both become (His) food, of whom Death is the seasoning — who thus knows where He (is)?","para":"The vallī closes with a striking image: the supreme is so vast that both the priestly and warrior orders (the two highest social principles) are His food, and Death itself is merely the condiment. Who can locate such a One?","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Brahma ca kṣatraṃ ca = the two highest powers of the cosmos (Veda-power and ruling-power). Both are odana (boiled rice) for the supreme; mṛtyu is upasecana (gravy). The supreme transcends all power-categories."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord consumes all powers in His samhāra-līlā; even Mṛtyu serves as His instrument. ‘Kaḥ itthā veda’ — only the bhakta-jñānin knows."],["Madhva","Viṣṇu at pralaya draws all into Himself; brahma, kṣatra, and mṛtyu are all subordinate. The image is of cosmic consumption."],["Sivānanda","A daunting image: even the highest social and cosmic principles are merely the food of the supreme. How then can the small mind locate Him?"],["Deussen","Deussen reads this as a Brāhmaṇa-style cosmic-meal metaphor (parallel to ŚB X.6.5.1 — Aitareya II), here re-employed for theological exaltation of the supreme."],["Aurobindo","The supreme Lord devours all powers as His food, has Death itself as His seasoning. Where He is, who can say? — only one in whom He has revealed Himself."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya I · Vallī iii","u":[{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.1","title":"","sa":["ऋतं पिबन्तौ सुकृतस्य लोके गुहां प्रविष्टौ परमे परार्धे । छायातपौ ब्रह्मविदो वदन्ति पञ्चाग्नयो ये च त्रिणाचिकेताः"],"iast":["ṛtaṃ pibantau sukṛtasya loke guhāṃ praviṣṭau parame parārdhe | chāyātapau brahmavido vadanti pañcāgnayo ye ca triṇāciketāḥ"],"words":[["ṛtaṃ pibantau sukṛtasya loke","acc.sg.n. + pres.act.ptcp.nom.dual + gen.sg. + loc.sg.","two who drink ṛta in the world of good deeds"],["guhāṃ praviṣṭau parame parārdhe","acc.sg.f. + p.p.p.dual + loc.adj. + loc.sg.","having entered the cave, in the supreme highest place"],["chāyā-ātapau brahma-vidaḥ vadanti","cpd.nom.dual + gen.cpd. + pres.3.pl.","as shade-and-sunshine the brahma-knowers call them"],["pañca-agnayaḥ ye ca tri-nāciketāḥ","cpd.nom.pl. + rel. + and + cpd.nom.pl.","the five-fire-knowers and the three-Nāciketa-knowers"]],"lit":"(Two there are) drinking ṛta in the world of good deeds, having entered the cave, in the supreme highest place. The knowers of Brahman call them shade and sunshine — both the pañcāgni-knowers and the tri-Nāciketa-knowers.","para":"Two enter the heart-cave: the jīva and the Paramātman, drinking the fruit of karma. They appear as shadow and light — the contracted soul and the all-luminous Lord. Both the pañcāgni-vidyā (Chāndogya V.10) and the Nāciketa-agni traditions affirm this duality-in-unity.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Two = jīva (chāyā, shadow) and Paramātman (ātapa, sunshine), both ‘drinking’ the fruits of karma — though only the jīva actually consumes; the Paramātman is the witness. Śaṅkara: the apparent duality is from the upādhi-bheda, not svarūpa."],["Rangarāmānuja","Cornerstone verse of Viśiṣṭādvaita: two entities, the jīva (chāyā = aṇu, dependent) and the Paramātman (ātapa = vibhu, luminous source), inseparably together. Antaryāmin-doctrine rests here."],["Madhva","Madhva's foundational verse for tāratamya: the two are categorically distinct — chāyā and ātapa cannot become each other. The pañca-bheda is established right here."],["Sivānanda","Both jīva and Paramātman dwell in the heart. The seeker discovers them as guhā-anu-praviṣṭau. The relationship is intimate yet asymmetric."],["Deussen","The verse is cited in Brahma-Sūtra I.2.11 (guhāṃ praviṣṭau). The two-self interpretation is the dominant traditional reading."],["Aurobindo","Two there are in every consciousness: the individual self drinking the cup of karma, and the supreme Witness for whom the cup is offered. The cave is one but holds both."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.2","title":"","sa":["यः सेतुरीजानानामक्षरं ब्रह्म यत् परम् । अभयं तितीर्षतां पारं नाचिकेतं शकेमहि"],"iast":["yaḥ seturījānānāmakṣaraṃ brahma yat param | abhayaṃ titīrṣatāṃ pāraṃ nāciketaṃ śakemahi"],"words":[["yaḥ setuḥ ījānānām","rel. + nom.sg.m. + gen.pres.ptcp.pl.","He who is the bridge for the sacrificers"],["akṣaraṃ brahma yat param","acc.sg.n. + acc.sg.n. + rel. + adj.","the imperishable Brahman, what is supreme"],["abhayaṃ titīrṣatāṃ pāraṃ","acc.sg.n. + gen.des.ptcp.pl. + acc.sg.n.","fearless, the shore for those wishing to cross over"],["nāciketaṃ śakemahi","acc. + opt.1.pl. of √śak","may we master the Nāciketa (-fire/-vidyā)"]],"lit":"(He who is) the bridge of the sacrificers, the imperishable, the supreme Brahman, the fearless shore for those wishing to cross — may we be able to (kindle/realise) the Nāciketa (-fire).","para":"A prayer-verse for ability to master the Nāciketa-discipline. The Nāciketa-agni is here made the setu (bridge) to the akṣara-brahma. Both ritual and supreme are linked.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Setuḥ = the bridge linking karma to jñāna-mokṣa. The Nāciketa-agni is praised as this very bridge. The verse acknowledges the karma-mārga's role in mokṣa-mārga's foundation."],["Rangarāmānuja","Setuḥ = the Lord himself as bridge. Abhayaṃ pāram = Vaikuṇṭha. The Nāciketa-vidyā is a sahakāri-kāraṇa for prapatti's success."],["Madhva","setu-vidyā is found explicitly in Chāndogya VIII.4 and Brahma-Sūtra I.3.1. The Lord as bridge crossing from saṃsāra to mokṣa is established Vedānta."],["Sivānanda","‘May we master the Nāciketa’ — a prayer for the practitioner. The discipline itself is a vidyā that builds the inner bridge."],["Deussen","Setu = ‘dam, dyke, bridge’ — a recurring Upaniṣadic image (Chāndogya VIII.4.1, Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka IV.4.22). The supreme as crossing-point of the saṃsāra-river."],["Aurobindo","The supreme is the bridge between the mortal and immortal shores; the Yoga is the means of climbing that bridge."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.3","title":"","sa":["आत्मानं रथिनं विद्धि शरीरं रथमेव तु । बुद्धिं तु सारथिं विद्धि मनः प्रग्रहमेव च"],"iast":["ātmānaṃ rathinaṃ viddhi śarīraṃ rathameva tu | buddhiṃ tu sārathiṃ viddhi manaḥ pragrahameva ca"],"words":[["ātmānaṃ rathinaṃ viddhi","acc. + acc.adj. + imv.2.sg.","know the ātman (as) the rider of the chariot"],["śarīraṃ ratham eva tu","acc. + acc. + emph.","the body (as) the chariot itself"],["buddhiṃ tu sārathiṃ viddhi","acc. + emph. + acc. + imv.","know the buddhi (as) the charioteer"],["manaḥ pragraham eva ca","acc. + acc. + emph. + and","and the manas (as) the reins"]],"lit":"Know the ātman as the rider of the chariot, the body as the chariot itself; know the buddhi as the charioteer, and the manas as the reins.","para":"The most famous metaphor in all Upaniṣads, the source of Plato's similar image in Phaedrus. The five-fold structure: ātman (rider) – buddhi (driver) – manas (reins) – indriya (horses) – viṣaya (terrain). Each verse elaborates.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The allegory: the ātman is the indweller, the puruṣa in the chariot. The body is the vehicle. The buddhi controls; manas channels. The śarīra-ratha is a kāmya-ratha when the buddhi is undeveloped."],["Rangarāmānuja","Ātman here = jīva (the embodied self), with Paramātman as ultimate ground. The allegory teaches the proper hierarchical yoga: buddhi-niyantraṇa is yoga's first task."],["Madhva","each component is distinct, real, irreducible. The allegory affirms the pañca-bheda within the individual: ātman, buddhi, manas, indriya, śarīra are five."],["Sivānanda","The verse should be memorised. The whole psychology of yoga is here. Train the charioteer (viveka-buddhi); hold the reins (manaḥ-saṃyama); discipline the horses (indriya-nigraha)."],["Deussen","Plato's Phaedrus 246a-b has a strikingly similar image. Independent invention is possible; cultural transmission has been argued by Garbe and others."],["Aurobindo","The diagram of the integral being: the Puruṣa in the chariot of nature, with buddhi for his guide and mind for his reins, the senses for his coursing horses."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.4","title":"","sa":["इन्द्रियाणि हयानाहुर्विषयां स्तेषु गोचरान् । आत्मेन्द्रियमनोयुक्तं भोक्तेत्याहुर्मनीषिणः"],"iast":["indriyāṇi hayānāhurviṣayāṃ steṣu gocarān | ātmendriyamanoyuktaṃ bhoktetyāhurmanīṣiṇaḥ"],"words":[["indriyāṇi hayān āhuḥ","nom.pl.n. + acc.pl.m. + perf.3.pl.","the senses they call the horses"],["viṣayān teṣu gocarān","acc.pl.m. + loc.pl. + acc.pl.adj.","the objects, in/of these, (as) the pasture"],["ātma-indriya-manaḥ-yuktaṃ","cpd.acc.","(the ātman) joined with senses and mind"],["bhoktā iti āhuḥ manīṣiṇaḥ","nom.sg.m. + quot. + perf.3.pl. + nom.pl.m.","the thinkers call (the ātman so joined) the experiencer"]],"lit":"The senses, they call the horses; the objects of the senses, the pasture (in which they graze). The ātman, joined with the senses and the mind, the wise call ‘the experiencer’.","para":"The horses are the senses; their grazing-ground is sense-objects. When the ātman is yoked to manas-and-indriyas, it functions as bhoktṛ (experiencer). The allegory is structurally complete.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Bhoktṛ = the experiencer-ātman is only ātman-with-upādhi. In itself, ātman is non-experiencer; with the manas-indriya yoga it appears as bhoktṛ. The verse expresses the saṃsāra-condition."],["Rangarāmānuja","The ātman is genuinely bhoktṛ at the embodied level; this is its real (not illusory) function. The Lord-as-antaryāmin sustains both ātman and its bhoga."],["Madhva","bhoktṛtva is jīva's real attribute, not adventitious. The verse confirms the five-fold structure and the jīva's intrinsic experiencer-nature."],["Sivānanda","Mark how the chariot becomes a runaway when the senses (horses) chase objects (pasture) without buddhi's restraint. The picture is exact in lived experience."],["Deussen","The five-term allegory predates Sāṃkhya's classical tattva-listing by centuries. Deussen sees in it an early structural psychology."],["Aurobindo","Senses grazing in the pastures of the objects of sense — the soul experiencing through the body-mind-instrument the play of the outer world."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.5","title":"","sa":["यस्त्वविज्ञानवान्भवत्ययुक्तेन मनसा सदा । तस्येन्द्रियाण्यवश्यानि दुष्टाश्वा इव सारथेः"],"iast":["yastvavijñānavānbhavatyayuktena manasā sadā | tasyendriyāṇyavaśyāni duṣṭāśvā iva sāratheḥ"],"words":[["yaḥ tu a-vijñāna-vān bhavati","rel. + emph. + cpd.adj. + pres.3.sg.","he who is without knowledge"],["a-yuktena manasā sadā","adj.instr. + instr.sg.n. + adv.","with un-yoked mind always"],["tasya indriyāṇi a-vaśyāni","gen.dem. + nom.pl. + adj.pl.","his senses are uncontrolled"],["duṣṭa-aśvāḥ iva sāratheḥ","cpd.nom.pl. + indecl. + gen.sg.","like wild horses of a charioteer"]],"lit":"He who is devoid of right-knowing, with mind always un-yoked — his senses are uncontrolled, like wild horses of a (weak) charioteer.","para":"When buddhi lacks vijñāna (clear discrimination) and manas is unyoked, the senses run wild — and the rider is helpless. The allegory's first failure case.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> A-vijñāna-vān = devoid of viveka-buddhi. Duṣṭa-aśvāḥ = horses gone bad, refusing the rein. The picture is of catastrophic loss of inner government."],["Rangarāmānuja","Without Lord-directed buddhi, the inner government collapses. The verse motivates the necessity of the antaryāmin-darśana."],["Madhva","a-vijñāna-vān = one without yathārtha-jñāna of the five-fold tāratamya. Such a one cannot govern his own faculties."],["Sivānanda","Daily experience: when buddhi sleeps, senses run riot. The verse explains every regretted action."],["Deussen","The pedagogical contrast (verses 5 and 6) is one of the most lucid in Upaniṣadic literature — the two cases laid side by side."],["Aurobindo","When discrimination is absent and mind is unyoked, the senses are mutinous horses dragging the chariot wherever they please."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.6","title":"","sa":["यस्तु विज्ञानवान्भवति युक्तेन मनसा सदा । तस्येन्द्रियाणि वश्यानि सदश्वा इव सारथेः"],"iast":["yastu vijñānavānbhavati yuktena manasā sadā | tasyendriyāṇi vaśyāni sadaśvā iva sāratheḥ"],"words":[["yaḥ tu vijñāna-vān bhavati","rel. + emph. + cpd.adj. + pres.3.sg.","he who is endowed with knowledge"],["yuktena manasā sadā","adj.instr. + instr. + adv.","with yoked mind always"],["tasya indriyāṇi vaśyāni","gen.dem. + nom.pl. + adj.","his senses are controlled"],["sat-aśvāḥ iva sāratheḥ","cpd.nom.pl. + indecl. + gen.","like good horses of a charioteer"]],"lit":"But he who is endowed with right-knowing, with mind always yoked — his senses are obedient, like good horses of a (skilled) charioteer.","para":"The contrasting case: vijñāna-vān + yukta-manas → vaśyāni indriyāṇi. The yogic ideal in one verse, paired with the previous failure-case.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Vijñāna-vān = endowed with viveka. Sat-aśvāḥ = trained horses. The pair (vv. 5–6) establishes the principle: inner government is a learnable skill."],["Rangarāmānuja","When the buddhi is directed by bhakti-prajñā and manas is held by smaraṇa-saṃyama, the indriyas serve."],["Madhva","The structural symmetry of vv. 5–6 underlines the practical doctrine: cultivate vijñāna, yoke the manas, and the senses follow."],["Sivānanda","A trained mind makes trained senses; trained senses make a peaceful life; a peaceful life makes a clear buddhi; a clear buddhi sees the ātman. The chain is real."],["Deussen","The pair of verses functions as an Upaniṣadic version of the via positiva and via negativa. Both clarify by contrast."],["Aurobindo","When discrimination wakes and the mind is yoked, the senses become disciplined runners carrying the soul-chariot toward its supreme destination."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.7","title":"","sa":["यस्त्वविज्ञानवान्भवत्यमनस्कः सदाऽशुचिः । न स तत्पदमाप्नोति संसारं चाधिगच्छति"],"iast":["yastvavijñānavānbhavatyamanaskaḥ sadā'śuciḥ | na sa tatpadamāpnoti saṃsāraṃ cādhigacchati"],"words":[["yaḥ tu a-vijñāna-vān bhavati","rel.+ emph. + cpd.adj. + pres.3.sg.","he who is without knowledge"],["a-manaskaḥ sadā a-śuciḥ","adj. + adv. + adj.","un-minded (un-collected), always impure"],["na saḥ tat padam āpnoti","neg. + dem. + acc. + acc.sg.n. + pres.3.sg.","he does not reach that state"],["saṃsāraṃ ca adhi-gacchati","acc. + and + pres.3.sg.","and (only) attains saṃsāra"]],"lit":"He who is devoid of right-knowing, un-collected (in mind), always impure — he does not reach that state; he goes only to saṃsāra.","para":"The negative phala: lack of vijñāna + lack of manas-saṃyama + lack of śuddhi = the chariot runs into saṃsāra. The next verse gives the positive phala.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> A-manaska = whose manas is dispersed; a-śuci = whose antaḥkaraṇa is impure. The verse names three deficiencies; each blocks param-padam."],["Rangarāmānuja","Lack of saraṇāgati-buddhi + lack of bhakti-eka-vṛtti + lack of antar-śauca → saṃsāra. Each blockage requires its own remedy."],["Madhva","the three negations are real, each carries its specific karma-vipāka. The verse maps the failure modes precisely."],["Sivānanda","Knowledge + concentration + purity — these three together. Lacking any one, no spiritual progress. Restate the chain to oneself frequently."],["Deussen","Deussen reads this as the negative-phala matched by v.8's positive-phala. Standard Upaniṣadic two-paths structure."],["Aurobindo","The unawakened soul, mind scattered, impure, cannot reach the supreme; it remains bound to the wheel of birth and death."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.8","title":"","sa":["यस्तु विज्ञानवान्भवति समनस्कः सदा शुचिः । स तु तत्पदमाप्नोति यस्माद्।ह्भूयो न जायते"],"iast":["yastu vijñānavānbhavati samanaskaḥ sadā śuciḥ | sa tu tatpadamāpnoti yasmād|hbhūyo na jāyate"],"words":[["yaḥ tu vijñāna-vān bhavati","rel. + emph. + cpd.adj. + pres.3.sg.","he who is endowed with knowledge"],["sa-manaskaḥ sadā śuciḥ","adj. + adv. + adj.","collected in mind, always pure"],["saḥ tu tat padam āpnoti","dem. + emph. + acc.dem. + acc. + pres.3.sg.","he reaches that state"],["yasmāt bhūyaḥ na jāyate","abl.rel. + adv. + neg. + pres.3.sg.","from which (one) is not born again"]],"lit":"But he who is endowed with right-knowing, collected in mind, always pure — he attains that state from which (one is) not born again.","para":"The positive phala: vijñāna + sa-manas + śauca → tat padam → no rebirth. This is mokṣa named in functional terms: an-āvṛtti.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘Yasmāt bhūyo na jāyate’ — the lakṣaṇa of mokṣa as an-āvṛtti. Śaṅkara: the chariot, rightly driven, reaches the ātman, which is itself the goal; the journey ends."],["Rangarāmānuja","Tat padam = paramaṃ padaṃ viṣṇoḥ = Vaikuṇṭha. From there, the bhakta does not return to saṃsāra; the journey is teleologically completed."],["Madhva","an-āvṛtti from Viṣṇu-loka is the highest mokṣa. The verse establishes the categorical difference between this attainment and svarga-lokas."],["Sivānanda","Every spiritual seeker should pause on this verse. The promise is explicit: from that state, no return to birth. Such is the goal."],["Deussen","‘Yasmād bhūyo na jāyate’ becomes a stock phrase in later Vedānta. The Upaniṣadic doctrine of non-return is here crystallised."],["Aurobindo","The supreme attainment is the end of the cycle — the soul has come home and the wheel ceases to turn."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.9","title":"","sa":["विज्ञानसारथिर्यस्तु मनः प्रग्रहवान्नरः । सोऽध्वनः पारमाप्नोति तद्विष्णोः परमं पदम्"],"iast":["vijñānasārathiryastu manaḥ pragrahavānnaraḥ | so'dhvanaḥ pāramāpnoti tadviṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam"],"words":[["vijñāna-sārathiḥ yaḥ tu","cpd.nom. + rel. + emph.","he who has knowledge for his charioteer"],["manaḥ-pragraha-vān naraḥ","cpd.adj. + nom.sg.m.","possessed of the mind-rein, the man"],["saḥ adhvanaḥ pāram āpnoti","dem. + gen.sg.m. + acc.sg.n. + pres.3.sg.","he attains the far shore of the path"],["tat viṣṇoḥ paramam padam","dem. + gen.sg. + adj. + nom.sg.n.","that — the supreme abode of Viṣṇu"]],"lit":"The man whose charioteer is knowledge, who holds the rein of the mind — he attains the far shore of the path: that supreme abode of Viṣṇu.","para":"The chariot allegory's climax: the journey's destination is paramaṃ padam viṣṇoḥ — a direct quotation of Ṛgveda I.22.20. The supreme is here named theistically.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Param padam viṣṇoḥ — Śaṅkara: Viṣṇu here = the all-pervading Brahman, not the Vaiṣṇava sectarian deity. The verse names mokṣa with a Vedic citation."],["Rangarāmānuja","Locus classicus for Vaiṣṇavism. ‘Tat viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam’ = Vaikuṇṭha, named explicitly. The chariot-allegory's destination is the Lord's own abode."],["Madhva","the verse is decisive — mokṣa is Viṣṇu-prāpti. The Vedic phrase from Ṛgveda is cited to anchor the doctrine in śruti."],["Sivānanda","Notice the name Viṣṇu — for theistic seekers, the destination is named. For non-theistic, it is param padam — the supreme state. The reality is one."],["Deussen","‘Tad viṣṇoḥ paramaṃ padam’ = Ṛgveda I.22.20. The Kaṭha here makes its Vedic anchoring explicit at the structural climax of the allegory."],["Aurobindo","The chariot reaches its supreme destination — the abode of the pervading Lord, which is the soul's true home."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.10","title":"","sa":["इन्द्रियेभ्यः परा ह्यर्था अर्थेभ्यश्च परं मनः । मनसस्तु परा बुद्धिर्बुद्धेरात्मा महान्परः"],"iast":["indriyebhyaḥ parā hyarthā arthebhyaśca paraṃ manaḥ | manasastu parā buddhirbuddherātmā mahānparaḥ"],"words":[["indriyebhyaḥ parā hi arthāḥ","abl.pl. + adj.nom. + emph. + nom.pl.","higher than the senses (are) the objects"],["arthebhyaḥ ca paraṃ manaḥ","abl.pl. + and + adj. + nom.sg.n.","and higher than the objects, the mind"],["manasaḥ tu parā buddhiḥ","abl.sg. + emph. + adj. + nom.sg.f.","higher than the mind, the intellect"],["buddheḥ ātmā mahān paraḥ","abl.sg. + nom. + adj.nom. + adj.","higher than the intellect, the Great Self"]],"lit":"Higher than the senses are the objects (of sense); higher than the objects, the mind; higher than the mind, the intellect; higher than the intellect, the Great Self.","para":"The graded hierarchy of tattvas: indriya → artha → manas → buddhi → mahat-ātman. The Sāṃkhya tattva-list is here in seed-form, with mahat as fourth-from-top.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Mahān ātmā = Hiraṇyagarbha-buddhi, the cosmic mahat. Śaṅkara establishes the ascending graded scale of sūkṣmatā: each level subtler than the one below, each closer to ātman."],["Rangarāmānuja","The hierarchy reaches up to the cosmic Person. The Lord stands beyond even the mahat. Each level is real and ontologically distinct."],["Madhva","Madhva enumerates the tāratamya precisely: indriya (gross), artha (still gross but subtler), manas (sūkṣma), buddhi (sūkṣmatara), mahat (sūkṣmatama-among-prakṛtis). The graded hierarchy is a foundation of Dvaita ontology."],["Sivānanda","Memorise this ascending ladder. Yoga is the act of climbing it — withdrawing from each lower level and resting in the next."],["Deussen","The earliest extant tattva-hierarchy. Deussen treats it as proto-Sāṃkhya, antedating the Sāṃkhya-Kārikā by centuries."],["Aurobindo","The ascending stairway of the integral being: each rung subtler than the last, each closer to the soul's true centre."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.11","title":"","sa":["महतः परमव्यक्तमव्यक्तात्पुरुषः परः । पुरुषान्न परं किंचित्सा काष्ठा सा परा गतिः"],"iast":["mahataḥ paramavyaktamavyaktātpuruṣaḥ paraḥ | puruṣānna paraṃ kiṃcitsā kāṣṭhā sā parā gatiḥ"],"words":[["mahataḥ param a-vyaktam","abl.sg. + adj. + nom.sg.n.","higher than mahat (is) the un-manifest"],["a-vyaktāt puruṣaḥ paraḥ","abl.sg.n. + nom.sg.m. + adj.","higher than the un-manifest is the Puruṣa"],["puruṣāt na paraṃ kiṃcit","abl. + neg. + adj. + indef.","than the Puruṣa, nothing (is) higher"],["sā kāṣṭhā sā parā gatiḥ","dem.f. + nom.sg.f. + dem. + adj. + nom.f.","that (is) the consummation, that the supreme goal"]],"lit":"Higher than the (cosmic) mahat is the unmanifest (avyakta); higher than the avyakta is the Puruṣa. Than the Puruṣa, nothing is higher; that is the consummation, that the supreme goal.","para":"The hierarchy continues to its summit: mahat → avyakta → puruṣa. The puruṣa is the kāṣṭhā (culminating limit) and parā gatiḥ (supreme goal). Sāṃkhya terminology is fully present.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Avyakta = mūla-prakṛti (Sāṃkhya), but Śaṅkara reads it as māyā-upādhi of brahman. Puruṣa = supreme ātman = Brahman. The hierarchy culminates in the non-dual Self."],["Rangarāmānuja","Avyakta = the divine prakṛti as the Lord's body; Puruṣa = the supreme Person, Nārāyaṇa. The verse names the Lord as the absolute summit."],["Madhva","Avyakta = prakṛti (Lakṣmī's body); Puruṣa = Viṣṇu. Madhva uses the verse to establish Viṣṇu's sole supremacy: para-tattva."],["Sivānanda","The summit is the Puruṣa. Beyond Him, nothing. Make this your destination; no intermediate goal will satisfy."],["Deussen","Proto-Sāṃkhya hierarchy at its clearest. Mahat — avyakta — puruṣa is the same sequence as in classical Sāṃkhya, but reversed (Sāṃkhya: puruṣa apart from prakṛti)."],["Aurobindo","The supreme Person stands beyond all the powers of nature — the highest culmination, the summit beyond which nothing is."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.12","title":"","sa":["एष सर्वेषु भूतेषु गूढोऽऽत्मा न प्रकाशते । दृश्यते त्वग्र्यया बुद्ध्या सूक्ष्मया सूक्ष्मदर्शिभिः"],"iast":["eṣa sarveṣu bhūteṣu gūḍho''tmā na prakāśate | dṛśyate tvagryayā buddhyā sūkṣmayā sūkṣmadarśibhiḥ"],"words":[["eṣaḥ sarveṣu bhūteṣu gūḍhaḥ ātmā na prakāśate","dem. + loc.pl. + loc.pl. + adj. + nom. + neg. + pres.3.sg.","this ātman, hidden in all beings, does not shine forth"],["dṛśyate tu agryayā buddhyā","pres.pass.3.sg. + emph. + adj.instr. + instr.","but is seen by the keen intellect"],["sūkṣmayā sūkṣma-darśibhiḥ","adj.instr. + cpd.instr.pl.","subtle, by the subtle-seers"]],"lit":"Hidden in all beings, the ātman does not shine forth; but He is seen by the keen, subtle intellect — by those who see the subtle.","para":"The ātman is universally present yet hidden; only the agryā sūkṣmā buddhi (keen, subtle intellect) of sūkṣma-darśins discerns it. Spiritual seeing is the cultivation of buddhi-fineness.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Agryā buddhi = the first-class, sharpest intellect. Sūkṣma-darśin = those whose vision has been refined by viveka-vairāgya-śama-dama. The verse names the epistemic prerequisite."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord-as-antaryāmin is hidden in every being; only the bhakta whose buddhi is refined by śaraṇāgati sees Him. Coarse perception cannot reach."],["Madhva","the inner Lord (= Viṣṇu) is present in each jīva but not directly perceptible. Sādhanā refines the antaḥkaraṇa until the inner darśana opens."],["Sivānanda","Cultivate sūkṣma-buddhi. Crude analysis cannot perceive ātman. Yoga refines the very instrument of seeing."],["Deussen","Deussen notes the epistemological doctrine: the means of knowing must be commensurate with the object known. ātman, being subtle, requires a subtle organ."],["Aurobindo","Hidden in all that lives, the Self does not reveal itself to the gross sight; only the refined inner vision of the subtle-seers discerns it."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.13","title":"","sa":["यच्छेद्वाङ्मनसी प्राज्ञस्तद्यच्छेज्ज्ञान आत्मनि । ज्ञानमात्मनि महति नियच्छेत्तद्यच्छेच्छान्त आत्मनि"],"iast":["yacchedvāṅmanasī prājñastadyacchejjñāna ātmani | jñānamātmani mahati niyacchettadyacchecchānta ātmani"],"words":[["yacchet vāk manasī prājñaḥ","opt.3.sg. + acc.dual. + nom.sg.m.","let the wise (one) restrain speech-and-mind"],["tat yacchet jñāna ātmani","dem. + opt.3.sg. + loc.sg. + loc.sg.","that, let him restrain in the knowing-Self"],["jñānam ātmani mahati niyacchet","nom.sg.n. + loc. + loc.adj. + opt.3.sg.","let him hold knowledge in the Great Self"],["tat yacchet śānta ātmani","dem. + opt.3.sg. + loc.adj. + loc.sg.","that, let him hold in the Peaceful Self"]],"lit":"Let the wise restrain speech and mind in the knowing-self; let him hold the knowing-self in the Great Self; let him hold the Great Self in the Peaceful Self.","para":"The yoga-sūtra of Kaṭha. A four-stage withdrawal: speech-and-mind → buddhi (= jñāna-ātman) → mahat-ātman → śānta-ātman (= puruṣa). The classical reversal up the hierarchy of v. 10–11.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Yacchet = ‘let him restrain/withdraw’. The verse prescribes pratyāhāra in reverse order: vāk-manas withdrawn into buddhi; buddhi into mahat; mahat into the śānta-ātman (= ātman beyond all upādhis)."],["Rangarāmānuja","Each level dissolved into the next-higher in bhakti-pratyāhāra. The śānta-ātman is the Lord, the place of supreme samādhi."],["Madhva","the layered yoga is real and graded. Each stage of withdrawal corresponds to an actual ontological recognition. The śānta-ātmā is Viṣṇu."],["Sivānanda","A precise yoga formula. Withdraw the senses, withdraw the mind, withdraw the intellect, rest in the Great Self, then in the Peace itself."],["Deussen","Deussen reads this verse as the proto-Yoga of pratyāhāra-dhāraṇā-dhyāna-samādhi, pre-dating Patañjali by perhaps half a millennium."],["Aurobindo","The ascent of the consciousness: from speech and surface mind to the deeper knowing, thence to the wide cosmic Self, and finally into the supreme Peace."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.14","title":"","sa":["उत्तिष्ठत जाग्रत प्राप्य वरान्निबोधत । क्षुरस्य धारा निशिता दुरत्यया दुर्गं पथस्तत्कवयो वदन्ति"],"iast":["uttiṣṭhata jāgrata prāpya varānnibodhata | kṣurasya dhārā niśitā duratyayā durgaṃ pathastatkavayo vadanti"],"words":[["uttiṣṭhata jāgrata","imv.2.pl. + imv.2.pl.","arise, awake"],["prāpya varān nibodhata","abs. + acc.pl.m. + imv.2.pl.","having approached the boon-givers, understand"],["kṣurasya dhārā niśitā duratyayā","gen.sg.m. + nom.f. + adj.f. + adj.f.","the razor's edge, sharpened, hard to cross"],["durgaṃ pathaḥ tat kavayaḥ vadanti","adj.acc. + gen.sg.m. + dem. + nom.pl. + pres.3.pl.","the path of that — hard, the sages declare"]],"lit":"Arise! Awake! Having approached the boon-givers (the great teachers), understand. The path is sharpened like a razor's edge, hard to cross — the sages declare it.","para":"The most quoted exhortation in Kaṭha. Three imperatives: stand up, wake up, approach the great ones (varān = those who can give boons = sat-gurus). The path is razor-edge difficult; do not delay.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Varān = adhikāri-puruṣān, qualified-teachers who can bestow brahma-vidyā. Kṣurasya dhārā = the razor's edge, an image of the path's narrowness and difficulty. Śaṅkara: the exhortation is to all aspirants, in every age."],["Rangarāmānuja","Varān = bhagavat-pratyaks-anubhāvinaḥ — those who have direct vision of the Lord. Approach such ācāryas; the path of mokṣa requires their guidance."],["Madhva","‘uttiṣṭhata jāgrata’ is the call to every jīva trapped in saṃsāra. Wake from the long sleep of ignorance; seek the guru; the path is razor-narrow but real."],["Sivānanda","The most stirring verse in the Upaniṣad. Recite it daily. ‘Arise, awake’ — the call is to you, now, today."],["Deussen","The verse was Swāmī Vivekānanda's favourite, taken as the motto of his mission. ‘Arise, awake’ — uttiṣṭhata jāgrata — became the watchword of the Indian renaissance."],["Aurobindo","The clarion-call to the soul: rise from the sleep of the senses, awaken to your supreme self-knowledge. Seek those who know, for the path is narrow as a razor's edge."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.15","title":"","sa":["अशब्दमस्पर्शमरूपमव्ययं तथाऽरसं नित्यमगन्धवच्च यत् । अनाद्यनन्तं महतः परं ध्रुवं निचाय्य तन्मृत्युमुखात् प्रमुच्यते"],"iast":["aśabdamasparśamarūpamavyayaṃ tathā'rasaṃ nityamagandhavacca yat | anādyanantaṃ mahataḥ paraṃ dhruvaṃ nicāyya tanmṛtyumukhāt pramucyate"],"words":[["a-śabdam a-sparśam a-rūpam a-vyayam","adj.acc.×4","soundless, touchless, formless, undecaying"],["tathā a-rasaṃ nityam a-gandhavat ca yat","adv. + adj.×3","likewise tasteless, eternal, smell-less, and (the) which"],["an-ādi an-antam mahataḥ paraṃ dhruvam","adj. + adj. + abl. + adj. + adj.","beginning-less, end-less, beyond mahat, firm"],["nicāyya tat mṛtyu-mukhāt pramucyate","abs. + dem. + cpd.abl. + pres.3.sg.middle","having recognised, one is freed from the jaws of death"]],"lit":"Soundless, touchless, formless, undecaying, likewise tasteless, eternal, smell-less, beginning-less, end-less, beyond mahat, firm — having recognised That, one is freed from the jaws of death.","para":"The supreme is described by negation of the five sense-objects (sound, touch, form, taste, smell) and of all change-attributes. Nicāyya = having recognised through dhyāna; the phala is mṛtyu-mukhāt pramukti.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The five negations of the tan-mātras + further negations of decay, beginning, end. Mahataḥ paraṃ = beyond the cosmic mahat. The verse is a sustained neti-neti of the highest order, culminating in mokṣa."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord transcends the tan-mātras and all empirical categories. ‘Mṛtyu-mukhāt pramucyate’ = released from saṃsāra-cakra. The verse names the supreme Lord by negation."],["Madhva","the negations apply to Viṣṇu's nirguṇa aspect (free of prakṛti-guṇas) but not to His sva-rūpa-guṇas (jñāna, ānanda, etc.). The negation is selective."],["Sivānanda","Daily meditation: ‘a-śabdam a-sparśam a-rūpam a-vyayam, tathā a-rasaṃ nityam a-gandhavac ca yat...’. The verse is a complete dhyāna-mantra in itself."],["Deussen","The systematic negation of the tan-mātras parallels Sāṃkhya's tan-mātra-doctrine. The Upaniṣad uses it apophatically."],["Aurobindo","Beyond all that is heard, touched, seen, tasted, smelt; eternal, infinite, supreme. To know That is to be free of the mouth of death."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.16","title":"","sa":["नाचिकेतमुपाख्यानं मृत्युप्रोक्तं सनातनम् । उक्त्वा श्रुत्वा च मेधावी ब्रह्मलोके महीयते"],"iast":["nāciketamupākhyānaṃ mṛtyuproktaṃ sanātanam | uktvā śrutvā ca medhāvī brahmaloke mahīyate"],"words":[["nāciketam upākhyānaṃ mṛtyu-proktam sanātanam","acc. + acc. + cpd.adj. + adj.","the Nāciketa narrative, told by Death, eternal"],["uktvā śrutvā ca medhāvī","abs. + abs. + and + nom.sg.m.","having spoken and heard, the wise (one)"],["brahma-loke mahīyate","loc.cpd. + pres.pass.3.sg.","is exalted in the world of Brahman"]],"lit":"(This) Nāciketa upākhyāna, told by Death, eternal — having spoken (it) and heard (it), the wise one is exalted in the world of Brahman.","para":"Phala-śruti: the very recitation (uktvā) and hearing (śrutvā) of this Upaniṣad confers brahma-loka. The text becomes itself an instrument of merit.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Phala-śruti — Śaṅkara discusses these closing verses as karma-mārga residual: even for those who do not fully realise, mere study yields brahma-loka, from which mokṣa follows (krama-mukti)."],["Rangarāmānuja","Study + teaching of the Nāciketa-upākhyāna is itself a bhakti-act; its phala is brahma-loka, the antechamber to the Lord's abode."],["Madhva","phala-śruti is real; mere recitation, with śraddhā, yields the brahma-loka. But mokṣa requires direct jñāna."],["Sivānanda","Recitation of sacred Upaniṣadic texts has its own efficacy. Even imperfectly understood, the words act on the antaḥkaraṇa over time."],["Deussen","Deussen flags this and the next verse as possibly later interpolations (phala-śruti of a redactor). Śaṅkara, however, treats them as integral."],["Aurobindo","The very telling of this teaching, the very hearing of it, is itself a step of the soul toward the supreme abode."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra I.iii.17","title":"","sa":["य इमं परमं गुह्यं श्रावयेद् ब्रह्मसंसदि । प्रयतः श्राद्धकाले वा तदानन्त्याय कल्पते । तदानन्त्याय कल्पत इति"],"iast":["ya imaṃ paramaṃ guhyaṃ śrāvayed brahmasaṃsadi | prayataḥ śrāddhakāle vā tadānantyāya kalpate | tadānantyāya kalpata iti"],"words":[["yaḥ imam paramam guhyaṃ śrāvayet brahma-saṃsadi","rel. + acc. + acc. + acc.adj. + opt.3.sg. + loc.cpd.","he who recites this supreme secret in the assembly of brahmins"],["prayataḥ śrāddha-kāle vā","adj.nom. + cpd.loc. + or","self-restrained, or at the time of the śrāddha"],["tat ānantyāya kalpate","dem. + dat.sg.n. + pres.3.sg.middle","that is conducive to (his) endlessness"],["tat ānantyāya kalpate iti","dem. + dat. + 3.sg. + quot.","— so, that is conducive to endlessness, (the text concludes)"]],"lit":"He who recites this supreme secret in an assembly of brahmins, (or who is) self-restrained at the time of the śrāddha (ritual for the ancestors) — that is conducive to his endlessness. That is conducive to endlessness, so (it is said).","para":"Final phala-śruti: recitation in a brahma-sabhā or during śrāddha-kāla yields ānantya (endlessness, immortality). The doubled closing phrase marks the formal end of the first adhyāya.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Brahma-saṃsadi = an assembly of brahma-jñānins. Śrāddha-kāle = the funeral-rite period, when the pitṛs are propitiated. Either context, the recitation confers ānantya. The doubled phrase is a formal closure-mark in Upaniṣadic style."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta who teaches in proper assembly or recites during śrāddha multiplies the phala. Bhagavat-kīrtana in any form is mukti-pradā."],["Madhva","the phala-śruti is to be taken at face value. The specific contexts (assembly, śrāddha) maximise the efficacy."],["Sivānanda","The text closes with a promise. Recite when possible; teach when invited; the act itself confers blessing — for self and others."],["Deussen","The doubled formula ‘tad ānantyāya kalpate iti’ is the redactional sigillum closing the first adhyāya. Standard Upaniṣadic literary marker."],["Aurobindo","The supreme secret, transmitted to the qualified, increases for all who hear it. The first part of the teaching closes with this assurance of permanence."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya II · Vallī i","u":[{"ref":"Mantra II.i.1","title":"","sa":["पराञ्चि खानि व्यतृणत् स्वयम्भू स्तस्मात्पराङ्पश्यति नान्तरात्मन् । कश्चिद्धीरः प्रत्यगात्मानमैक्ष दावृत्तचक्षुरमृतत्वमिच्छन्"],"iast":["parāñci khāni vyatṛṇat svayambhū stasmātparāṅpaśyati nāntarātman | kaściddhīraḥ pratyagātmānamaikṣa dāvṛttacakṣuramṛtatvamicchan"],"words":[["parāñci khāni vyatṛṇat svayam-bhūḥ","adj.acc.pl. + acc.pl.n. + impf.3.sg. + nom.sg.m.","the Self-born bored the apertures outward"],["tasmāt parāṅ paśyati na antar-ātman","abl. + adv. + pres.3.sg. + neg. + acc.adj.","therefore (one) sees outward, not the inner Self"],["kaḥ cit dhīraḥ pratyak ātmānam aikṣat","indef.adj. + nom.sg.m. + adv. + acc. + impf.3.sg.","some wise one (alone) beheld the inward Self"],["āvṛtta-cakṣuḥ amṛtatvam icchan","cpd.nom. + acc.sg.n. + pres.ptcp.","(with) eye turned inward, desiring immortality"]],"lit":"The Self-born (Brahmā) pierced the (sense-)apertures outward; therefore (man) sees outward, not the inner Self. Some wise one — desiring immortality — beheld the inward Self with eye turned inward.","para":"The cosmogonic explanation of why mokṣa is rare: the senses are constitutively outward-facing. To see the ātman, one must reverse the direction of looking (āvṛtta-cakṣuḥ).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Svayam-bhūḥ = the Creator. Vyatṛṇat = bored, perforated — the indriyas are openings outward. Pratyak-ātman = the inward-facing ātman. The verse is the locus classicus of the antaḥ-mukha-vṛtti doctrine."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord himself made the senses outward-facing so that the jīva might initially perceive the world and then turn inward through viveka. The intention is pedagogical."],["Madhva","Svayam-bhūḥ = Brahmā, under Viṣṇu's direction. The verse establishes that ātman-darśana requires deliberate reversal — pravṛtti is natural, nivṛtti must be cultivated."],["Sivānanda","Read this verse every morning. The whole strategy of yoga is in ‘āvṛtta-cakṣuḥ’ — turn the gaze inward. This single act, sustained, is sādhanā."],["Deussen","Pratyak vs. parāñc — a foundational Upaniṣadic distinction. The inner is structurally harder to know than the outer; the cosmos itself is built that way."],["Aurobindo","The Self-existent has made the doors of consciousness open outward; man must by his own choice turn the gaze inward if he is to find the immortal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.2","title":"","sa":["पराचः कामाननुयन्ति बाला स्ते मृत्योर्यन्ति विततस्य पाशम् । अथ धीरा अमृतत्वं विदित्वा ध्रुवमध्रुवेष्विह न प्रार्थयन्ते"],"iast":["parācaḥ kāmānanuyanti bālā ste mṛtyoryanti vitatasya pāśam | atha dhīrā amṛtatvaṃ viditvā dhruvamadhruveṣviha na prārthayante"],"words":[["parācaḥ kāmān anu-yanti bālāḥ","adv. + acc.pl. + pres.3.pl. + nom.pl.","outward, the children follow the desires"],["te mṛtyoḥ yanti vitatasya pāśam","dem. + gen.sg. + pres.3.pl. + adj.gen. + acc.sg.","they go into the snare of wide-spread Death"],["atha dhīrāḥ a-mṛtatvaṃ viditvā","adv. + nom.pl. + acc.n. + abs.","but the wise, having known immortality"],["dhruvam a-dhruveṣu iha na prārthayante","adj.acc.n. + loc.adj.pl. + adv. + neg. + 3.pl.middle","do not seek the permanent in the impermanent things here"]],"lit":"Outward run the children after desires; they go into the wide-spread snare of Death. But the wise, having known immortality, do not seek the permanent among the impermanent things here.","para":"The bāla (childish, undeveloped) chases external kāmas — Death's bait. The dhīra knows amṛtatva and does not look for dhruva among the adhruva. The verse paraphrases the entire śreyas-preyas doctrine concisely.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Parācaḥ = outward, externally. Pāśam = the snare — the senses-objects-attachment knot. The wise look for dhruva (the eternal) only in dhruva-dharman = ātman, not in adhruva-dharman = the world."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta does not seek the Lord in objects. Vitatasya pāśam = the cosmic net of māyā-prakṛti. Only Lord-darśana cuts the net."],["Madhva","dhruva = Viṣṇu; adhruva = all else. The mistake of seeking the permanent in impermanent things is the root of saṃsāra. The verse names the error precisely."],["Sivānanda","Daily examination: am I seeking the dhruva in dhruva, or in adhruva? The wise are not those without desires but those whose desires are properly aimed."],["Deussen","‘Mṛtyor pāśam vitatam’ = the wide-cast net of death — a Vedic image (cf. Ṛgveda VII.59.12). Deussen sees Vedic substrate sublimated into Upaniṣadic doctrine."],["Aurobindo","The unwise chase the outward fleeting things and walk into the net of Death; the wise have set their hearts on the eternal and refuse the perishable."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.3","title":"","sa":["येन रूपं रसं गन्धं शब्दान् स्पर्शांश्च मैथुनान् । एतेनैव विजानाति किमत्र परिशिष्यते । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["yena rūpaṃ rasaṃ gandhaṃ śabdān sparśāṃśca maithunān | etenaiva vijānāti kimatra pariśiṣyate | etadvai tat"],"words":[["yena rūpaṃ rasaṃ gandhaṃ śabdān","instr.rel. + acc. + acc. + acc. + acc.pl.","by which one (knows) form, taste, smell, sounds"],["sparśān ca maithunān","acc.pl. + and + acc.pl.","and touches and sexual pleasures"],["etena eva vijānāti","instr.dem. + emph. + pres.3.sg.","by this very one (the ātman) one knows (all)"],["kim atra pari-śiṣyate","interr. + adv. + pres.pass.3.sg.","what is left over here?"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"That by which (one knows) form, taste, smell, sounds, touches, and pleasures — by THIS very one (the ātman) one knows (all). What is left over? THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The ātman as the single knower behind all sense-modalities. The famous closing refrain ‘etat vai tat’ (this is verily that) — answering the Naciketā question of I.ii.14 — now begins to mark the supreme. It recurs through II.i.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The ātman as the constant subject of all sense-cognitions. No object is known except by the ātman. ‘Kim atra pari-śiṣyate’ = what could be left over? — i.e., what besides ātman is there to know? Etat vai tat = the answer to the question of brahma-vidyā."],["Rangarāmānuja","The jīvātman, illumined by the Lord, is the inner knower. Etat vai tat = this jīva, with the antaryāmin within, is the secret you sought."],["Madhva","ātman as bhoktṛ-jñātṛ is real. The verse establishes the jīva's cognitive capacity. ‘Etat vai tat’ ≠ identity with Brahman but recognition of the jīva-svarūpa."],["Sivānanda","Behind every sensation is the same Knower. Trace any perception back to its knower — you arrive at the same one. This is the simplest pratyak-ātma-vicāra."],["Deussen","‘Etad vai tat’ becomes the refrain of vallī II.i. Deussen notes the deliberate punctuation: each major insight is sealed with this formula."],["Aurobindo","By the same self all the senses know — the one consciousness through many windows. What more is there to know than this one Knower?"]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.4","title":"","sa":["स्वप्नान्तं जागरितान्तं चोभौ येनानुपश्यति । महान्तं विभुमात्मानं मत्वा धीरो न शोचति"],"iast":["svapnāntaṃ jāgaritāntaṃ cobhau yenānupaśyati | mahāntaṃ vibhumātmānaṃ matvā dhīro na śocati"],"words":[["svapna-antaṃ jāgarita-antaṃ ca ubhau","cpd.acc. + cpd.acc. + and + acc.dual","dream-state and waking-state, both"],["yena anu-paśyati","instr.rel. + pres.3.sg.","by which one observes"],["mahāntaṃ vibhum ātmānaṃ","acc.adj. + acc.adj. + acc.","the great, all-pervading Self"],["matvā dhīraḥ na śocati","abs. + nom. + neg. + pres.3.sg.","having thought (on Him), the wise does not grieve"]],"lit":"By which (Self) one observes both the dream-state and the waking-state — having thought on (Him as) the great, all-pervading Self, the wise (one) does not grieve.","para":"The ātman witnesses both jāgrat and svapna. To recognise this single witness across states is to be freed from oscillation — the locus classicus of avasthātraya-vicāra in seed.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The witness of both states is the same; therefore that witness is neither the dream-nor the waking-content. Avasthātraya-sākṣin doctrine begins here, fully unfolded in Māṇḍūkya."],["Rangarāmānuja","The jīva, sustained by the antaryāmin, witnesses dream and waking. The Lord is the constant; the states pass."],["Madhva","jīva-bhoktṛtva is real in both states; the ātman is the abiding witness. The verse fits the bhoktṛ-doctrine, not Advaita's sākṣi-only reading."],["Sivānanda","Reflect on yourself: you have woken from dream — the witness was constant. You will wake from waking — the same witness. ‘Matvā dhīraḥ na śocati’."],["Deussen","Deussen reads this as Kaṭha's tribute to the avasthātraya-method later developed in Māṇḍūkya. The Kaṭha version is in seed, but unambiguous."],["Aurobindo","The Self observes the dream and the waking from a station above both; recognising this, the soul ceases to grieve over the passing scenes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.5","title":"","sa":["य इमं मध्वदं वेद आत्मानं जीवमन्तिकात् । ईशानं भूतभव्यस्य न ततो विजुगुप्सते । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["ya imaṃ madhvadaṃ veda ātmānaṃ jīvamantikāt | īśānaṃ bhūtabhavyasya na tato vijugupsate | etadvai tat"],"words":[["yaḥ imaṃ madhv-adaṃ veda","rel. + acc.dem. + cpd.acc. + pres.3.sg. of √vid","he who knows this honey-eater"],["ātmānaṃ jīvam antikāt","acc. + acc. + adv.","the ātman, the jīva, intimately"],["īśānaṃ bhūta-bhavyasya","acc.sg.m. + cpd.gen.","ruler of past and future"],["na tataḥ vi-jugupsate","neg. + adv. + pres.3.sg.middle of √gup","from him (one) is not shrunk away from"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"He who knows this honey-eater — the ātman, the jīva — intimately, the ruler of past and future: from him he is not shrunk away (i.e., he ceases to fear). THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"Madhv-adam = the experiencer of karma-phala (honey = pleasure of action). To know this inner enjoyer as the lord of time is to cease to fear. The avidyā-pakṣa is the fear of death; recognition of ātman removes it.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Madhv-adam = the enjoyer of karma-fruit. Antikāt = at close range, by direct cognition. Īśānaṃ bhūta-bhavyasya = the controller of past and future = the very ātman, identical with Brahman in the Advaita reading."],["Rangarāmānuja","Madhv-adam = jīva as enjoyer of karma-honey. Antaryāmin (Lord) is the īśānaṃ. The verse names two: jīva (the honey-eater) and the Lord (the ruler)."],["Madhva","the verse decisively distinguishes jīva (madhv-adam) from Paramātman (īśāna). ‘Antikāt’ — both are known intimately but as distinct."],["Sivānanda","The honey-eater within is the experiencer. He who is master of his own ātman is master of time. From such a one, fear has no purchase."],["Deussen","‘Madhv-ad’ recurs in Ṛgveda I.164.20 (the two birds on the tree). Kaṭha here picks up the imagery and applies it to ātman-vidyā."],["Aurobindo","He who knows the eater of the honey — the soul tasting the experiences of life — and the Lord of past and future, is freed from shrinking and from all fear."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.6","title":"","sa":["यः पूर्वं तपसो जातमद्।ह्भ्यः पूर्वमजायत । गुहां प्रविश्य तिष्ठन्तं यो भूतेभिर्व्यपश्यत । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["yaḥ pūrvaṃ tapaso jātamad|hbhyaḥ pūrvamajāyata | guhāṃ praviśya tiṣṭhantaṃ yo bhūtebhirvyapaśyata | etadvai tat"],"words":[["yaḥ pūrvaṃ tapasaḥ jātam","rel. + adv. + abl.sg.n. + p.p.p.","He who was born before, from tapas"],["adbhyaḥ pūrvam ajāyata","abl.pl. + adv. + impf.3.sg.","who came forth even before the waters"],["guhām praviśya tiṣṭhantaṃ","acc.f. + abs. + pres.ptcp.acc.","having entered the (heart-)cave, abiding"],["yaḥ bhūtebhiḥ vy-apaśyata","rel. + instr.pl. + impf.3.sg.","He who looked forth through the elements"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"He who was born before (anything), from tapas, who came forth even before the waters; having entered the (heart-)cave, He abides — He who looked forth through the (cosmic) elements. THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The cosmogonic identity: that which was first-born from tapas (cosmic ardour), prior to the primal waters (cf. Ṛgveda X.129), is the same as that which now indwells the heart-cave. Macrocosm and microcosm meet in the ātman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Pūrvaṃ tapasaḥ jātam — Hiraṇyagarbha, born of cosmic tapas. Adbhyaḥ pūrvam = before the primordial waters. The same one indwells the guhā. The macro-micro identity is the verse's burden."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord, the first-born, indwells the heart of every being. Bhūtebhir vyapaśyata = He sees through (= in) the elements. Antaryāmin doctrine in seed."],["Madhva","Pūrvam jātam = the first-born Hiraṇyagarbha (= Brahmā), as Viṣṇu's first manifestation. Madhva preserves the hierarchical distinction even in cosmogony."],["Sivānanda","The first-born of cosmic tapas is the same as the dweller in your heart. Macrocosm = microcosm. Worship within and you worship the primal."],["Deussen","‘Adbhyaḥ pūrvam’ = before the waters — a direct echo of Ṛgveda X.129.3 (the Nāsadīya). Kaṭha consciously links to the great Vedic cosmogony."],["Aurobindo","He who was first-born in the depths of cosmic tapasyā, who looked forth through the primordial elements — the same dwells now in the cave of the heart."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.7","title":"","sa":["या प्राणेन संभवत्यदितिर्देवतामयी । गुहां प्रविश्य तिष्ठन्तीं या भूतेभिर्व्यजायत । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["yā prāṇena saṃbhavatyaditirdevatāmayī | guhāṃ praviśya tiṣṭhantīṃ yā bhūtebhirvyajāyata | etadvai tat"],"words":[["yā prāṇena saṃbhavati aditiḥ devatā-mayī","rel.f. + instr. + pres.3.sg. + nom.f. + cpd.adj.","She who arises with prāṇa — Aditi, of divine-nature"],["guhāṃ praviśya tiṣṭhantīm","acc.f. + abs. + pres.ptcp.acc.f.","having entered the cave, abiding"],["yā bhūtebhiḥ vy-ajāyata","rel.f. + instr.pl. + impf.3.sg.","who came forth through the elements"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"She who arises with prāṇa, Aditi, of divine nature; having entered the (heart-)cave, abiding; who came forth through the elements — THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"Pairing the previous verse (masculine puruṣa-form) with feminine Aditi-form. Both indwell the cave; both manifest through the bhūtas; both are the same. The verse incorporates Vedic devī-tradition.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Aditi = the primal goddess of Vedic mythology, here identified with the ātman as self-as-deva-svarūpa. Devatā-mayī = comprising all divinities. Same as ātman in feminine grammatical aspect."],["Rangarāmānuja","Aditi = Śrī (Lakṣmī), inseparable from the Lord. Both Lord (v. 6) and Goddess (v. 7) indwell the cave. The bhakta worships both."],["Madhva","Aditi here = Lakṣmī. Madhva preserves the feminine principle as ontologically distinct from Viṣṇu yet eternally united with Him."],["Sivānanda","The same supreme principle, in masculine and feminine aspects, indwells the heart. Worship as you will; the Truth is one."],["Deussen","Aditi has a long Vedic history. Here she is re-deployed as a synonym for the indwelling ātman. The Upaniṣad assimilates Vedic mythology to its metaphysics."],["Aurobindo","The Mother-power who emerges with the breath, presides over the divinities, dwells in the cave of the heart, and looks forth through all the elements."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.8","title":"","sa":["अरण्योर्निहितो जातवेदा गर्भ इव सुभृतो गर्भिणीभिः । दिवे दिवे ईड्यो जागृवद्भिर्हविष्मद्भिर्मनुष्येभिरग्निः । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["araṇyornihito jātavedā garbha iva subhṛto garbhiṇībhiḥ | dive dive īḍyo jāgṛvadbhirhaviṣmadbhirmanuṣyebhiragniḥ | etadvai tat"],"words":[["araṇyoḥ ni-hitaḥ jāta-vedāḥ","loc.dual + p.p.p. + cpd.nom.sg.m.","set in the two fire-sticks, the Knower-of-the-born (Agni)"],["garbhaḥ iva su-bhṛtaḥ garbhiṇībhiḥ","nom. + indecl. + p.p.p. + instr.pl.","like a foetus well-borne by pregnant women"],["dive dive īḍyaḥ jāgṛvat-bhiḥ haviṣmat-bhiḥ","adv. + nom.gerundive + cpd.instr.pl. + cpd.instr.pl.","daily to be worshipped by those who are awake, with oblations"],["manuṣyebhiḥ agniḥ","instr.pl. + nom.","by men, this Agni (— is to be worshipped)"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"Set in the two fire-sticks, like a foetus well-borne by pregnant women, is Jātaveda (Agni) — to be daily worshipped by the wakeful, by men with oblations. THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"Agni's three-fold concealment: in the araṇi-sticks, in the womb-of-women (as the next fire-of-life), in the wakeful mind. The same ātman-fire underlies all. The cosmic-and-ritual fire is identified with the inner ātman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Jātavedāḥ = the all-knowing fire = the ātman in its illumining function. The double concealment (in the fire-sticks, in the womb) is parallel to its concealment in the heart-cave. The vidvāns worship it daily."],["Rangarāmānuja","Agni here = the Lord as the inner fire of jñāna-bhakti. Daily ritual + daily upāsanā kindle Him."],["Madhva","Agni is one of Viṣṇu's avatāric forms (cf. Ṛgveda I.1.1 — agnir hotā). The fire-sticks contain Him potentially; the wakeful kindle Him actually."],["Sivānanda","Agni in the body is the digestive-and-vital fire (jaṭhara-agni); Agni in the mind is the dīpta-buddhi. Tend both; both are aspects of the inner Self."],["Deussen","‘Araṇyoḥ nihitaḥ’ is Vedic technical phrase for the ritual fire latent in the friction-sticks. The Upaniṣad converts it into a meditation on the ever-latent ātman-fire."],["Aurobindo","The Fire is hidden in the two pieces of wood, in the mother's womb, in the heart of the wakeful — the same flame in all, the immortal in the mortal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.9","title":"","sa":["यतश्चोदेति सूर्योऽस्तं यत्र च गच्छति । तं देवाः सर्वेऽर्पितास्तदु नात्येति कश्चन । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["yataścodeti sūryo'staṃ yatra ca gacchati | taṃ devāḥ sarve'rpitāstadu nātyeti kaścana | etadvai tat"],"words":[["yataḥ ca udeti sūryaḥ astam yatra ca gacchati","rel.abl. + and + pres.3.sg. + nom. + acc.sg.n. + rel. + and + pres.3.sg.","from where the sun rises, where it goes to setting"],["taṃ devāḥ sarve arpitāḥ","acc. + nom.pl. + adj. + p.p.p.","on Him all the gods are fixed"],["tat u na atyeti kaḥ cana","dem. + emph. + neg. + pres.3.sg. + indef.nom.","beyond That none whatsoever passes"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"From which the sun rises, in which it goes to its setting — on Him all the gods are fixed; beyond Him none passes. THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The source of sunrise and locus of sunset; the ground on which all devas rest; the limit no one transcends. The cosmic absolute named in cosmological terms.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘Yataḥ ca udeti’ = the source from which manifestation arises; ‘astam yatra ca gacchati’ = the dissolution-ground. Brahman is both ādi and anta. The gods (= cosmic functions) are arpitāḥ — fixed/founded — upon Him."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord is the source and the goal of all manifestation. Devāḥ sarve arpitāḥ — all gods are dependent upon Him. ‘Na atyeti kaścana’ — none transcends Him; He is the limit."],["Madhva","Viṣṇu is the support of all devas. The verse confirms Viṣṇu's sole supremacy: ‘na atyeti kaścana’ — none transcends Him, not even Brahmā or Rudra."],["Sivānanda","The Self is the ground of the cosmic process. Every rising, every setting, every circulation of the world's life — all in Him. He is the limit beyond limit."],["Deussen","Cosmologically, the verse echoes Ṛgveda I.50 (sūrya-sūkta) — the sun's path as the circuit of cosmic order. Kaṭha makes the path's source-and-end ātman."],["Aurobindo","The supreme is the source of all our risings and the abode of all our settings; the gods themselves are anchored in Him; nothing reaches beyond."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.10","title":"","sa":["यदेवेह तदमुत्र यदमुत्र तदन्विह । मृत्योः स मृत्युमाप्नोति य इह नानेव पश्यति"],"iast":["yadeveha tadamutra yadamutra tadanviha | mṛtyoḥ sa mṛtyumāpnoti ya iha nāneva paśyati"],"words":[["yat eva iha tat amutra","rel.dem. + emph. + adv. + dem. + adv.","what (is) here, that (is) there"],["yat amutra tat anu iha","rel. + adv. + dem. + adv. + adv.","what (is) there, that follows here"],["mṛtyoḥ saḥ mṛtyum āpnoti","gen. + dem. + acc. + pres.3.sg.","from death to death he goes"],["yaḥ iha nānā iva paśyati","rel. + adv. + indecl. + indecl. + pres.3.sg.","who here sees as if it were many"]],"lit":"What is here, that is there; what is there, that is here too. From death to death he goes who sees as if many here.","para":"The Advaitic refrain: the same Reality is here and there — to see multiplicity is to be trapped in saṃsāra. The verse is identical to Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka IV.iv.19 — the textual link is direct.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The locus classicus of non-difference: yat iha tat amutra, yad amutra tad iha — same reality in every locus. ‘Mṛtyor mṛtyum āpnoti yaḥ nāneva paśyati’ — to see plurality is to fall from death to death."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord is the same in His various forms; the bhakta who sees fragmentation in the Lord's manifestations falls. The unity sought is of the Lord's vyāpti, not of identity-of-everything."],["Madhva","Madhva reads the verse cautiously: ‘nānā iva paśyati’ — sees ‘as if’ many, where the ‘as if’ marks māyā-confusion at one level, but does not erase the real pañca-bheda. The seer who sees the Lord as multiple and unrelated falls."],["Sivānanda","Behind the apparent diversity is the one Reality. He who has not glimpsed the unity is reborn endlessly, chasing fragments."],["Deussen","Identical to Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka IV.iv.19. Deussen treats this as a textual borrowing — either direction is possible; the Kaṭha is generally taken as later."],["Aurobindo","Here and there are one to the awakened soul; to see difference where there is unity is to remain entangled in the chain of death."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.11","title":"","sa":["मनसैवेदमाप्तव्यं नेह नानाऽस्ति किंचन । मृत्योः स मृत्युं गच्छति य इह नानेव पश्यति"],"iast":["manasaivedamāptavyaṃ neha nānā'sti kiṃcana | mṛtyoḥ sa mṛtyuṃ gacchati ya iha nāneva paśyati"],"words":[["manasā eva idam āptavyaṃ","instr.sg.n. + emph. + acc.dem. + gerundive","by the mind alone is this to be attained"],["na iha nānā asti kiṃ cana","neg. + adv. + indecl. + 3.sg. + indef.","here there is no multiplicity whatsoever"],["mṛtyoḥ saḥ mṛtyum gacchati","gen. + dem. + acc. + pres.3.sg.","from death to death he goes"],["yaḥ iha nānā iva paśyati","rel. + adv. + indecl. + pres.3.sg.","who here sees as if it were many"]],"lit":"By the (purified) mind alone is this to be attained. There is no multiplicity whatsoever here. From death to death he goes who here sees as if many.","para":"The repetition with intensification: the means is manas (refined mind, dhī); the goal is unity-perception. The verse establishes a dhyāna-pratyaya method.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Manasā eva = by the mind alone (no external means). ‘Na iha nānā asti’ — there is no multiplicity here at the param-mārthika level. The dṛṣṭi-bheda is the saṃsāra-cause."],["Rangarāmānuja","The mind, turned bhakti-ward, alone realises the Lord. ‘Nānā iva’ — seeing the Lord as if disjoint is the false vision."],["Madhva","Madhva reads ‘manasā eva idam āptavyam’ as upāsanā-vidhi: by the manas with bhakti-saṃskāra, the Lord is to be approached."],["Sivānanda","Refine the manas; the Reality cannot be reached by any other instrument. Both negative (neti-neti) and positive (mantra-japa, dhyāna) practices refine."],["Deussen","‘Manasā eva idam āptavyam’ — a key meditation-doctrine. Mind alone is the instrument; but the mind here is buddhi-suddhā, not the ordinary discursive manas."],["Aurobindo","Only by the refined and concentrated mind can this be reached. Here in the unity-vision there is no multiplicity; otherwise we wander from death to death."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.12","title":"","sa":["अङ्गुष्ठमात्रः पुरुषो मध्य आत्मनि तिष्ठति । ईशानं भूतभव्यस्य न ततो विजुगुप्सते । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["aṅguṣṭhamātraḥ puruṣo madhya ātmani tiṣṭhati | īśānaṃ bhūtabhavyasya na tato vijugupsate | etadvai tat"],"words":[["aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ","cpd.nom. + nom.sg.m.","thumb-sized is the puruṣa"],["madhye ātmani tiṣṭhati","adv. + loc. + pres.3.sg.","in the middle of the self he abides"],["īśānaṃ bhūta-bhavyasya","acc. + cpd.gen.","the ruler of past and future"],["na tataḥ vi-jugupsate","neg. + adv. + pres.3.sg.","from him (one) does not shrink"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"Thumb-sized is the puruṣa; in the middle of the self he abides — ruler of past and future. From him (one) shrinks not. THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The aṅguṣṭha-mātra-puruṣa, ‘thumb-sized person’ — the indweller of the heart. The image is striking and recurs at II.iii.17. Reading varies: jīva (Madhva, Rangarāmānuja) or ātman-Brahman as locally manifest (Śaṅkara).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ = of the size of the thumb (the heart-cavity is so measured). The puruṣa is in fact all-pervading; the thumb-size is from the locus of meditation, not from intrinsic dimension. Śaṅkara is careful: this is upāsanā-rūpa, not svarūpa."],["Rangarāmānuja","The jīvātman is genuinely aṇu — of finite size, fittingly located in the heart-space of the size of a thumb. Paramātman is vibhu (all-pervading) but resides as antaryāmin in the same locus."],["Madhva","aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ describes the jīva's actual size (aṇu). This verse is a major scriptural locus for the doctrine of the finite jīva."],["Sivānanda","Whether ‘size’ is taken literally or as locus of meditation, the practical instruction is the same: meditate on the puruṣa in the heart of thumb-size."],["Deussen","Deussen reads aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ as an early micro-psychological localisation of the ātman — a doctrine paralleled in Maitrī VI.38 and Śvetāśvatara III.13."],["Aurobindo","The supreme Person occupies the inner heart of man as a thumb-sized flame — small in appearance, infinite in reach, master of all that was and shall be."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.13","title":"","sa":["अङ्गुष्ठमात्रः पुरुषो ज्योतिरिवाधूमकः । ईशानो भूतभव्यस्य स एवाद्य स उ श्वः । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["aṅguṣṭhamātraḥ puruṣo jyotirivādhūmakaḥ | īśāno bhūtabhavyasya sa evādya sa u śvaḥ | etadvai tat"],"words":[["aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ jyotiḥ iva a-dhūmakaḥ","cpd. + nom. + nom.sg.n. + indecl. + adj.","thumb-sized puruṣa, like a smokeless light"],["īśānaḥ bhūta-bhavyasya","nom. + cpd.gen.","ruler of past and future"],["saḥ eva adya saḥ u śvaḥ","dem. + emph. + adv. + dem. + emph. + adv.","He alone today, He even tomorrow"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"Thumb-sized is the puruṣa — like a smokeless flame — ruler of past and future. He alone is today, He even tomorrow. THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The image refined: not just thumb-sized but luminous as a smokeless flame. The temporal identity-affirmation: ‘saḥ eva adya saḥ u śvaḥ’ — He alone today, He even tomorrow — the ātman is time-invariant.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Jyotiḥ iva a-dhūmakaḥ = like a flame without smoke — pure luminosity. The smokelessness is significant: no admixture of any other element. The ātman's self-luminosity is here exalted."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord (or in another reading, the jīva-with-Lord) is the same yesterday and tomorrow — the ontological stability of the supreme."],["Madhva","‘sa eva adya sa u śvaḥ’ affirms the eternal sameness of Viṣṇu. The smokeless flame is the supreme jyotiḥ, without admixture of prakṛtic guṇas."],["Sivānanda","Meditate on the inner light, smokeless and steady. Stabilise the dhyāna there; this is the most accessible form of the supreme for the seeker."],["Deussen","Smokeless flame — a recurring image in Upaniṣadic and Yogic texts (cf. Śvetāśvatara II.14 — the same image, perhaps borrowed)."],["Aurobindo","A smokeless flame in the inner chamber — the same eternal Person, yesterday, today, tomorrow, the unchanging Witness of all that changes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.14","title":"","sa":["यथोदकं दुर्गे वृष्टं पर्वतेषु विधावति । एवं धर्मान् पृथक् पश्यंस्तानेवानुविधावति"],"iast":["yathodakaṃ durge vṛṣṭaṃ parvateṣu vidhāvati | evaṃ dharmān pṛthak paśyaṃstānevānuvidhāvati"],"words":[["yathā udakaṃ durge vṛṣṭam parvateṣu vidhāvati","rel. + nom.sg.n. + loc.adj. + p.p.p. + loc.pl. + pres.3.sg.","as water rained on a rugged (mountain) runs about on the peaks"],["evaṃ dharmān pṛthak paśyan tān eva anu-vidhāvati","adv. + acc.pl. + adv. + pres.ptcp. + acc.dem. + emph. + pres.3.sg.","thus seeing the dharmas as separate, after them only he runs"]],"lit":"As water rained on a rugged (mountain) runs about over its peaks, so seeing the dharmas as separate, after them only does (the unwise one) run.","para":"The dispersive metaphor: water on mountain peaks runs everywhere wastefully. So the consciousness that sees only multiplicity dissipates itself among the many.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Dharmān = the various powers/qualities/objects. Pṛthak paśyan = seeing them separately. The image is of dispersal-and-waste — consciousness scattering itself across appearances."],["Rangarāmānuja","Seeing the various aspects of the world as disjoint from the Lord, one runs after each in turn. Bhakti is the gathering of all dharmas into the One."],["Madhva","the verse warns against treating dharmas as independent rather than as tāratamya-ordered under the Lord. Each is real; their hierarchy must not be lost."],["Sivānanda","Daily observation: when the mind sees disparity, it runs after each thing in turn — and so wastes itself. Train it to see unity; energy gathers."],["Deussen","The metaphor is striking. Deussen reads it as a Vedic-style upamā: the parallel between cosmic water-scatter and mental scatter is precisely drawn."],["Aurobindo","The mind that sees multiplicity scatters its energies among the many; like water rained on rough mountain tops, it flows everywhere but reaches nowhere."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.i.15","title":"","sa":["यथोदकं शुद्धे शुद्धमासिक्तं तादृगेव भवति । एवं मुनेर्विजानत आत्मा भवति गौतम"],"iast":["yathodakaṃ śuddhe śuddhamāsiktaṃ tādṛgeva bhavati | evaṃ munervijānata ātmā bhavati gautama"],"words":[["yathā udakaṃ śuddhe śuddham āsiktaṃ tādṛk eva bhavati","rel. + nom. + loc.adj. + acc.adj. + p.p.p. + adj. + emph. + pres.3.sg.","as pure water poured into pure (water) becomes just like that"],["evaṃ muneḥ vi-jānataḥ ātmā bhavati gautama","adv. + gen.sg.m. + gen.pres.ptcp. + nom. + pres.3.sg. + voc.","so, O Gautama, becomes the self of the discerning sage"]],"lit":"As pure water poured into pure (water) becomes just like that — so, O Gautama, becomes the (awakened) ātman of the discerning sage.","para":"The unity-image: the ātman of the muni merges with the ātman as pure water with pure water — without trace of difference. The verse closes the vallī with a striking simile of identity-realisation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Locus classicus for Advaita's ‘merging-into-Brahman’ image — though Śaṅkara is careful: it is not actual transformation but the realisation of pre-existing identity. The water was always one; the seer simply sees so."],["Rangarāmānuja","‘Tādṛk eva bhavati’ — becomes like that, not identical with that. The muni's ātman attains similitude to (sādṛśya with) the Lord, retaining its distinct identity. The Vaiśiṣṭādvaita reading is precise."],["Madhva","‘tādṛk eva’ = becomes of similar (not identical) nature. The jīva attains Viṣṇu-sādṛśya in mokṣa — the doctrine of sarūpya-mukti."],["Sivānanda","Whether read as identity (Advaita) or similitude (Vaiṣṇava), the experiential implication is the same: the seeker's individuality dissolves into the Real."],["Deussen","The image of pure-water-into-pure-water is one of the most evocative in Upaniṣadic literature. Schopenhauer cites it in his discussion of the Upaniṣads in Welt als Wille und Vorstellung."],["Aurobindo","The soul of the awakened one becomes one with the infinite Self as pure water poured into pure water — no longer separable, no longer distinguishable."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya II · Vallī ii","u":[{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.1","title":"","sa":["पुरमेकादशद्वारमजस्यावक्रचेतसः । अनुष्ठाय न शोचति विमुक्तश्च विमुच्यते । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["puramekādaśadvāramajasyāvakracetasaḥ | anuṣṭhāya na śocati vimuktaśca vimucyate | etadvai tat"],"words":[["puram ekādaśa-dvāram","acc.sg.n. + cpd.adj.","the city of eleven gates"],["ajasya avakra-cetasaḥ","gen.sg.m. + cpd.gen.","of the unborn, of unbent intellect"],["anu-ṣṭhāya na śocati","abs. + neg. + pres.3.sg.","having approached / meditated, (one) does not grieve"],["vi-muktaḥ ca vi-mucyate","adj.nom. + and + pres.3.sg.","and freed, (one) is (truly) freed"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"The city of eleven gates of the unborn, whose intellect is unbent — having drawn near (Him) one does not grieve; and freed (here), one is (truly) freed (hereafter). THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The body is the eleven-gated city: 7 in the head (eyes×2, ears×2, nostrils×2, mouth), plus navel, urethra, anus, and the crown-aperture (brahma-randhra). The unborn ātman is its lord. Knowing this confers jīvanmukti (freed-here, freed-hereafter).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Eleven gates: seven in the head + navel + two excretory + brahma-randhra. The ātman is the indweller. ‘Vimuktaś ca vimucyate’ = the locus classicus of jīvanmukti — freed in this body, freed at body-fall."],["Rangarāmānuja","Pura = the embodied complex. The Lord-as-antaryāmin is its true ruler. Bhakti-yoga produces jīvanmukti — present freedom that ripens into mokṣa."],["Madhva","eleven gates literally enumerated. The Lord pervades the body; the jīva experiences through the gates; freed from delusion, the jīva attains true freedom."],["Sivānanda","Watch the eleven gates: each is a doorway through which prāṇa and consciousness move. Yoga is the control of the gates and the recognition of the indweller."],["Deussen","Deussen treats this as one of the most lucid body-as-city metaphors. The same image occurs in Maitrī VI.28 and elsewhere; Kaṭha is the original locus."],["Aurobindo","The city of eleven gates is the human body; its lord is the unborn Spirit; knowing him the soul is delivered from grief, and freed even here, is finally freed."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.2","title":"","sa":["हंसः शुचिषद्वसुरन्तरिक्षसद् होता वेदिषदतिथिर्दुरोणसत् । नृषद्वरसदृतसद्व्योमसद् अब्जा गोजा ऋतजा अद्रिजा ऋतं बृहत्"],"iast":["haṃsaḥ śuciṣadvasurantarikṣasad hotā vediṣadatithirduroṇasat | nṛṣadvarasadṛtasadvyomasad abjā gojā ṛtajā adrijā ṛtaṃ bṛhat"],"words":[["haṃsaḥ śuci-ṣat","nom. + adj.","the Swan abiding in pure (heaven)"],["vasuḥ antarikṣa-sat","adj. + cpd.","the Vasu dwelling in the mid-air"],["hotā vedi-ṣat","nom. + cpd.","the Hotṛ at the altar"],["atithiḥ duroṇa-sat","nom. + cpd.","the guest in the house"],["nṛ-ṣat vara-sat ṛta-sat vyoma-sat","cpd.×4","in men, in the best, in ṛta, in space"],["abjāḥ gojāḥ ṛtajāḥ adrijāḥ","cpd.nom.pl.","born of waters, of earth, of ṛta, of mountains"],["ṛtaṃ bṛhat","nom.acc.n. + adj.","the great ṛta"]],"lit":"The Swan abiding in pure (heaven), the Vasu in the mid-air, the Hotṛ at the altar, the guest in the house; abiding in men, in the highest, in ṛta, in space; born of waters, of earth, of ṛta, of mountains — the great ṛta.","para":"The famous ‘haṃsa-mantra’ — Ṛgveda IV.40.5 in toto. The ātman as universal Swan, present in every locus, as every form of life. The Vedic verse is quoted entire as a cosmic identification of the ātman.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The verse is a direct quotation from Ṛgveda IV.40.5. Haṃsa = the moving-soul, ham-sa-ham. Each location-epithet identifies the ātman with a cosmic principle. The verse is a complete cosmic identification."],["Rangarāmānuja","Each epithet names the Lord in a different manifestational locus. The Lord pervades every domain — heaven, mid-air, altar, house, men, ṛta, space, waters, earth, mountains."],["Madhva","each name applies to Viṣṇu in His all-pervasive lordship. The Ṛgvedic quotation establishes Viṣṇu's omnipresence with Vedic authority."],["Sivānanda","Recite this mantra as a complete dhyāna. Each location-name expands the meditator's sense of the ātman's universality."],["Deussen","Ṛgveda IV.40.5 quoted in full. Deussen sees this as the Kaṭha's deliberate Vedic anchoring at a structural crescendo."],["Aurobindo","The Swan moves through all the cosmic abodes — heaven, sky, fire-altar, the human house, the inner heart, the high places, the Right itself — the eternal one in all."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.3","title":"","sa":["ऊर्ध्वं प्राणमुन्नयत्यपानं प्रत्यगस्यति । मध्ये वामनमासीनं विश्वे देवा उपासते"],"iast":["ūrdhvaṃ prāṇamunnayatyapānaṃ pratyagasyati | madhye vāmanamāsīnaṃ viśve devā upāsate"],"words":[["ūrdhvaṃ prāṇam un-nayati","adv. + acc. + pres.3.sg. of ud-√nī","upward he leads the prāṇa"],["apānaṃ pratyak asyati","acc. + adv. + pres.3.sg. of √as","the apāna he drives downward"],["madhye vāmanam āsīnam","adv. + acc.adj. + pres.ptcp.acc.","in the middle, sitting (the) dwarf-like"],["viśve devāḥ upāsate","adj.nom.pl. + nom.pl. + pres.3.pl.middle","all the gods worship"]],"lit":"Upward he leads the prāṇa; the apāna he drives downward; the ‘dwarf-like one’ sitting in the middle (heart-cave) — all the gods worship.","para":"The vital-airs and the heart-puruṣa. Prāṇa rises, apāna descends, and the vāmana (compact, small) sits between in the heart-centre — worshipped by all the cosmic functions (gods).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Vāmana = the dwarfish one, here = the heart-puruṣa, compactly seated. Viśve devāḥ = the senses-and-cosmic-faculties — all centred on the ātman in the heart."],["Rangarāmānuja","Vāmana echoes the Viṣṇu-avatāra; the Lord as the heart-indweller is worshipped by the indriya-devatās in their inward function."],["Madhva","Vāmana is unambiguous — Viṣṇu in His Vāmana-form indwells the heart, even of the most minute jīva. All devas, themselves jīvas, worship Him."],["Sivānanda","Trace the prāṇa upward, the apāna downward; rest awareness in the heart-centre between. The verse is a precise prāṇāyāma-dhyāna instruction."],["Deussen","Vāmana = ‘dwarf, small one’ — likely an early reference to the heart-purusha of thumb-size, here in synonym. Deussen reads as a parallel formulation."],["Aurobindo","The breath rises, the lower vital descends; in the centre sits the small one — the compact Lord whom all the divine powers worship."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.4","title":"","sa":["अस्य विस्रंसमानस्य शरीरस्थस्य देहिनः । देहाद्विमुच्यमानस्य किमत्र परिशिष्यते । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["asya visraṃsamānasya śarīrasthasya dehinaḥ | dehādvimucyamānasya kimatra pariśiṣyate | etadvai tat"],"words":[["asya vi-sraṃsamānasya śarīra-sthasya dehinaḥ","gen.dem. + pres.ptcp.gen. + cpd.gen. + gen.","of this (jīva) when it is being loosed (from the body), embodied in the body"],["dehāt vi-mucyamānasya","abl.sg. + pres.ptcp.gen.","being released from the body"],["kim atra pari-śiṣyate","interr. + adv. + pres.pass.3.sg.","what is left over here?"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"When this embodied one is being loosed (at death), being released from the body — what is left over here? THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"At the moment of death, what remains? — the ātman, which is THIS (the same one being taught). The verse confronts the death-question directly, recalling Naciketā's original third boon.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Vi-sraṃsamānasya = at the dis-jointing of the embodied. ‘Kim atra pariśiṣyate’ — what remains? — the ātman. The body falls, the ātman is. ‘Etat vai tat’ = the answer to the vicikitsā of I.i.20."],["Rangarāmānuja","At dehānta, the jīva separates with its sūkṣma-śarīra; the Lord retains it in His presence. ‘Etat vai tat’ names the inner reality that persists."],["Madhva","the jīva is what remains; it does not perish with the body. The Cārvāka position is refuted here directly."],["Sivānanda","Contemplate the moment of death. What goes? — the body. What stays? — that which sees the body go. The verse is an instruction in pratyak-vicāra under the most extreme test."],["Deussen","The verse answers Naciketā's foundational question. Deussen treats it as the structural climax of II.ii: the response to ‘is there or is there not?’."],["Aurobindo","When the body is falling away from the soul, what remains? — the Spirit that was always there, the one Person whom every dissolution leaves untouched."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.5","title":"","sa":["न प्राणेन नापानेन मर्त्यो जीवति कश्चन । इतरेण तु जीवन्ति यस्मिन्नेतावुपाश्रितौ"],"iast":["na prāṇena nāpānena martyo jīvati kaścana | itareṇa tu jīvanti yasminnetāvupāśritau"],"words":[["na prāṇena na apānena martyaḥ jīvati kaścana","neg. + instr. + neg. + instr. + nom. + pres.3.sg. + indef.","no mortal lives by prāṇa or apāna alone"],["itareṇa tu jīvanti yasmin etau upāśritau","instr.adj. + emph. + pres.3.pl. + loc.rel. + nom.dual + p.p.p.dual","but they live by the Other in whom these two are sheltered"]],"lit":"No mortal lives by prāṇa, nor by apāna; but they live by the Other (= ātman) in whom these two are sheltered.","para":"The vital-airs are not the principle of life; they are sheltered in something Other — the ātman. The verse decisively rejects the prāṇa-ātma-vāda (life-is-breath theory).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Itareṇa = by ‘the other’ = the ātman, distinct from both prāṇa-types. The verse refutes prāṇa-vāda — the doctrine identifying life with breath. The ātman is the supporting ground of the vital functions."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord-as-antaryāmin sustains the vital functions. Without Him, prāṇa is dead air. ‘Itareṇa’ = the Other, distinct from prāṇa-apāna and yet the ground of both."],["Madhva","prāṇa is dependent on jīva-with-ātman, not vice versa. The verse refutes the materialist reduction of life to breath."],["Sivānanda","Do not identify with the breath. The breath comes and goes; YOU remain. The Other in whom both breaths are anchored — that is the seeker's true centre."],["Deussen","Deussen sees this verse as Kaṭha's specific refutation of the prāṇa-cosmology of Praśna I and Ṛgveda X.121.2 (the breath as cosmic principle). The Kaṭha goes deeper."],["Aurobindo","The breaths come and go; not by them does the mortal live, but by the Self that holds them — the Spirit deeper than every breath."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.6","title":"","sa":["हन्त त इदं प्रवक्ष्यामि गुह्यं ब्रह्म सनातनम् । यथा च मरणं प्राप्य आत्मा भवति गौतम"],"iast":["hanta ta idaṃ pravakṣyāmi guhyaṃ brahma sanātanam | yathā ca maraṇaṃ prāpya ātmā bhavati gautama"],"words":[["hanta te idaṃ pravakṣyāmi guhyam brahma sanātanam","exclam. + dat.2.sg. + acc. + fut.1.sg. + acc. + acc. + adj.","well then, I shall declare to thee this secret, the eternal Brahman"],["yathā ca maraṇaṃ prāpya ātmā bhavati","rel. + and + acc. + abs. + nom. + pres.3.sg.","and how, having met death, the ātman becomes (i.e., what its fate is)"],["gautama","voc.","O Gautama (Naciketā)"]],"lit":"Now I shall declare to thee, O Gautama, this secret, the eternal Brahman — and how, on meeting death, the ātman fares.","para":"Yama announces the post-mortem doctrine — the destiny of the ātman after death. The transition verse to the doctrine of yoni and karma-determined rebirth.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘Yathā maraṇaṃ prāpya ātmā bhavati’ — how the ātman fares after death is the question. Yama, lord of death, now speaks ex officio. The teaching: ātman's post-mortem state is karma-determined re-embodiment or mokṣa."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord teaches through Yama the destiny of the jīva — birth-after-birth until prapatti, then mokṣa. The introduction is to the doctrine of saṃsṛti and its end."],["Madhva","the post-mortem doctrine is foundational to Vedānta. Yama, who oversees death, is the supreme authority here. The teaching unfolds the karma-determined rebirth doctrine."],["Sivānanda","The supreme question is now broached. After death, what becomes of you? The next verses deliver the doctrine."],["Deussen","Structural break-marker. Deussen reads ‘yathā maraṇam prāpya ātmā bhavati’ as the Upaniṣad's most direct address to its own foundational question."],["Aurobindo","The Master prepares the soul for the great secret: how the eternal Spirit fares when the body is left behind."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.7","title":"","sa":["योनिमन्ये प्रपद्यन्ते शरीरत्वाय देहिनः । स्थाणुमन्येऽनुसंयन्ति यथाकर्म यथाश्रुतम्"],"iast":["yonimanye prapadyante śarīratvāya dehinaḥ | sthāṇumanye'nusaṃyanti yathākarma yathāśrutam"],"words":[["yonim anye pra-padyante","acc.f. + nom.indef. + pres.3.pl. of pra-√pad","some go to (a new) womb"],["śarīratvāya dehinaḥ","dat.sg.n. + gen.pl.","for the (re-)embodiment of the embodied ones"],["sthāṇum anye anu-saṃyanti","acc.sg.m. + nom.indef. + pres.3.pl.","others enter into the immovable (= stones, plants)"],["yathā-karma yathā-śrutam","adv. + adv.","according to (their) karma, according to (their) learning"]],"lit":"Some (souls) go to a (new) womb for re-embodiment; others enter into the immovable (plant or stone forms) — according to (their) karma, according to (their) learning.","para":"The Upaniṣadic doctrine of trans-migration: post-death, the jīva enters either a womb or a sthāvara existence based on its karma and śruta (Vedic learning).","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Yathā-karma yathā-śrutam — both action and knowledge condition the re-embodiment. The verse is the canonical Upaniṣadic statement of karma-determined rebirth."],["Rangarāmānuja","The jīva's destiny is fixed by its accumulated puṇya-pāpa and its vidyā-level. The Lord, as karma-phala-dātṛ, oversees the apportionment."],["Madhva","re-embodiment in lower forms (sthāṇu = immobile, plant, stone) is real for those whose karma is heavily adharmic. The hierarchy of births is precisely karma-determined."],["Sivānanda","The doctrine is clear: action and learning together determine the next birth. Cultivate both rightly while embodied. Death is no annihilation — it is a transit point."],["Deussen","The verse is one of the earliest unambiguous Upaniṣadic statements of metempsychosis into plants/stones as well as wombs. Karma's reach is comprehensive."],["Aurobindo","Some pass into new wombs to take up the human play again; others fall into the stillness of the inanimate — each according to the soul's own work and self-knowing."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.8","title":"","sa":["य एष सुप्तेषु जागर्ति कामं कामं पुरुषो निर्मिमाणः । तदेव शुक्रं तद्।ह्ब्रह्म तदेवामृतमुच्यते । तस्मिंल्लोकाः श्रिताः सर्वे तदु नात्येति कश्चन । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["ya eṣa supteṣu jāgarti kāmaṃ kāmaṃ puruṣo nirmimāṇaḥ | tadeva śukraṃ tad|hbrahma tadevāmṛtamucyate | tasmiṃllokāḥ śritāḥ sarve tadu nātyeti kaścana | etadvai tat"],"words":[["yaḥ eṣaḥ supteṣu jāgarti","rel. + dem. + loc.pl. + pres.3.sg.","He who, when (others) sleep, is awake"],["kāmaṃ kāmaṃ puruṣaḥ nir-mimāṇaḥ","acc. + acc. (iter.) + nom. + pres.ptcp.","the Person, fashioning desire after desire"],["tat eva śukraṃ tat brahma","dem. + emph. + nom.sg.n. + dem. + nom.sg.n.","that very is the bright, that is Brahman"],["tat eva amṛtam ucyate","dem. + emph. + nom.sg.n. + pres.3.sg.pass.","that very is called the immortal"],["tasmin lokāḥ śritāḥ sarve","loc.dem. + nom.pl. + p.p.p. + adj.","on Him all the worlds rest"],["tat u na atyeti kaḥ cana","dem. + emph. + neg. + pres.3.sg. + indef.","beyond That none whatsoever passes"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"He who is awake while (others) sleep, the Person fashioning desire after desire — that very (is) the bright, that (is) Brahman, that very is called the immortal. On Him all the worlds rest; beyond Him none whatsoever passes. THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The dream-state proof: when the senses sleep, what fashions dream-objects? The puruṣa — the dream-fashioner — is itself the witness, the Brahman, the immortal. Locus classicus for taijasa-doctrine.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The dream-state is evidence: the inner self is awake even when the body sleeps and fashions kāmas (objects of desire) from its own light. This self-luminosity is the Brahman, the immortal. Taijasa-doctrine in early form."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord is the antaryāmin who remains awake in the jīva's sleep. The verse identifies the inner illuminator as the supreme Lord."],["Madhva","the dream-fashioner is the Lord, who supports the jīva's mental fabrication. The verse establishes the Lord's continuous active presence even in dream-sleep."],["Sivānanda","Reflect: who fashions the dreams? Not the sleeping body. The same self that fashions them is awake in the deepest sleep. This self is your true centre."],["Deussen","Deussen sees this verse as the precursor of Māṇḍūkya's taijasa-doctrine. The dream-evidence is one of the most original Upaniṣadic philosophical moves."],["Aurobindo","He who is awake when all sleep, fashioning desire after desire — that is the Spirit, the bright eternal in whom all the worlds rest; none transcends Him."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.9","title":"","sa":["अग्निर्यथैको भुवनं प्रविष्टो रूपं रूपं प्रतिरूपो बभूव । एकस्तथा सर्वभूतान्तरात्मा रूपं रूपं प्रतिरूपो बहिश्च"],"iast":["agniryathaiko bhuvanaṃ praviṣṭo rūpaṃ rūpaṃ pratirūpo babhūva | ekastathā sarvabhūtāntarātmā rūpaṃ rūpaṃ pratirūpo bahiśca"],"words":[["agniḥ yathā ekaḥ bhuvanaṃ praviṣṭaḥ","nom. + rel. + nom. + acc.sg.n. + p.p.p.","as the fire, one, having entered the world"],["rūpaṃ rūpaṃ prati-rūpaḥ babhūva","acc. (iter.) + adj.nom. + perf.3.sg.","became counter-formed to each form"],["ekaḥ tathā sarva-bhūta-antar-ātmā","nom. + adv. + cpd.nom.","so the One, the inner Self of all beings"],["rūpaṃ rūpaṃ prati-rūpaḥ bahiḥ ca","acc. (iter.) + adj.nom. + adv. + and","counter-formed to each form, and (yet) outside"]],"lit":"As the one fire, having entered the world, becomes counter-formed to each form, so the One, the inner Self of all beings, (becomes) counter-formed to each form, and (yet remains) outside (= beyond).","para":"The fire-image of unity-in-diversity: one fire takes many forms as fuel varies, yet remains itself. The ātman, one, manifests as many while remaining transcendent.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Fire takes the shape of whatever it burns yet remains itself. The ātman appears as each being's form yet is one — and bahiḥ (transcendent) even while immanent. Both vyāpti and parā-tva are affirmed."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord, like fire, takes each form yet retains His transcendence. The bhakta sees the Lord-in-every-form yet acknowledges the Lord-beyond-all-form."],["Madhva","the simile is exact — Viṣṇu pervades each form yet exceeds each. ‘Bahiś ca’ emphasises His categorical separateness from the forms He pervades."],["Sivānanda","The same Self in every form — yet beyond all forms. The image of fire taking the shape of every fuel yet remaining one is unforgettable."],["Deussen","Verses 9–11 form a triad: fire, air, sun — three cosmic-physical exemplars of unity-in-multiplicity. Vedic-style upamā-progression."],["Aurobindo","The one Fire takes the shape of each form yet stands alone; the one Self of all beings becomes the appearance of each yet abides beyond them all."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.10","title":"","sa":["वायुर्यथैको भुवनं प्रविष्टो रूपं रूपं प्रतिरूपो बभूव । एकस्तथा सर्वभूतान्तरात्मा रूपं रूपं प्रतिरूपो बहिश्च"],"iast":["vāyuryathaiko bhuvanaṃ praviṣṭo rūpaṃ rūpaṃ pratirūpo babhūva | ekastathā sarvabhūtāntarātmā rūpaṃ rūpaṃ pratirūpo bahiśca"],"words":[["vāyuḥ yathā ekaḥ bhuvanaṃ praviṣṭaḥ","nom. + rel. + nom. + acc. + p.p.p.","as the wind, one, having entered the world"],["rūpaṃ rūpaṃ prati-rūpaḥ babhūva","acc. (iter.) + adj.nom. + perf.3.sg.","became counter-formed to each form"],["ekaḥ tathā sarva-bhūta-antar-ātmā","nom. + adv. + cpd.nom.","so the One, the inner Self of all beings"],["rūpaṃ rūpaṃ prati-rūpaḥ bahiḥ ca","adj.nom. + adv. + and","counter-formed to each form, and (yet) outside"]],"lit":"As the one wind, having entered the world, becomes counter-formed to each form, so the One, the inner Self of all beings, counter-formed to each, and (yet) outside.","para":"The same image with vāyu instead of agni: wind blowing through various openings produces various sounds-and-shapes yet is one air. The ātman likewise.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Repetition with variation strengthens the doctrine. Wind in flutes, conches, lungs becomes diverse sounds; one wind. Likewise one ātman in many forms."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord as universal life-breath. Each form's animation is His; yet He is beyond."],["Madhva","The wind-image: Vāyu, who is one of Viṣṇu's foremost devatā-instruments, exemplifies the Lord's unity-in-diversity at the cosmic level."],["Sivānanda","Repetition is not redundancy: each simile (fire, wind, sun in next) drives the same truth from a different angle. Internalise all three."],["Deussen","The fire-wind-sun triad is a standard Upaniṣadic teaching device. Cf. Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka II.5 for a fuller exposition."],["Aurobindo","The one wind takes the form of every breath, every air; the one Self assumes the form of every soul, every life — yet stands beyond every form it takes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.11","title":"","sa":["सूर्यो यथा सर्वलोकस्य चक्षुः न लिप्यते चाक्षुषैर्बाह्यदोषैः । एकस्तथा सर्वभूतान्तरात्मा न लिप्यते लोकदुःखेन बाह्यः"],"iast":["sūryo yathā sarvalokasya cakṣuḥ na lipyate cākṣuṣairbāhyadoṣaiḥ | ekastathā sarvabhūtāntarātmā na lipyate lokaduḥkhena bāhyaḥ"],"words":[["sūryaḥ yathā sarva-lokasya cakṣuḥ","nom. + rel. + cpd.gen. + nom.sg.n.","as the sun, the eye of the whole world"],["na lipyate cākṣuṣaiḥ bāhya-doṣaiḥ","neg. + pres.3.sg.pass. + adj.instr.pl. + cpd.instr.pl.","is not stained by the (visual) faults outside (the seer)"],["ekaḥ tathā sarva-bhūta-antar-ātmā","nom. + adv. + cpd.nom.","so the One, the inner Self of all beings"],["na lipyate loka-duḥkhena bāhyaḥ","neg. + pres.3.sg.pass. + cpd.instr. + adj.nom.","is not stained by the world's sorrow, (being) outside (it)"]],"lit":"As the sun, the eye of the whole world, is not stained by the (visual) faults outside (the seer) — so the One, the inner Self of all beings, is not stained by the world's sorrow, being outside it.","para":"The sun-image: the sun illumines impurities (rotting carcasses, refuse) without itself being polluted. So the ātman illumines the entire suffering world without being touched by its suffering.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Locus classicus for the asaṅgatva-doctrine. The ātman is the sākṣin of all dukhas yet untouched by them. Cf. Gītā 13.31–32 ‘yathā sarvagataṃ saukṣmyād ākāśaṃ nopalipyate’."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord, witness of all suffering, is not stained. The bhakta who knows this is freed from the world's grief, though embodied."],["Madhva","Viṣṇu is doṣa-rahita absolutely. He witnesses world's suffering without Himself being touched. The verse establishes His categorical purity."],["Sivānanda","The sun-image is the simplest meditation on the ātman's non-attachment. Sit in the sun; reflect: it touches everything without becoming any of it. So is the ātman in you."],["Deussen","Quoted in Brahma-Sūtra-Bhāṣya by Śaṅkara as proof-text of ātman's asaṅgatā. The simile is also alluded to in Gītā 13.31."],["Aurobindo","The sun-eye of the world is not stained by what it lights; the inner Self lights all the sorrows of being and is itself untouched."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.12","title":"","sa":["एको वशी सर्वभूतान्तरात्मा एकं रूपं बहुधा यः करोति । तमात्मस्थं येऽनुपश्यन्ति धीराः तेषां सुखं शाश्वतं नेतरेषाम्"],"iast":["eko vaśī sarvabhūtāntarātmā ekaṃ rūpaṃ bahudhā yaḥ karoti | tamātmasthaṃ ye'nupaśyanti dhīrāḥ teṣāṃ sukhaṃ śāśvataṃ netareṣām"],"words":[["ekaḥ vaśī sarva-bhūta-antar-ātmā","nom. + adj. + cpd.nom.","one, the Controller, the inner Self of all beings"],["ekaṃ rūpaṃ bahudhā yaḥ karoti","nom. + nom. + adv. + rel. + pres.3.sg.","He who makes one form into many"],["tam ātma-stham ye anu-paśyanti dhīrāḥ","acc. + cpd.acc. + rel.pl. + pres.3.pl. + nom.pl.","those wise (ones) who behold Him in the (their own) Self"],["teṣāṃ sukhaṃ śāśvataṃ na itareṣām","gen.pl. + nom.sg.n. + adj. + neg. + adj.gen.","to them (is) eternal joy, not to others"]],"lit":"One, the Controller, the inner Self of all beings, who makes one (His) form into many — those wise (ones) who behold Him in their own Self, to them (belongs) eternal joy, not to others.","para":"The Lord as one-with-multiplicity, controller (vaśī), inner Self of every being. The wise see Him within themselves and attain eternal sukha. Others remain in transient pleasure.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Vaśī = the supreme controller. Ekaṃ rūpaṃ bahudhā = the one form multiplied appearingly. The inner darśana yields śāśvata-sukha — eternal joy, distinguished from the temporary pleasures of bhoga."],["Rangarāmānuja","Locus classicus for Vaiśiṣṭādvaita: the Lord is one, controller, inner Self of all. Ātma-stham — within the (jīva's) self as antaryāmin. Eternal joy is bhagavat-prāpti."],["Madhva","the verse names Viṣṇu's sovereignty (vaśī), His unity (eka), His indwelling (sarva-bhūta-antar-ātmā), and His manifestational multiplicity. Mokṣa = eternal joy in His proximity."],["Sivānanda","Eternal joy is the lakṣaṇa of mokṣa. Pleasure is fleeting; joy is constant. The distinction is decisive."],["Deussen","Quoted in Vivekacūḍāmaṇi 471 and elsewhere. One of the Kaṭha's most often-cited verses for both theistic and non-theistic Vedānta."],["Aurobindo","The One Lord makes the one Form into many; those who see Him in the inner Self drink the eternal delight — for others it is not."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.13","title":"","sa":["नित्योऽनित्यानां चेतनश्चेतनानाम् एको बहूनां यो विदधाति कामान् । तमात्मस्थं येऽनुपश्यन्ति धीराः तेषां शान्तिः शाश्वती नेतरेषाम्"],"iast":["nityo'nityānāṃ cetanaścetanānām eko bahūnāṃ yo vidadhāti kāmān | tamātmasthaṃ ye'nupaśyanti dhīrāḥ teṣāṃ śāntiḥ śāśvatī netareṣām"],"words":[["nityaḥ a-nityānāṃ cetanaḥ cetanānām","adj.nom. + adj.gen.pl. + adj.nom. + adj.gen.pl.","eternal among the non-eternals, conscious among the conscious"],["ekaḥ bahūnāṃ yaḥ vidadhāti kāmān","adj.nom. + adj.gen.pl. + rel. + pres.3.sg. + acc.pl.","one (of) many who dispenses the desires (of all)"],["tam ātma-stham ye anu-paśyanti dhīrāḥ","acc. + cpd.acc. + rel.pl. + pres.3.pl. + nom.pl.","those wise who behold Him in the (their) Self"],["teṣāṃ śāntiḥ śāśvatī na itareṣām","gen.pl. + nom.f. + adj.f. + neg. + adj.gen.","to them (is) eternal peace, not to others"]],"lit":"Eternal among the non-eternals, conscious among the conscious — One among many, who dispenses the desires (of all) — those wise who see Him in the Self, to them (belongs) eternal peace, not to others.","para":"The famous ‘eko bahūnāṃ vidadhāti kāmān’ — One among the many, fulfilling each one's desires. The Lord is qualitatively distinguished (nityo nityānām, cetanaś cetanānām) and yet immanent.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘Nityo nityānām’ — the eternal among the (relatively) eternal (= the gods, the cosmic principles). Śaṅkara: the supreme ātman is the genuinely nitya; others are upādhi-nitya."],["Rangarāmānuja","Vaiśiṣṭādvaita's foundational pramāṇa. ‘Nityo nityānām’ = the eternal among (the many real) eternals — both jīvas and Lord are eternal, but the Lord is the eternal of the eternals, the supreme of the supremes."],["Madhva","Locus classicus for Madhva's pañca-bheda. ‘Nityo nityānām, cetanaś cetanānām, eko bahūnām’ — the structure of reality is hierarchical: many real eternals, many real consciousnesses, but the Lord is qualitatively supreme."],["Sivānanda","Memorise this verse. It captures the Vedānta's ontology in three phrases: the eternal of the eternals, the conscious of the conscious, the one of the many who fulfils all."],["Deussen","The most often-quoted Kaṭha verse in Vaiṣṇava literature. Sūtra-vṛttis from Rāmānuja to Vallabha cite it as the foundation."],["Aurobindo","The eternal in all the eternals, the consciousness behind all consciousnesses, the One who supplies the desires of the many — the wise who find Him in themselves know everlasting peace."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.14","title":"","sa":["तदेतदिति मन्यन्तेऽनिर्देश्यं परमं सुखम् । कथं नु तद्विजानीयां किमु भाति विभाति वा"],"iast":["tadetaditi manyante'nirdeśyaṃ paramaṃ sukham | kathaṃ nu tadvijānīyāṃ kimu bhāti vibhāti vā"],"words":[["tat etat iti manyante a-nirdeśyaṃ paramaṃ sukham","dem. + dem. + quot. + pres.3.pl. + adj. + adj. + nom.sg.n.","this (is) that — they think the indescribable, supreme joy"],["kathaṃ nu tat vi-jānīyām","interr. + indecl. + dem. + opt.1.sg.","how then shall I know it?"],["kim u bhāti vi-bhāti vā","interr. + emph. + pres.3.sg. + pres.3.sg. + or","does it shine, or does it shine forth (variously)?"]],"lit":"‘This is That’ — (the knowers) think (so), of the indescribable supreme joy. How then can I know it? Does it (itself) shine, or shine forth (variously through other things)?","para":"Naciketā's culminating question (or the inner question of every seeker): how can the supreme be known? Does it self-illumine, or is it illumined? The answer comes in v. 15 — the most famous verse of the Upaniṣad.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘Bhāti vi-bhāti vā’ — does the supreme have its own light (svayaṃ-prakāśa), or is it illuminated by another (para-prakāśya)? The question sets up v. 15's decisive answer."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta's question: in what manner does the Lord disclose Himself? The next verse answers: by His own self-light, by which all else shines."],["Madhva","the question is whether the Lord is svayaṃ-prakāśa. The answer is yes — He alone is self-luminous; all other knowing depends on Him."],["Sivānanda","The seeker's perplexity expressed exactly. The supreme cannot be known as an object; yet without it, no knowing is possible. The next verse resolves the puzzle."],["Deussen","Question-and-answer structure. Deussen reads the rhetorical question as a transitional device to set up the celebrated v. 15."],["Aurobindo","The seeker stands before the inexpressible Joy: how shall he know it? does it shine with its own light, or is it shown by another? The answer comes."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.ii.15","title":"","sa":["न तत्र सूर्यो भाति न चन्द्रतारकं नेमा विद्युतो भान्ति कुतोऽयमग्निः । तमेव भान्तमनुभाति सर्वं तस्य भासा सर्वमिदं विभाति"],"iast":["na tatra sūryo bhāti na candratārakaṃ nemā vidyuto bhānti kuto'yamagniḥ | tameva bhāntamanubhāti sarvaṃ tasya bhāsā sarvamidaṃ vibhāti"],"words":[["na tatra sūryaḥ bhāti","neg. + adv. + nom. + pres.3.sg.","there the sun does not shine"],["na candra-tārakam","neg. + cpd.nom.sg.n.","nor moon-and-stars"],["na imā vidyutaḥ bhānti","neg. + nom.f.pl. + nom.pl.f. + pres.3.pl.","these lightnings do not shine"],["kutaḥ ayam agniḥ","interr.adv. + dem. + nom.","whence (then) this fire?"],["tam eva bhāntam anubhāti sarvam","acc.dem. + emph. + acc.pres.ptcp. + pres.3.sg. + nom.adj.","Him alone, shining, all (the world) shines after"],["tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vi-bhāti","gen.dem. + instr.sg.f. + adj. + dem. + pres.3.sg.","by His light all this (universe) shines forth"]],"lit":"There the sun shines not, nor moon and stars; nor do these lightnings shine — whence then this fire? Him alone, shining, all (the world) shines after; by His light all this (universe) shines forth.","para":"The most exalted verse in Kaṭha — the self-luminous Brahman that lights all other lights. Quoted verbatim in Muṇḍaka II.ii.10, Śvetāśvatara VI.14, and Bhagavad-Gītā 15.6.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The supreme is the source of all luminosity. Sun, moon, stars, lightning, fire — each shines because it borrows the supreme's light. This is the doctrine of svayaṃ-prakāśa Brahman. Locus classicus of the self-luminosity doctrine."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord is the universal illuminator. ‘Tam eva bhāntam anubhāti sarvam’ — by His light alone, all that shines, shines. Vaikuṇṭha needs no other light."],["Madhva","Viṣṇu is the sole svayaṃ-prakāśa. All other luminous bodies derive their light from Him. ‘Tasya bhāsā sarvam idaṃ vi-bhāti’ — by His light all this shines forth."],["Sivānanda","The verse to recite at sunrise and sunset. The light of the sun is not its own; it is the Lord's. Behind every visible light is the invisible Source."],["Deussen","Quoted verbatim in Muṇḍaka II.ii.10, Śvetāśvatara VI.14, Gītā 15.6. Deussen treats Kaṭha as the source; the verse is among the most-quoted in Sanskrit philosophical literature."],["Aurobindo","There the sun shines not, nor the stars, nor the lightnings, nor this fire of ours — by His light alone all these shine, by His effulgence all this world is lit."]],"notes":[]}]},{"t":"Adhyāya II · Vallī iii","u":[{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.1","title":"","sa":["ऊर्ध्वमूलोऽवाक्शाख एषोऽश्वत्थः सनातनः । तदेव शुक्रं तद्ब्रह्म तदेवामृतमुच्यते । तस्मिंल्लोकाः श्रिताः सर्वे तदु नात्येति कश्चन । एतद्वै तत्"],"iast":["ūrdhvamūlo'vākśākha eṣo'śvatthaḥ sanātanaḥ | tadeva śukraṃ tadbrahma tadevāmṛtamucyate | tasmiṃllokāḥ śritāḥ sarve tadu nātyeti kaścana | etadvai tat"],"words":[["ūrdhva-mūlaḥ avāk-śākhaḥ","cpd.nom. + cpd.nom.","root-above, branch-below"],["eṣaḥ aśvatthaḥ sanātanaḥ","dem. + nom.sg.m. + adj.","this Aśvattha, eternal"],["tat eva śukraṃ tat brahma","dem. + emph. + nom. + dem. + nom.","that very is the bright, that is Brahman"],["tat eva amṛtam ucyate","dem. + emph. + nom. + pres.3.sg.pass.","that very is called immortal"],["tasmin lokāḥ śritāḥ sarve","loc.dem. + nom.pl. + p.p.p. + adj.","on Him all the worlds rest"],["tat u na atyeti kaḥ cana","dem. + emph. + neg. + pres.3.sg. + indef.","beyond That none whatsoever passes"],["etat vai tat","dem. + emph. + dem.","THIS, verily, IS THAT"]],"lit":"Root-above, branches-below — this is the eternal Aśvattha. That very is the bright, that is Brahman, that very is called immortal. On Him all the worlds rest; beyond Him none passes. THIS, verily, IS THAT.","para":"The inverted aśvattha tree — root above, branches below — a Vedic-cosmological image of saṃsāra and Brahman. The root in Brahman (above), the branches in the world (below). Expanded in Bhagavad-Gītā 15.1–3.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Ūrdhva-mūlaḥ = with root upward — in Brahman. Avāk-śākhaḥ = branches downward — the world. The cosmic tree is rooted in the imperishable; its branches are the perishable names-and-forms. Locus classicus, expanded in Gītā 15."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord is the upward root; the cosmos is His branching manifestation. The bhakta must climb back to the root by śaraṇāgati."],["Madhva","Viṣṇu is the root above, śrī (Lakṣmī) and brahmā downward, then the whole saṃsāra-tree. The hierarchy is rigorously maintained."],["Sivānanda","The saṃsāra-tree is rooted in the immortal. Trace any branch upward; you arrive at Brahman. The yoga is precisely this upward tracing."],["Deussen","Quoted in Gītā 15.1 in expanded form. Deussen treats Kaṭha as the source. The inverted-tree symbol has Indo-European antecedents (Yggdrasil etc.)."],["Aurobindo","The tree of cosmic Existence is rooted above in the eternal and branches below into the universe of forms; that root is the bright Brahman, the supreme immortal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.2","title":"","sa":["यदिदं किं च जगत् सर्वं प्राण एजति निःसृतम् । महद्।ह्भयं वज्रमुद्यतं य एतद्विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्ति"],"iast":["yadidaṃ kiṃ ca jagat sarvaṃ prāṇa ejati niḥsṛtam | mahad|hbhayaṃ vajramudyataṃ ya etadviduramṛtāste bhavanti"],"words":[["yat idaṃ kiṃ ca jagat sarvam","rel.dem. + indef. + and + nom.sg.n. + adj.","whatever this whole world (is)"],["prāṇe ejati niḥ-sṛtam","loc.sg.m. + pres.3.sg. + p.p.p.","in the prāṇa it trembles, having issued forth"],["mahat-bhayam vajram udyatam","cpd.nom. + nom.sg.n. + p.p.p.","a great fear, an uplifted thunderbolt"],["yaḥ etat viduḥ a-mṛtāḥ te bhavanti","rel. + acc. + perf.3.pl. + adj.nom.pl. + dem. + pres.3.pl.","those who know this — immortal they become"]],"lit":"Whatever this whole world (is) — issuing forth, it trembles in (the supreme) Prāṇa: a great fear, an uplifted thunderbolt. Those who know this become immortal.","para":"The cosmos trembles in the supreme Prāṇa (= Brahman) as in a great fear; Brahman is like a vajra raised over all manifestation. To know this is to become immortal.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘Mahad-bhayaṃ vajram udyatam’ — a great fear, an uplifted thunderbolt. Brahman's cosmic law (the inviolability of dharma-niyama) keeps everything in its place. Trembling = the orderly trembling of cosmic functioning under that authority."],["Rangarāmānuja","The Lord's sovereignty is the great fear; under Him, the cosmos performs its functions. ‘Vajram udyatam’ = the Lord's will, inviolable."],["Madhva","under Viṣṇu's authority all cosmic functionaries operate. ‘Mahad-bhayam’ = the awesome unimpeachable command. Knowing this hierarchy yields mokṣa."],["Sivānanda","All cosmic activity is a trembling-in-the-Lord. The verse instils awe and reverence — the recognition of cosmic order as divine sovereignty."],["Deussen","‘Mahad-bhayam vajram udyatam’ recurs in Taittirīya II.8.1 in similar context. The fear-cosmology is a recurring Upaniṣadic motif."],["Aurobindo","All this universe trembles in the supreme Breath, like a thunder uplifted; those who know this become immortal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.3","title":"","sa":["भयादस्याग्निस्तपति भयात्तपति सूर्यः । भयादिन्द्रश्च वायुश्च मृत्युर्धावति पञ्चमः"],"iast":["bhayādasyāgnistapati bhayāttapati sūryaḥ | bhayādindraśca vāyuśca mṛtyurdhāvati pañcamaḥ"],"words":[["bhayāt asya agniḥ tapati","abl.dem. + nom. + pres.3.sg.","from fear of Him, fire burns"],["bhayāt tapati sūryaḥ","abl. + pres.3.sg. + nom.","from fear, the sun blazes"],["bhayāt indraḥ ca vāyuḥ ca","abl. + nom. + and + nom. + and","from fear, Indra and Vāyu"],["mṛtyuḥ dhāvati pañcamaḥ","nom. + pres.3.sg. + nom.ord.","Death runs (his) fifth (course)"]],"lit":"From fear of Him, fire burns; from fear (of Him), the sun blazes; from fear, Indra and Vāyu; Death runs (his course) as the fifth.","para":"The cosmic functionaries — fire, sun, Indra, Vāyu, and finally Death — each performs its duty under the great fear (= reverence) of the supreme. Even Death obeys.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> ‘Bhayāt’ = under the awe (= governance) of Brahman, each cosmic functionary discharges its task. The verse asserts Brahman's regulatory sovereignty over all cosmic forces."],["Rangarāmānuja","Each devatā functions under Lord's command. The verse establishes the Lord as cosmic antaryāmin even of fire, sun, Indra, Vāyu, Mṛtyu."],["Madhva","Viṣṇu is the controller of all five — including Yama Himself. Yama as a functionary acknowledges his subordination. The verse is decisive against any pan-theonic equality."],["Sivānanda","Even Death runs his course out of awe of the supreme. There is no escaping the cosmic order — and the order is the Lord's. Live with this reverence."],["Deussen","Quoted in Taittirīya II.8 with structural variants. The fear-cosmology is one of the most striking Upaniṣadic devices for asserting brahma-sovereignty."],["Aurobindo","In awe of the supreme the fire burns and the sun blazes, Indra and the Wind labour, and Death himself runs his appointed fifth course — all bound in obedience."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.4","title":"","sa":["इह चेदशकद्।ह्बोद्धुं प्राक्षरीरस्य विस्रसः । ततः सर्गेषु लोकेषु शरीरत्वाय कल्पते"],"iast":["iha cedaśakad|hboddhuṃ prākṣarīrasya visrasaḥ | tataḥ sargeṣu lokeṣu śarīratvāya kalpate"],"words":[["iha cet aśakat boddhuṃ","adv. + cond. + aor.3.sg. + inf.","if here he was able to know"],["prāk śarīrasya vi-srasaḥ","adv. + gen.sg. + gen.sg.f.","before the falling away of the body"],["tataḥ sargeṣu lokeṣu","adv. + loc.pl. + loc.pl.","then, in the manifest worlds"],["śarīratvāya kalpate","dat.sg.n. + pres.3.sg.middle","he is (re-)appointed for embodiment"]],"lit":"If here (in this life) he was able to know (Brahman) before the falling away of the body — (well, then he is freed). Otherwise, in the manifest worlds, he is (re-)appointed for embodiment.","para":"The doctrine of timely realisation: brahma-jñāna must be attained before death; otherwise, rebirth is mandatory. The window is the embodied lifespan.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Locus classicus for ‘this life or none’ — the urgency of brahma-jñāna. After body-fall, if jñāna was not attained, sargeṣu lokeṣu śarīratvāya kalpate — re-embodiment in the next manifestation cycle."],["Rangarāmānuja","Bhakti must reach maturity in this lifetime. Otherwise the jīva is re-clothed in body in subsequent sṛṣṭi. Each lifespan is the precious window."],["Madhva","jñāna's fruit is final mokṣa only if it ripens in this embodied life. Otherwise saṃsāra continues across kalpa-cycles."],["Sivānanda","Daily reminder: this life is the opportunity. Postponement is fatal — not in the sense of mortal death, but of indefinite continuation of saṃsāra."],["Deussen","‘Iha cet’ — the conditional has urgency-force. The verse functions as a memento mori of the most sober kind, applied to spiritual practice."],["Aurobindo","If here, before the body falls, the soul has known — then it is freed; otherwise it must return to the worlds for fresh embodiment."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.5","title":"","sa":["यथाऽऽदर्शे तथाऽऽत्मनि यथा स्वप्ने तथा पितृलोके । यथाऽप्सु परीव ददृशे तथा गन्धर्वलोके छायातपयोरिव ब्रह्मलोके"],"iast":["yathā''darśe tathā''tmani yathā svapne tathā pitṛloke | yathā'psu parīva dadṛśe tathā gandharvaloke chāyātapayoriva brahmaloke"],"words":[["yathā ādarśe tathā ātmani","adv. + loc.sg.m. + adv. + loc.","as in a mirror, so in oneself"],["yathā svapne tathā pitṛ-loke","adv. + loc.sg.m. + adv. + cpd.loc.","as in a dream, so in the pitṛ-world"],["yathā apsu pari iva dadṛśe","adv. + loc.pl. + adv. + indecl. + perf.passmiddle","as in waters (one) is seen as if (reflected)"],["tathā gandharva-loke","adv. + cpd.loc.","so in the gandharva-world"],["chāyā-ātapayoḥ iva brahma-loke","cpd.loc.dual + indecl. + cpd.loc.","as in shadow-and-sunshine, in the brahma-loka"]],"lit":"As in a mirror, so in oneself; as in a dream, so in the pitṛ-loka; as one is seen as if (reflected) in waters, so in the gandharva-loka; as in (the contrast of) shadow-and-sunshine, (so the supreme is seen) in the brahma-loka.","para":"Four graded clarities of brahma-darśana: mirror (clearest, in this body) &gt; dream (less clear, in pitṛ-loka) &gt; water-reflection (still less, in gandharva-loka) &gt; shadow-and-light (clearest of all, in brahma-loka). The doctrine of graduated clarity by attainment-level.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The four similes establish a hierarchy: clearest is the human body (mirror-clear), less clear after death in pitṛ-loka (dream-clear), still less in gandharva-loka (water-reflection), and finally in brahma-loka — clear as shadow-and-sunshine, i.e., totally distinct. The graded ascent."],["Rangarāmānuja","Each loka offers a different sādhana-medium. The Lord-darśana sharpens by stages until, in brahma-loka, the contrast is as clear as light-and-shadow."],["Madhva","the lokas are real, the gradations real, the visibility real. Brahma-loka is the clearest darśana-medium; human body, second clearest. Karma-determined trajectory."],["Sivānanda","Each level of being offers a different clarity of inner vision. Use this body — the clearest mirror — to see the Self now, not later."],["Deussen","Deussen reads the four similes as graded epistemology: mirror = direct; dream = imaginal; water = subliminal; shadow-and-sun = direct contrast. The epistemic gradation is striking."],["Aurobindo","Different worlds give different clearnesses of vision — the body's mirror, the realm of the fathers' dream, the waters of the gandharvas, the shadow-and-sun of Brahman's own world."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.6","title":"","sa":["इन्द्रियाणां पृथग्भावमुदयास्तमयौ च यत् । पृथगुत्पद्यमानानां मत्वा धीरो न शोचति"],"iast":["indriyāṇāṃ pṛthagbhāvamudayāstamayau ca yat | pṛthagutpadyamānānāṃ matvā dhīro na śocati"],"words":[["indriyāṇām pṛthak-bhāvam udaya-astamayau ca yat","gen.pl. + cpd.acc. + cpd.acc.dual + and + rel.","the separateness of the senses, their rising and setting"],["pṛthak ut-padyamānānām","adv. + pres.ptcp.gen.pl.","of those (senses) arising separately"],["matvā dhīraḥ na śocati","abs. + nom. + neg. + pres.3.sg.","having known, the wise one grieves not"]],"lit":"Of the senses — their separateness, their arising and setting, of those arising separately — having understood (this), the wise one grieves not.","para":"Recognising that the senses (indriyas) arise and set independently of the ātman is the freedom from grief. The ātman is the constant; the indriyas are sporadic functions.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Pṛthak-bhāvam = the separateness of the indriyas from the ātman. Udaya-astamayau = their rise and set (in waking, dream, sleep). The ātman is independent of them."],["Rangarāmānuja","The indriyas are real but distinct from the jīva, which is in turn distinct from the Lord. The verse confirms the hierarchical structure."],["Madhva","the indriyas have their own arising and ceasing; the jīva witnesses them; the Lord superintends both. The pañca-bheda within the individual."],["Sivānanda","The senses come and go; you remain. Watch this directly in meditation. Each thought, each sensation, has its dawn and its setting — but you, who watch, do not."],["Deussen","The verse anticipates Sāṃkhya's tattva-distinction between puruṣa and the indriyas — a doctrinal foundation in seed."],["Aurobindo","The senses have their separate births and deaths; knowing this distinction the wise is freed from sorrow over their comings and goings."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.7","title":"","sa":["इन्द्रियेभ्यः परं मनो मनसः सत्त्वमुत्तमम् । सत्त्वादधि महानात्मा महतोऽव्यक्तमुत्तमम्"],"iast":["indriyebhyaḥ paraṃ mano manasaḥ sattvamuttamam | sattvādadhi mahānātmā mahato'vyaktamuttamam"],"words":[["indriyebhyaḥ paraṃ manaḥ","abl.pl. + adj.nom. + nom.sg.n.","higher than the senses, the mind"],["manasaḥ sattvam uttamam","abl. + nom.sg.n. + adj.nom.","higher than the mind, sattva (= buddhi-sattva)"],["sattvāt adhi mahān ātmā","abl. + adv. + adj. + nom.","beyond sattva, the Great Self"],["mahataḥ a-vyaktam uttamam","abl. + adj.nom. + adj.","beyond the Great, the Un-manifest, highest"]],"lit":"Higher than the senses, the mind; higher than the mind, sattva (the intellect-essence); beyond sattva, the Great Self; beyond the Great, the Unmanifest, highest.","para":"The hierarchy of I.iii.10–11 restated and slightly extended. Sattva here = the pure-intellect-principle (= buddhi in its sattvic mode). The Sāṃkhya tattva-list is essentially present.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Sattva = buddhi-sattva, the discriminating principle. The hierarchy is the same as I.iii.10–11 with sattva for buddhi. The reiteration deepens the doctrine."],["Rangarāmānuja","Each level real, each higher real, the Lord above all. The bhakta climbs the ladder by progressive sublimation."],["Madhva","the hierarchy is ontologically real. The Lord transcends even the avyakta. The doctrine of tāratamya at the cosmic-individual structure."],["Sivānanda","Daily yoga: detach from sense; rest in mind; refine mind into sattva; thence to buddhi; thence to the avyakta; thence to the Person beyond. The ladder is to be climbed."],["Deussen","Repetition of I.iii.10–11 with sattva-substitution. Deussen sees this as evidence of two strata of redaction, or of intentional doctrinal reinforcement."],["Aurobindo","Above the senses is the mind; above the mind, the truth-stuff; above the truth-stuff, the great Self; above the Great, the supreme Unmanifest."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.8","title":"","sa":["अव्यक्तात्तु परः पुरुषो व्यापकोऽलिङ्ग एव च । यं ज्ञात्वा मुच्यते जन्तुरमृतत्वं च गच्छति"],"iast":["avyaktāttu paraḥ puruṣo vyāpako'liṅga eva ca | yaṃ jñātvā mucyate janturamṛtatvaṃ ca gacchati"],"words":[["a-vyaktāt tu paraḥ puruṣaḥ","abl. + emph. + adj.nom. + nom.","beyond the Un-manifest, however, the Person"],["vy-āpakaḥ a-liṅgaḥ eva ca","adj.nom. + adj.nom. + emph. + and","all-pervading, sign-less, indeed"],["yaṃ jñātvā mucyate jantuḥ","acc.rel. + abs. + pres.3.sg. + nom.","knowing whom, the creature is freed"],["amṛtatvaṃ ca gacchati","acc. + and + pres.3.sg.","and goes to immortality"]],"lit":"Beyond the Un-manifest, however, (is) the Person — all-pervading and sign-less. Knowing Him, the creature is freed, and goes to immortality.","para":"The supreme Puruṣa: vyāpaka (all-pervading), aliṅga (without distinguishing mark). To know Him is mokṣa and amṛtatva. The vallī's culminating doctrine.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Aliṅga = without sex/sign/mark = beyond the categories. Vyāpaka = omnipresent. Knowing this puruṣa = jñāna; the phala is mokṣa + amṛtatva. Mokṣa's lakṣaṇa here is doubly named."],["Rangarāmānuja","Vyāpaka = the Lord's omnipresence as antaryāmin. Aliṅga = beyond prakṛti's linga-bheda. Knowing through bhakti = mukti."],["Madhva","the puruṣa = Viṣṇu. Vyāpaka and aliṅga are His specific qualities. Knowledge of Him = release. The verse names the supreme by attributes."],["Sivānanda","All-pervading, sign-less — the supreme has no marks for the gross perception. Yet knowing Him is freedom. Subtle vision alone perceives."],["Deussen","‘Aliṅga’ has dual sense: without distinguishing characteristic, and without sex. Both apply: the supreme is beyond all empirical categories."],["Aurobindo","Beyond the unmanifest, the Person — pervading all, without sign, the supreme; knowing Him the soul is freed and reaches immortality."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.9","title":"","sa":["न संदृशे तिष्ठति रूपमस्य न चक्षुषा पश्यति कश्चनैनम् । हृदा मनीषा मनसाऽभिक्लृप्तो य एतद्विदुरमृतास्ते भवन्ति"],"iast":["na saṃdṛśe tiṣṭhati rūpamasya na cakṣuṣā paśyati kaścanainam | hṛdā manīṣā manasā'bhiklṛpto ya etadviduramṛtāste bhavanti"],"words":[["na sandṛśe tiṣṭhati rūpam asya","neg. + loc.sg.n. + pres.3.sg. + nom.sg.n. + gen.","not within (visual) range stands His form"],["na cakṣuṣā paśyati kaścana enam","neg. + instr.sg.n. + pres.3.sg. + indef. + acc.dem.","none with the eye sees Him"],["hṛdā manīṣā manasā abhiklṛptaḥ","instr.sg.n. + instr.sg.f. + instr.sg.n. + p.p.p.","grasped by heart, by understanding, by mind"],["yaḥ etat viduḥ a-mṛtāḥ te bhavanti","rel. + acc. + perf.3.pl. + adj.nom.pl. + dem. + pres.3.pl.","those who know this become immortal"]],"lit":"His form stands not within (visual) range; none sees Him with the eye. Grasped by the heart, by understanding, by mind — those who know this become immortal.","para":"The supreme is not visible by physical sight but is grasped by hṛd (heart-feeling), manīṣā (intellectual penetration), manas (mental concentration). Three-fold inner organ of perception.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Three internal organs: hṛd (heart-locus of vidyā), manīṣā (sharp prajñā), manas (concentrated attention). Their synthesis perceives the supreme. Direct sight cannot."],["Rangarāmānuja","Hṛd-manīṣā-manasa abhiklṛptaḥ — by the integrated inner instrument, the bhakta apprehends the Lord. Three faculties co-operating in bhakti-darśana."],["Madhva","the three internal organs each have their function in apperception of the Lord. The verse establishes the antaḥkaraṇa as the proper instrument."],["Sivānanda","Heart-feeling + intellectual clarity + concentrated mind — this triple synergy is the real instrument of brahma-jñāna. Cultivate all three."],["Deussen","‘Hṛdā manīṣā manasā abhiklṛptaḥ’ is one of the most precise epistemological statements in the Upaniṣads. Three distinct cognitive modes are named."],["Aurobindo","Not by sight is His form known; only by heart and luminous mind and intuition is He grasped; those who know this become immortal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.10","title":"","sa":["यदा पञ्चावतिष्ठन्ते ज्ञानानि मनसा सह । बुद्धिश्च न विचेष्टते तामाहुः परमां गतिम्"],"iast":["yadā pañcāvatiṣṭhante jñānāni manasā saha | buddhiśca na viceṣṭate tāmāhuḥ paramāṃ gatim"],"words":[["yadā pañca avatiṣṭhante jñānāni manasā saha","adv. + nom. + pres.3.pl. + nom.pl.n. + instr.sg.n. + adv.","when the five jñāna(-indriyas) stand still together with the mind"],["buddhiḥ ca na vi-ceṣṭate","nom. + and + neg. + pres.3.sg.","and the intellect does not stir"],["tām āhuḥ paramāṃ gatim","acc.f. + perf.3.pl. + acc.adj. + acc.f.","that they call the supreme goal"]],"lit":"When the five (sense-)knowings stand still together with the mind, and the intellect stirs not — that, they call the supreme goal.","para":"The first of two verses defining yoga: the five jñānendriyas plus manas plus buddhi all becoming still. This is the state of samādhi.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Five jñāna-indriyas + manas + buddhi = the antaḥ-karaṇa-system. Their complete cessation = samādhi = paramā gatiḥ. The locus classicus of yoga-definition."],["Rangarāmānuja","When the sevenfold sensorium ceases its movement, the Lord's antaryāmin-form is revealed. The bhakta's samādhi is this very stillness."],["Madhva","stillness here = full pratyāhāra. The bhakta in this state perceives Viṣṇu directly. The phala is paramā gatiḥ — Viṣṇu-loka."],["Sivānanda","Yoga begins where the sensorium ends. Cultivate the stilling. Each indriya stilled is a victory; all stilled together is the prize."],["Deussen","Read with v. 11, the two verses constitute Kaṭha's definition of yoga — earliest such Upaniṣadic formulation, antedating the Yoga-Sūtras by perhaps a millennium."],["Aurobindo","When the five knowings are still with the mind, and the discerning will moves not — that is what the seers call the supreme attainment."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.11","title":"","sa":["तां योगमिति मन्यन्ते स्थिरामिन्द्रियधारणाम् । अप्रमत्तस्तदा भवति योगो हि प्रभवाप्ययौ"],"iast":["tāṃ yogamiti manyante sthirāmindriyadhāraṇām | apramattastadā bhavati yogo hi prabhavāpyayau"],"words":[["tāṃ yogam iti manyante","acc.f. + acc.sg.m. + quot. + pres.3.pl.","that they think to be Yoga"],["sthirām indriya-dhāraṇām","adj.acc.f. + cpd.acc.f.","steady restraint of the senses"],["apramattaḥ tadā bhavati","adj.nom. + adv. + pres.3.sg.","then he becomes vigilant (= un-heedless)"],["yogaḥ hi prabhava-apyayau","nom. + emph. + cpd.nom.dual","for Yoga is (subject to) arising and disappearing"]],"lit":"That, they call Yoga — the steady restraint of the senses; then he becomes vigilant. For Yoga is (subject to) coming-into-being and passing-away.","para":"Yoga = sthira indriya-dhāraṇā (steady restraint of senses). The note of caution: yoga is prabhava-apyaya — it arises and lapses. The yogin must remain apramatta (vigilant), never settling into complacency.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Yoga's definition: sthirā indriya-dhāraṇā. Prabhava-apyayau = it can be gained and lost. The vigilance must be continuous; one's samādhi is not a permanent possession until jñāna-niṣṭhā stabilises it."],["Rangarāmānuja","Yoga as bhakti-niṣṭhā is similarly liable to fluctuation. Apramattaḥ — vigilance is essential through the path until siddhi."],["Madhva","yoga is a state-of-mind that arises and ceases until it ripens into mokṣa. The verse rightly warns against premature confidence."],["Sivānanda","Crucial warning: yoga is not a one-time achievement. It rises and falls. Daily practice, daily vigilance. Apramattatā — non-heedlessness — is the constant requirement."],["Deussen","‘Yoga = sthirā indriya-dhāraṇā’ — among the earliest definitions of yoga in Sanskrit literature. Patañjali's ‘cittavṛtti-nirodha’ is its logical descendant."],["Aurobindo","This Yoga is a steady restraint of the senses; then the seeker is unwavering. But Yoga has its rises and ebbs — it must be ceaselessly remade."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.12","title":"","sa":["नैव वाचा न मनसा प्राप्तुं शक्यो न चक्षुषा । अस्तीति ब्रुवतोऽन्यत्र कथं तदुपलभ्यते"],"iast":["naiva vācā na manasā prāptuṃ śakyo na cakṣuṣā | astīti bruvato'nyatra kathaṃ tadupalabhyate"],"words":[["na eva vācā na manasā prāptum śakyaḥ","neg. + emph. + instr.sg.f. + neg. + instr.sg.n. + inf. + gerundive","not by speech, not by mind, is (He) to be reached"],["na cakṣuṣā","neg. + instr.sg.n.","not by the eye"],["asti iti bruvataḥ anyatra kathaṃ tat upalabhyate","pres.3.sg. + quot. + gen.pres.ptcp. + adv. + interr. + dem. + pres.3.sg.pass.","of one saying \"He is\", otherwise how can He be apprehended?"]],"lit":"Not by speech, not by mind, not by the eye is He to be reached. Of him who says ‘He is’ — otherwise, how can He be apprehended?","para":"Crucial: the supreme is not reached by any pramāṇa other than the affirmation ‘He is’ (asti). The verse is the foundation of the asti-iti doctrine of brahma-recognition.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Asti iti bruvataḥ = of the one who says ‘He is’. Without the prior asti-vāda — the affirmation that He exists — no further apprehension is possible. Faith first, then investigation."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta begins from the affirmation. The Lord is grasped not by argument but by śraddhā-affirmation followed by experience."],["Madhva","the existence-affirmation (asti-iti) is the first jñāna-step. Without it, all sādhanā is rootless."],["Sivānanda","Begin with the simple affirmation: He IS. From that root, all spiritual sadhana grows. Without it, no progress."],["Deussen","‘Asti’ as the prior affirmation — Deussen sees in this the Vedānta's epistemic-theological starting point: existence asserted before nature investigated."],["Aurobindo","Not by word, mind, or sight is the supreme grasped; only by him who says ‘He is’ — to such alone does He become known."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.13","title":"","sa":["अस्तीत्येवोपलब्धव्यस्तत्त्वभावेन चोभयोः । अस्तीत्येवोपलब्धस्य तत्त्वभावः प्रसीदति"],"iast":["astītyevopalabdhavyastattvabhāvena cobhayoḥ | astītyevopalabdhasya tattvabhāvaḥ prasīdati"],"words":[["asti iti eva upalabdhavyaḥ","pres.3.sg. + quot. + emph. + gerundive","as \"He is\" (and only thus) is He to be apprehended"],["tattva-bhāvena ca ubhayoḥ","cpd.instr. + and + gen.dual","and as His real nature, of both (modes)"],["asti iti eva upalabdhasya","pres.3.sg. + quot. + emph. + p.p.p.gen.","of him who has thus apprehended Him as \"is\""],["tattva-bhāvaḥ pra-sīdati","cpd.nom. + pres.3.sg.","the real-nature (of Him) (then) becomes clear"]],"lit":"As ‘He is’ (alone) is He to be apprehended, and as His real-nature — of both (modes). To him who has apprehended Him as ‘is’, His real nature (then) becomes clear.","para":"Two-stage epistemology: first apprehend simply that He is (asti); then His tattva-svarūpa becomes clear. Existence-affirmation precedes essence-apprehension. The asti-iti-upalabdhi is the gateway.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Tattva-bhāvaḥ = the real essence. First grasp existence; then the essence-form becomes clear. The two-step approach is the Vedantic epistemology in essence."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta first affirms the Lord's existence; then the Lord, pleased, reveals His tattva. The asti-bhāva is the door of bhakti-vidyā."],["Madhva","the verse is the locus classicus of the existence-essence distinction in Vedānta epistemology. Existence first; essence second; mokṣa is the convergence."],["Sivānanda","First be sure that He IS. Then explore what He IS. The two-stage method works in every investigation, divine or mundane."],["Deussen","Deussen sees in vv. 12–13 the explicit two-stage method that became standard in later Vedānta. The asti-iti-upalabdhi as prajñā-pūrva-condition."],["Aurobindo","First the soul affirms that He is — then His true being shines forth. The supreme reveals itself to the one who has first believed in its existence."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.14","title":"","sa":["यदा सर्वे प्रमुच्यन्ते कामा येऽस्य हृदि श्रिताः । अथ मर्त्योऽमृतो भवत्यत्र ब्रह्म समश्नुते"],"iast":["yadā sarve pramucyante kāmā ye'sya hṛdi śritāḥ | atha martyo'mṛto bhavatyatra brahma samaśnute"],"words":[["yadā sarve pra-mucyante kāmāḥ","adv. + adj.nom.pl. + pres.3.pl. + nom.pl.","when all the desires are released"],["ye asya hṛdi śritāḥ","rel.pl. + gen.dem. + loc.sg.n. + p.p.p.pl.","which are lodged in his heart"],["atha martyaḥ a-mṛtaḥ bhavati","adv. + nom. + adj.nom. + pres.3.sg.","then the mortal becomes immortal"],["atra brahma sam-aśnute","adv. + acc. + pres.3.sg.middle","here itself he attains Brahman"]],"lit":"When all the desires that lodge in his heart are released, then the mortal becomes immortal; here itself he attains Brahman.","para":"The jīvanmukti formula: when all kāmas in the heart are dissolved, mortal becomes immortal atra eva — here itself, in this very body. The locus classicus of present-life liberation.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Atra brahma sam-aśnute — here, in this very life, he attains Brahman. The verse is Śaṅkara's pramāṇa-vākya for jīvanmukti. All kāmas released = the heart freed of kāma-vāsanās; the mortal-body remains, but the consciousness is now Brahman."],["Rangarāmānuja","Bhakti dissolves the worldly kāmas; only the bhāgavata-kāma remains. ‘Atra brahma sam-aśnute’ = even here, the bhakta tastes the Lord — preliminary to full mukti."],["Madhva","when all kāmas are dropped, the jīva becomes ‘immortal’ in a relative sense (saṃsāra-mukta). Full immortality is post-mortem in Viṣṇu-loka."],["Sivānanda","‘Atra brahma sam-aśnute’ — the locus classicus of jīvanmukti. Here, now, in this body, in this life. The promise is exact: when desires fall away, immortality arrives."],["Deussen","Deussen sees this verse as the Upaniṣadic doctrine of present-life liberation, differentiated from the more common doctrine of post-mortem attainment."],["Aurobindo","When all the desires that lodge in the heart are loosed, the mortal becomes immortal; even here, in this body, he tastes the Eternal."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.15","title":"","sa":["यदा सर्वे प्रभिद्यन्ते हृदयस्येह ग्रन्थयः । अथ मर्त्योऽमृतो भवत्येतावद्ध्यनुशासनम्"],"iast":["yadā sarve prabhidyante hṛdayasyeha granthayaḥ | atha martyo'mṛto bhavatyetāvaddhyanuśāsanam"],"words":[["yadā sarve pra-bhidyante hṛdayasya iha granthayaḥ","adv. + adj. + pres.3.pl. + gen.sg.n. + adv. + nom.pl.","when all the knots of the heart here are cut asunder"],["atha martyaḥ a-mṛtaḥ bhavati","adv. + nom. + adj. + pres.3.sg.","then the mortal becomes immortal"],["etāvat hi anuśāsanam","adj.nom. + emph. + nom.sg.n.","this much, indeed, is the teaching"]],"lit":"When all the knots of the heart here are cut asunder, then the mortal becomes immortal — this much, indeed, is the teaching.","para":"The hṛdaya-granthi doctrine: there are knots in the heart (often enumerated as three — avidyā, kāma, karma) which, when severed, yield immortality. The final reduction of the Upaniṣad's teaching: ‘etāvad hi anuśāsanam.’","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Hṛdaya-granthayaḥ = the knots of the heart = avidyā-kāma-karma three-fold. ‘Etāvad hi anuśāsanam’ — this much is the entire teaching. The Upaniṣad declares its own conclusion."],["Rangarāmānuja","The knots = ego-ties to the world. Bhakti cuts them. The teaching's essence is the release into the Lord's grace."],["Madhva","the granthayaḥ are real saṃskāra-ties. Their dissolution = mukti-yogyatā. ‘Etāvad hi’ — this is the sum and substance of the Upaniṣad."],["Sivānanda","Three knots in the heart: ignorance, desire, action. Cut all three; the prison dissolves. ‘This much is the teaching’ — the whole of Vedānta in one sentence."],["Deussen","‘Hṛdaya-granthi-bheda’ becomes a stock phrase in later Vedānta. Cf. Muṇḍaka II.ii.8: ‘bhidyate hṛdaya-granthiḥ...’. The Kaṭha and Muṇḍaka here are mutually citing."],["Aurobindo","When all the knots of the heart are loosened, the mortal becomes immortal — and that alone is the whole of the teaching."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.16","title":"","sa":["शतं चैका च हृदयस्य नाड्य स्तासां मूर्धानमभिनिःसृतैका । तयोर्ध्वमायन्नमृतत्वमेति विष्वङ्ङन्या उत्क्रमणे भवन्ति"],"iast":["śataṃ caikā ca hṛdayasya nāḍya stāsāṃ mūrdhānamabhiniḥsṛtaikā | tayordhvamāyannamṛtatvameti viṣvaṅṅanyā utkramaṇe bhavanti"],"words":[["śataṃ ca ekā ca hṛdayasya nāḍyaḥ","nom. + and + nom. + and + gen.sg.n. + nom.pl.f.","a hundred and one are the nāḍīs of the heart"],["tāsām mūrdhānam abhi-niḥ-sṛtā ekā","gen.pl. + acc.sg. + p.p.p.f. + nom.","of these, one passes upward through the crown"],["tayā ūrdhvam āyan a-mṛtatvam eti","instr.f. + adv. + pres.ptcp. + acc. + pres.3.sg.","by that, going upward, one attains immortality"],["viṣvaṅ anyāḥ ut-kramaṇe bhavanti","adv. + nom.pl.f. + loc.cpd. + pres.3.pl.","the others (lead) various directions, at the departure (= at death)"]],"lit":"A hundred and one are the nāḍīs of the heart; of these, one passes upward through the crown. By that, going upward, one attains immortality; the others are (for departure) in various directions.","para":"The 101 nāḍī doctrine: the heart has 101 channels; one — the suṣumnā — exits through the crown (brahma-randhra) and leads to mokṣa; the others lead to various lokas. Cited in Praśna III.6, Chāndogya VIII.6.6, and Maitrī VI.21.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> The 101st nāḍī = suṣumnā. The yogin who exits via suṣumnā at death attains krama-mukti (gradual liberation via brahma-loka). The other 100 lead to various rebirths."],["Rangarāmānuja","Suṣumnā-prasthāna = the path of bhakti-yogins. The Lord conducts the bhakta along the suṣumnā-track upward to His abode. The other tracks lead to lesser destinations."],["Madhva","the 101 nāḍīs are real and the doctrine of utkrānti (final departure) specifies the exit-route. Suṣumnā exit = Viṣṇu-loka."],["Sivānanda","The yogin trains the prāṇa to ascend the suṣumnā in life, so that at death the path is familiar. The kuṇḍalinī-yoga has this verse as one foundation."],["Deussen","The 101-nāḍī doctrine is in Chāndogya VIII.6.6, Praśna III.6, Maitrī VI.21. Kaṭha's formulation is among the most concise."],["Aurobindo","A hundred and one are the channels of the heart; one of them leads upward through the crown — by that one ascending, the soul touches immortality; the others lead elsewhere."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.17","title":"","sa":["अङ्गुष्ठमात्रः पुरुषोऽन्तरात्मा सदा जनानां हृदये संनिविष्टः । तं स्वाच्छरीरात्प्रवृहेन्मुञ्जादिवेषीकां धैर्येण । तं विद्याच्छुक्रममृतं तं विद्याच्छुक्रममृतमिति"],"iast":["aṅguṣṭhamātraḥ puruṣo'ntarātmā sadā janānāṃ hṛdaye saṃniviṣṭaḥ | taṃ svāccharīrātpravṛhenmuñjādiveṣīkāṃ dhairyeṇa | taṃ vidyācchukramamṛtaṃ taṃ vidyācchukramamṛtamiti"],"words":[["aṅguṣṭha-mātraḥ puruṣaḥ antar-ātmā","cpd.nom. + nom. + cpd.nom.","thumb-sized is the puruṣa, the inner Self"],["sadā janānāṃ hṛdaye saṃ-niviṣṭaḥ","adv. + gen.pl. + loc. + p.p.p.","ever in the heart of mankind, set within"],["taṃ sva-āt śarīrāt pra-vṛhet mūñjāt iva iṣīkām dhairyeṇa","acc. + cpd.abl. + opt.3.sg. of pra-√vṛh + abl. + indecl. + acc.f. + instr.","Him from one's own body let one draw out, as a stalk from the muñja-reed, with patience"],["taṃ vidyāt śukram a-mṛtaṃ","acc. + opt.3.sg. + acc. + acc.","let him know Him as the bright, the immortal"],["taṃ vidyāt śukram a-mṛtam iti","acc. + opt. + acc. + acc. + quot.","let him know Him as the bright, the immortal — so (it is said)"]],"lit":"Thumb-sized is the puruṣa, the inner Self — ever seated in the heart of mankind. Him from one's own body let one draw out, as a stalk from the muñja-reed, with patience. Let him know Him as the bright, the immortal — let him know Him as the bright, the immortal — so (it is said).","para":"The closing instruction. The aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa (recurring from II.i.12) is to be ‘drawn forth’ from the body as one separates a fine inner stalk from the surrounding muñja-grass. Patience (dhairya) is the method. The doubled phrase marks the formal end.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Pra-vṛhet = let one extract. The muñja-iṣīkā simile: a fine inner stalk drawn out from the coarse outer reed. So the ātman is to be discerned from its bodily-mental sheathing. Dhairyeṇa = with calm patience. The verse seals the entire teaching."],["Rangarāmānuja","The bhakta separates his ātman from the body-identification by sustained discrimination and bhakti-saṃyama. The muñja-iṣīkā image is repeated for emphasis on dhairya."],["Madhva","the jīva is to be discerned as distinct from the gross-and-subtle bodies. The muñja-iṣīkā simile is precise — what is extracted is not what surrounded."],["Sivānanda","The closing dhyāna instruction. Daily, with patience (dhairyeṇa), extract awareness of the inner from the outer sheaths. ‘Know Him as bright, as immortal’ — let this be the meditator's final word."],["Deussen","Muñja-iṣīkā image recurs in Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka II.4.13 and Maitrī VI.38 with same import. A pan-Upaniṣadic teaching-image."],["Aurobindo","Patient extraction of the inner Person from the outer sheath — as one draws the slender stalk from the rough reed. Know that Person as the bright, the immortal. So is the supreme teaching sealed."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Mantra II.iii.18","title":"","sa":["मृत्युप्रोक्तां नचिकेतोऽथ लब्ध्वा विद्यामेतां योगविधिं च कृत्स्नम् । ब्रह्मप्राप्तो विरजोऽभूद्विमृत्यु रन्योऽप्येवं यो विदध्यात्ममेव"],"iast":["mṛtyuproktāṃ naciketo'tha labdhvā vidyāmetāṃ yogavidhiṃ ca kṛtsnam | brahmaprāpto virajo'bhūdvimṛtyu ranyo'pyevaṃ yo vidadhyātmameva"],"words":[["mṛtyu-proktāṃ naciketaḥ atha labdhvā","cpd.acc.f. + voc.proper + adv. + abs.","having gained (the vidyā) taught by Death, O Naciketā"],["vidyām etām yoga-vidhim ca kṛtsnam","acc.f. + acc.dem.f. + cpd.acc. + and + acc.adj.","this vidyā and the entire yoga-method"],["brahma-prāptaḥ vi-rajaḥ a-bhūt vi-mṛtyuḥ","cpd.nom. + adj.nom. + aor.3.sg. + adj.nom.","Brahman-attained, dustless, deathless he became"],["anyaḥ api evaṃ yaḥ vi-dadhyāt ātmam eva","adj.nom. + emph. + adv. + rel. + opt.3.sg. + acc. + emph.","any other (man) too who would so apply (this) — to himself, indeed"]],"lit":"Having gained the vidyā taught by Death, and the entire yoga-method, O Naciketā, (the boy) became Brahman-attained, dust-free, deathless. So, too, any other who would (likewise) apply (this teaching) to himself.","para":"The Upaniṣad's closing phala-śruti: Naciketā himself attained Brahman, became dustless (virajaḥ — free of rajas, of impurity), deathless. And so will any other practitioner. The text ends with universal application.","coms":[["Śaṅkara","<em>(Gambhīrānanda)</em> Vi-rajaḥ = dust-free, impurity-free. Vi-mṛtyuḥ = beyond death. Brahma-prāptaḥ = Brahman-attained. The verse is the final phala-vacana, applying to every adhikāri who follows the teaching."],["Rangarāmānuja","Naciketā became Brahman-prāpta = attained the Lord. The verse extends the promise to any bhakta who follows similarly. ‘Ātmam eva’ — the realisation applies to oneself."],["Madhva","Naciketā's attainment is paradigmatic. ‘Anyo api’ — every qualified seeker, following the same path, attains the same fruit. Universalisation of the teaching."],["Sivānanda","The Upaniṣad ends with universal promise. What Naciketā attained, every sincere seeker can attain. The path is open. ‘Ātmam eva’ — apply it to yourself."],["Deussen","Standard Upaniṣadic phala-śruti closing. Deussen notes the universalisation as a characteristic feature — the teaching is not exclusive but extends to every adhikārin."],["Aurobindo","Naciketā, having received the supreme knowledge and the complete Yoga from the lips of Death, became one with the Eternal — dustless, deathless. And so does every soul that follows the same path in itself."]],"notes":[]},{"ref":"Visual Representations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Eight diagrams of the Kaṭha's geometry. Each diagram is referenced to the verses that establish it. Together they map the Upaniṣad's architecture from the outer narrative frame inward to the heart-cave."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"1. The dialogue architecture","title":"three boons, three nights, three doctrines","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(I.i.9 — three nights → three boons; I.i.10–29 unfolds them.)","<pre>\n        Naciketā waits 3 nights unfed at Yama&#x27;s threshold\n                          │\n                          ▼\n        Yama returns → 3 boons offered (I.i.9)\n        ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n        BOON 1 (I.i.10–11)         BOON 2 (I.i.13–19)         BOON 3 (I.i.20–29)\n        Father&#x27;s peace             The heaven-fire             What survives death?\n        (śānti for Gautama)        (Nāciketa-agni)             (brahma-vidyā)\n            ↓                          ↓                            ↓\n        Resolves the                Karma-mārga                  Jñāna-mārga\n        outer human bond            attainment of svarga         attainment of mokṣa\n            ↓                          ↓                            ↓\n        Granted (I.i.11)            Granted + bonus              Yama tries to deflect\n                                    (I.i.15–19)                  (I.i.21, 23–25);\n                                                                 Naciketā refuses substitution\n                                                                 (I.i.26–29)\n                                                                       ↓\n                                                              Teaching begins (I.ii.1)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"2. The two paths","title":"śreyas and preyas","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(I.ii.1–2 — the foundational discrimination.)","<pre>\n                          MAN  ⟷  (śreyas ∦ preyas, two paths approach)\n                            │\n                ┌───────────┴───────────┐\n                ▼                       ▼\n            ŚREYAS                   PREYAS\n            (the preferable          (the pleasant\n             good)                    good)\n                │                       │\n            chosen by the           chosen by the\n            DHĪRA (wise)            MANDA (dull)\n                │                       │\n            sādhu bhavati           hīyate arthāt\n            (well goes              (falls from\n             with him)               the true goal)\n                │                       │\n                ▼                       ▼\n            ↑ towards mokṣa         ↓ towards saṃsāra\n            (vidyā, I.ii.4)         (avidyā, I.ii.5–6)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"3. The chariot allegory (ratha-rūpaka)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(I.iii.3–9 — the most famous Upaniṣadic image.)","<pre>\n                              ⟵──── path (adhvan) ────⟶\n                              │                      │\n                          ┌───┴──────────────────────┴───┐\n                          │      ━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━ │      ⇒  PARAM PADAM VIṢṆOḤ\n                          │     │  (i)   (ii)  (iii)   │ │         (I.iii.9 — supreme abode)\n       ⟵  Pasture           │      Indriyas (horses)   │ │              ↑\n       (viṣayas)           │     ───────────────       │ │              │\n                          │       Manas (reins) ⟵━━━━━│ │              │\n                          │      ─────────────         │ │              │\n                          │       Buddhi (sārathi)     │ │\n                          │      ──────────────        │ │\n                          │      Ātman (rathin)        │ │\n                          └──────────────────────────────┘\n                                  Body (ratha)\n\n        Failure case (v.5):                     Success case (vv.6, 9):\n        ─────────────────                      ─────────────────────\n        No vijñāna + unyoked manas              vijñāna + yoked manas\n                ↓                                       ↓\n        wild horses (duṣṭa-aśvāḥ)               trained horses (sat-aśvāḥ)\n                ↓                                       ↓\n        runs to saṃsāra (v.7)                  runs to paramaṃ padam (vv.8–9)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"4. The graded hierarchy of tattvas","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(I.iii.10–11; restated II.iii.7–8 — ascending refinement.)","<pre>\n                                  PURUṢA  ←  supreme; kāṣṭhā, parā gatiḥ\n                                    │           (no higher; I.iii.11)\n                                AVYAKTA\n                                    │\n                              MAHĀN ĀTMĀ    ←  cosmic Mahat (Hiraṇyagarbha)\n                                    │\n                                  BUDDHI    ←  discriminative intellect\n                                  /sattva\n                                    │\n                                  MANAS     ←  unifying mind\n                                    │\n                                  ARTHA     ←  sense-objects\n                                    │\n                                  INDRIYA   ←  sense-organs\n                                    │\n                                  ŚARĪRA    ←  body (chariot)\n\n       The yoga (I.iii.13) is the upward withdrawal:\n       vāk-manas → buddhi (jñāna-ātman) → mahat-ātman → śānta-ātman (puruṣa)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"5. The eleven-gated city (puram ekādaśa-dvāram)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(II.ii.1 — the body of the unborn ātman.)","<pre>\n                            ┌─────────────────┐\n                            │   [11] crown    │  ←  brahma-randhra (suṣumnā exit)\n                            │     ╱─ ─ ─╲     │       cf. II.iii.16\n                            │  [1] [2]    │  │  ←  eyes (2)\n                            │   [3] [4]   │  │  ←  ears (2)\n                            │    [5][6]   │  │  ←  nostrils (2)\n                            │     [7]     │  │  ←  mouth\n                            │             │  │\n                            │    HEART  ★ │  │  ←  aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa (II.i.12)\n                            │             │  │       in the cave (guhā)\n                            │             │  │\n                            │     [8]     │  │  ←  navel\n                            │     [9]     │  │  ←  urethra (= prajanana-dvāra)\n                            │    [10]     │  │  ←  anus (= gudā)\n                            └─────────────────┘\n\n       Eleven gates = 7 (head) + navel + 2 excretory + brahma-randhra\n       The unborn ātman is the city&#x27;s true lord; knowing him → jīvanmukti\n       (vimuktaś ca vimucyate — freed-here, freed-hereafter)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"6. The hundred-and-one nāḍīs of the heart","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(II.iii.16 — the post-mortem exit-route map.)","<pre>\n                                  brahma-randhra (crown)\n                                       ▲\n                                       │  ↑  the 101st = SUṢUMNĀ\n                                       │      → leads upward → amṛtatva\n                                       │         (krama-mukti)\n                                       │\n                          ◢━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━◣\n                          ┃     HEART (hṛdaya)     ┃\n                          ┃  ●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●  ┃\n                          ┃  ●●●●  ★(suṣumnā mūla) ┃     ←  the 101 nāḍīs\n                          ┃  ●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●  ┃         radiate from here\n                          ┃  ●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●●  ┃\n                          ◤━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━◥\n                          ↓ ↓ ↓ ↙ ↘ → ↗  ↖ ↑ ←   (the other 100)\n                          go in various directions\n                          → various lokas, various rebirths</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"7. The inverted Aśvattha (ūrdhva-mūla)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(II.iii.1 — expanded in Bhagavad-Gītā 15.1–3.)","<pre>\n                            ROOT (Brahman, the immortal)\n                            ⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗⟗\n                                    │\n                                    │ trunk\n                              ╱┃╲\n                             ╱ ┃ ╲\n                            ╱  ┃  ╲\n                           ╱   ┃   ╲\n                          ╱    ┃    ╲\n                         ╱  branches  ╲  ←  the cosmos\n                        ╱     │   │    ╲     (names-and-forms,\n                       ╱    leaves        ╲   the worlds)\n                      ╱   ↓  ↓  ↓  ↓   ↓    ╲\n\n       To cut this tree (Gītā 15.3) = to detach from the branches,\n       to trace back to the upward root, which is the supreme Brahman.\n       That root: tat eva śukraṃ tad brahma tad evāmṛtam ucyate (II.iii.1).</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"8. The unfolding of the Upaniṣad (structural map)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(The progression of the doctrine across 6 vallīs.)","<pre>\n        ADHYĀYA I                                  ADHYĀYA II\n        ──────────                                ──────────\n        I.i   NARRATIVE FRAME                     II.i  THE OUTGOING SENSES\n              (Naciketā-Yama,                            (the senses face outward;\n               3 boons)                                   yad eveha tad amutra;\n                                                          aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa I)\n                ↓                                                  ↓\n        I.ii  THE TWO PATHS                        II.ii THE ELEVEN-GATED CITY\n              (śreyas vs preyas;                         (the swan-mantra; the One\n               OM; the unborn ātman;                     in many; SVAYAṂ-PRAKĀŚA\n               yam evaiṣa vṛṇute)                        BRAHMAN — na tatra sūryo)\n                ↓                                                  ↓\n        I.iii THE CHARIOT                          II.iii THE INVERTED AŚVATTHA\n              (ratha-rūpaka;                             (yoga-definition;\n               graded tattvas;                            knot-cutting;\n               uttiṣṭhata jāgrata)                        101 nāḍīs;\n                                                          aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa II)\n\n                              CONVERGENCE: amṛtatvam — immortality\n                                  (II.iii.18 — Naciketā attains)</pre>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Practical Steps and Observations","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Each section pairs a Kaṭha doctrine with a concrete practice. The Upaniṣad is not a philosophical treatise but a sādhanā-manual; these readings unpack each major teaching as something to do, not merely to think."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"A. Discriminate between śreyas and preyas, daily","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: I.ii.1–2)","In every meaningful decision of the day — what to read, whom to call, where to spend energy — pause and apply the Kaṭha test: is this śreyas (toward the supreme good) or preyas (toward present pleasure)? At first the categories blur; with practice they sharpen. The aim is not to refuse preyas absolutely (most lives must accommodate it), but to know clearly which is which, and never to mistake the second for the first."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"B. Sit with the chariot daily","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: I.iii.3–9)","Each morning, spend five minutes mapping the five components in oneself:<br>  · the body — is it the chariot tonight, or has it become the rider?<br>  · the senses — are they horses obedient to manas, or are they grazing wildly?<br>  · the manas — does buddhi hold its rein, or is it slack?<br>  · the buddhi — is it clear, or clouded by yesterday's residues?<br>  · the ātman — is the rider present at all, or absent in the day's noise?<br>Then enter the day knowing which level needs attention. Over weeks, the inquiry itself trains the alignment."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"C. Practise the upward withdrawal (I.iii.13)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: I.iii.13 — the yoga-sūtra of Kaṭha)","The Kaṭha's own yoga formula is a four-stage withdrawal:<br>  1. speech-and-mind → withdraw into buddhi (= jñāna-ātman)<br>  2. buddhi → withdraw into the great Self (= mahat)<br>  3. mahat → withdraw into the peaceful Self (= śānta-ātman, the puruṣa)<br>  4. rest there.<br>This is the practice of the ascending stillness. Do it for at most 20 minutes at a session; over months, the boundaries between stages dissolve, and the ‘śānta-ātman’ becomes a known location."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"D. The razor-edge resolution (I.iii.14)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: I.iii.14 — ‘uttiṣṭhata jāgrata’)","Memorise this verse in Sanskrit. Recite it on rising. Recite it when sleep tempts before the practice is done. Recite it before the day's hardest decision. ‘Arise, awake; having approached the masters, understand’ — the verse is itself a small but reliable engine for getting up off the floor when the path falters."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"E. Turn the gaze inward (II.i.1)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: II.i.1 — āvṛtta-cakṣuḥ)","The Self-born made the senses outward-facing. This is the cosmic situation; the sādhanā is to reverse it. A simple daily practice: at any random moment, ask ‘what am I looking at right now — outward or inward?’ Most days, the answer is outward most of the time. The aim is to increase the proportion of inward moments. Half a day inward is remote; even 5% is significant."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"F. The asti-iti affirmation (II.iii.12–13)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: II.iii.12–13)","Begin every meditation, and every difficult conversation, with the silent affirmation: ‘asti’ — He is. Not as dogma, but as the prior posture without which the rest is impossible. The Kaṭha's epistemology requires existence be affirmed before essence is investigated; this is true also of the supreme."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"G. The heart-knots inventory (II.iii.15)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: II.iii.15 — hṛdaya-granthi)","Once a quarter, take a slow afternoon. Make an honest inventory of the heart-knots currently in place — the avidyā-knot (what am I confused about?), the kāma-knot (what do I crave?), the karma-knot (what unfinished action holds me?). Name each; the naming itself loosens. Over years, this inventory becomes shorter — and the verse's promise (mortal becomes immortal here itself) becomes less abstract."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"H. The aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa contemplation (II.i.12 and II.iii.17)","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["(Foundation: II.i.12; II.iii.17)","Daily, before sleep: locate the aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa in the heart-cave. Treat the location as concrete — about a thumb's measure below the sternum-notch. Hold attention there. Let breath slow. Let the day's residues settle out of buddhi as silt out of water. The verse's promise: ‘from him (one) shrinks not’ — fear dissolves at this locus. With patience (dhairyeṇa), the inner Person is drawn forth as a stalk from the muñja-reed."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Sanskrit editions","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["• Kāṭhakopaniṣad (with Śaṅkara-bhāṣya). Critical edition, Anandāśrama Sanskrit Series no. 7, Pune, 1888 (repr. 1936, 1968).","• Kaṭhopaniṣad. Sanskritdocuments.org ITRANS digital edition (katha.itx), proofread by John Manetta, Adriano Aprigliano, Sunder Hattangadi; last updated Feb 2025.","• Olivelle, Patrick (ed., tr.). The Early Upaniṣads — Annotated Text and Translation. New York: Oxford University Press, 1998. [Critical apparatus and English.]","• Limaye, V.P. &amp; Vadekar, R.D. (eds.). Eighteen Principal Upaniṣads, vol. 1. Pune: Vaidika Saṃśodhana Maṇḍala, 1958. [Includes accent-marked text.]"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Commentaries","title":"primary","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Sivānanda (Swāmī). The Kathopanishad. Rishikesh","Divine Life Society, 1949 (8th edn 2003). English translation and discursive commentary."],["Aurobindo, Sri. Kena and Other Upanishads. Pondicherry","Sri Aurobindo Ashram, 1952 (rev. 1996). [Contains partial Kaṭha commentary, vv. I.i.1 – I.iii.9.]"]],"notes":["• Śaṅkara. Kaṭhopaniṣad-Bhāṣya. Tr. Swāmī Gambhīrānanda, in Eight Upaniṣads with the Commentary of Śaṅkarācārya, vol. I. Kolkata: Advaita Ashrama, 1957 (15th impression 2009).","• Rangarāmānuja. Kaṭhopaniṣad-Prakāśikā. Ed. P.B. Annangaracharya. Madras: Granthamālā, 1939 (in his collected Upaniṣad-bhāṣyas).","• Madhva (Ānandatīrtha). Kāṭhakopaniṣad-Bhāṣya. In Sarvamūla-grantha-saṅgraha, vol. III (Upaniṣad-prasthāna). Udupi: Akhila-Bhārata-Mādhva-Mahā-Maṇḍala, 1969–80.","• Deussen, Paul. Sechzig Upaniṣad's des Veda, aus dem Sanskrit übersetzt. Leipzig: F.A. Brockhaus, 1897. Eng. tr. V.M. Bedekar &amp; G.B. Palsule, Sixty Upaniṣads of the Veda, Delhi: Motilal Banarsidass, 1980."],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Commentaries","title":"secondary and comparative","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[["Radhakrishnan, S. The Principal Upaniṣads. London","George Allen &amp; Unwin, 1953. [Synthetic introduction and translation.]"],["Garbe, Richard. Die Sāṃkhya-Philosophie. Leipzig","Haessel, 1894 (2nd edn 1917). [On Kaṭha as proto-Sāṃkhya.]"]],"notes":["• Hume, Robert Ernest. The Thirteen Principal Upanishads. Oxford: 1921 (2nd edn 1931). [Translation only; helpful for cross-checking.]","• Whitney, William Dwight. ‘Translation of the Katha-Upanishad.’ Transactions of the American Philological Association, 21 (1890): 88–112.","• Krishnananda (Swāmī). The Philosophy of the Panchadasi. Rishikesh: Divine Life Society, 1991. [Includes thematic Kaṭha exposition.]","• Belvalkar, S.K. &amp; Ranade, R.D. History of Indian Philosophy, vol. 2: The Creative Period. Poona: Bilvakuñja Publishing House, 1927.","• Brereton, Joel P. ‘The Composition of the Maitreyī Dialogue in the Bṛhadāraṇyaka Upaniṣad.’ JAOS 126 (2006): 323–45. [Comparative; sheds light on Kaṭha's own redactional history.]"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Vedic-ritual background","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["• Taittirīya-Brāhmaṇa III.11.7–8 (the Naciketas legend in Brāhmaṇa form). Ed. R. Mitra, Bibliotheca Indica.","• Caland, W. Das śrautasūtra des Āpastamba (translation). Göttingen 1921. [On the agnicayana background to the Nāciketa-fire.]"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"Cross-references within the Upaniṣadic corpus and beyond","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["The Kaṭha is dense with intertextual citations — both as quoting earlier texts (Ṛgveda, Brāhmaṇas) and as quoted by later ones (Gītā, Brahma-Sūtra, later Upaniṣads). The table below maps the most important parallels verse-by-verse.","<table><tr><td>Kaṭha verse</td><td>Parallel locus</td><td>Relation</td></tr><tr><td>I.i.1–6 (Naciketā story)</td><td>Taittirīya-Brāhmaṇa III.11.8</td><td>The Naciketas legend in older Brāhmaṇa form — Kaṭha is the Upaniṣadic re-shaping.</td></tr><tr><td>I.i.20 (asti / nāsti debate)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka III.9.28</td><td>Same ontological question; Kaṭha presents Yama answering, BĀU has Yājñavalkya.</td></tr><tr><td>I.ii.7 (āścaryo vaktā)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 2.29</td><td>Cited almost verbatim by Kṛṣṇa to Arjuna.</td></tr><tr><td>I.ii.14 (anyatra dharmād…)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 8.11</td><td>Five-fold negation of the supreme; Gītā cites directly.</td></tr><tr><td>I.ii.18 (na jāyate mriyate…)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 2.20</td><td>The deathless ātman doctrine, verbatim with slight metrical adjustment.</td></tr><tr><td>I.ii.19 (hantā ced…)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 2.19</td><td>The non-slayer / non-slain; Gītā cites directly.</td></tr><tr><td>I.ii.20 (aṇor aṇīyān)</td><td>Śvetāśvatara III.20; Mahānārāyaṇa-Up. 8.3</td><td>The same paradox of smaller-than-small.</td></tr><tr><td>I.ii.23 (yam evaiṣa vṛṇute)</td><td>Muṇḍaka III.ii.3</td><td>The grace-doctrine verse; identical text. Kaṭha is generally taken as the source.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.1 (guhāṃ praviṣṭau)</td><td>Brahma-Sūtra I.2.11</td><td>Bādarāyaṇa cites this verse as the locus for the ‘two-in-the-cave’ adhikaraṇa.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.3–9 (chariot)</td><td>Plato, Phaedrus 246a–254e</td><td>Striking parallel image; independent invention or shared inheritance — disputed.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.9 (paramaṃ padaṃ viṣṇoḥ)</td><td>Ṛgveda I.22.20</td><td>Direct quotation of the most ancient Vedic ‘supreme abode’ verse.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.10–11 (tattva hierarchy)</td><td>Sāṃkhya-Kārikā 22; Bhagavad-Gītā 3.42</td><td>Proto-Sāṃkhya tattva-list; classical Sāṃkhya formalises the same sequence.</td></tr><tr><td>I.iii.14 (uttiṣṭhata jāgrata)</td><td>Vivekānanda's mission motto</td><td>Taken as the watchword of the 19th-c. Indian spiritual renaissance.</td></tr><tr><td>II.i.1 (parāñci khāni)</td><td>Maitrī VI.31</td><td>The outward-facing senses; Maitrī develops the doctrine.</td></tr><tr><td>II.i.3 (yena rūpaṃ…)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 13.5–6</td><td>The ātman as single knower of all sense-modalities; Gītā elaborates.</td></tr><tr><td>II.i.10–11 (yad eveha tad amutra)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka IV.iv.19</td><td>Verbatim identical text; direction of borrowing disputed.</td></tr><tr><td>II.i.12–13 (aṅguṣṭha-puruṣa)</td><td>Śvetāśvatara III.13; Maitrī VI.38</td><td>The same thumb-sized indweller in identical language.</td></tr><tr><td>II.i.15 (pure water in pure water)</td><td>Schopenhauer, WWV §41</td><td>Cited explicitly by Schopenhauer as an Upaniṣadic image of identity-realisation.</td></tr><tr><td>II.ii.2 (haṃsa-mantra)</td><td>Ṛgveda IV.40.5</td><td>Verbatim quotation of the most famous Vedic Swan-mantra.</td></tr><tr><td>II.ii.11 (sūrya-asaṅgatā)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 13.31–32</td><td>The sun-image of non-attachment; Gītā develops.</td></tr><tr><td>II.ii.13 (nityo nityānām)</td><td>Śvetāśvatara VI.13</td><td>The verse on the eternal-of-eternals; near-verbatim in Śvetāśvatara.</td></tr><tr><td>II.ii.15 (na tatra sūryo bhāti)</td><td>Muṇḍaka II.ii.10; Śvetāśvatara VI.14; Gītā 15.6</td><td>The self-luminous Brahman; three later texts cite Kaṭha as source.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.1 (ūrdhva-mūla aśvattha)</td><td>Bhagavad-Gītā 15.1–3</td><td>The inverted tree; Gītā expands with the prescription to ‘cut with the axe of non-attachment’.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.10–11 (yoga-definition)</td><td>Yoga-Sūtra 1.2 (citta-vṛtti-nirodha)</td><td>The earliest Upaniṣadic yoga-definition; Patañjali formalises.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.15 (hṛdaya-granthi)</td><td>Muṇḍaka II.ii.8</td><td>‘Bhidyate hṛdaya-granthiḥ…’ — the same knot-cutting image.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.16 (101 nāḍīs)</td><td>Chāndogya VIII.6.6; Praśna III.6; Maitrī VI.21</td><td>The nāḍī-doctrine appears in four major Upaniṣads with shared numerology.</td></tr><tr><td>II.iii.17 (muñja-iṣīkā image)</td><td>Bṛhad-Āraṇyaka II.4.13</td><td>The reed-stalk extraction image as analogue for ātma-vivecana.</td></tr></table>"],"kind":"note"},{"ref":"A closing note on the redactional layers","title":"","sa":[],"words":[],"lit":"","para":"","coms":[],"notes":["Modern philology (Whitney 1890, Hume 1931, Belvalkar-Ranade 1927, Olivelle 1998) identifies probable redactional strata in the Kaṭha: the narrative frame (I.i.1–29) is likely the oldest layer, derived from the Taittirīya-Brāhmaṇa legend; the doctrinal core (I.ii.1 – II.ii.15) shows the most consistent metre and doctrine; the closing vallī (II.iii) bears the marks of a slightly later expansion, with verses paralleling Muṇḍaka, Śvetāśvatara, and Maitrī. Verses I.iii.17–18 (phala-śruti) are sometimes regarded as appended, though Śaṅkara treats them as integral. None of this affects the doctrinal coherence of the received text, which is unified by the recurring refrain ‘etad vai tat’ — ‘this, verily, is that’."],"kind":"note"}]}]};
